<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/194" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**5. :fas-info-circle: [Suggestion] Make W6.3 Debugging Less Verbose**
</div>

As it stands, it is likely that a good chunk of [W6.3 Debugging](https://nus-cs2103-ay2627-s1.github.io/website/schedule/week6/topics.html#w6-3-debugging) is generated with AI. 

I don't have a problem with it per se, but the way it is written is _extremely, extremely_ verbose and makes it very hard to read and understand. Example:

> The chain assumes the fault lies in code — the common case, but not the only one. A failure can equally originate in configuration, in data left by an earlier version, in a dependency, in the deployment, or in a requirement that was wrong to begin with. Sometimes the program is right while the test is wrong — the test may hold its own defect, or the expectation it encodes may never have been correct. Only an error in the code is a defect; where the fault lies elsewhere, call it the cause. Either way the search is the same: locate whatever has to change.

We could probably make it less verbose:

> Failures are not always code bugs. They often stem from bad configuration, legacy data, broken dependencies, flawed deployments, or mistaken requirements as well. Sometimes, the code works fine and the test itself is wrong. Only an error in the code is known as a _defect_. This contrasts with a _cause_, which occurs when the fault lies elsewhere. Either ways, we still need to search for whatever has to change.

Again, this is not criticism about the use of AI to write, just that I hope that maybe the sections could be tidied up a bit more to be less verbose and get straight to the point?
</panel>

