<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/151#issuecomment-5548862713" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**27 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I agree with the above, and would just like to add on some thoughts:
- We should be consistent within one diagram (i.e. if we want to have an architecture diagram, we usually just have class / component names and their relationships, if we want to sketch out a tricky algorithm / an extension proposal, we do include private members, sequence diagrams etc). Full signatures on one class and a bare name on other neighbours will make it seem like it is an oversight and the reader might wonder if the empty class really has no methods.
- For private methods / attributes, I would think that most diagrams have classes in two categories: (1) blackboxed, (2) subjects.
For the subjects, private methods can carry important explanation, so we may want to include it. But for blackboxed classes on the periphery, we usually only want the interface and so private members are just noise. I guess the most common reason for including it would be that a companion sequence diagram calls it, eg `parser -&gt; parser : createCommand(...)`, then the method should probably exist in the class box.

At the end of the day, I believe the most important consideration is about consistency and making sure your diagram is clear and understandable to others. So no hard and fast rule, diagrams are meant for communication with others after all. 
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/168#issuecomment-5583757924" expanded>
