I think a useful one is MCPs (Model Context Protocols)! In practice, these really help because you can use them to connect to external data sources and tools. 

E.g. Connect it to Slack, and your agent can ping you with a summary once it is done with something, or connect it to Databricks / Supabase etc for easy access to data. Or you can have it connected to Confluence / Jira (wouldn't entirely recommend writing docs with AI yet though 🙃) 
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/152#issuecomment-5583978915" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**29 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I'm not entirely sure what the context is for `#define ZERO 0`, I've personally never ever seen that done... 

But I'll just like to point out there are some cases where you may want the raw 0 or a macro (examples mostly taken from C programming):
- when using syscalls like `dup2()`, official POSIX definitions like `STDIN_FILENO` being 0 does help readability a lot
- but when you're using `fork()`, the standard pattern of

'''c
pid_t pid = fork();

if (pid &lt; 0) ``{``
    // error
``}`` else if (pid == 0) ``{``
    // child
``}`` else ``{``
    // parent
``}``
'''

is definitely still very very common
- further examples, code like `if (posix_spawn(...) == 0)` are also still very common

I think at the end of the day, abstracting these zeroes into a macro makes life a lot harder, because people need to go and find the macro to ensure you aren't doing something weird. So it really depends, it's a trade-off between readability and ease of understanding, so I don't think there should be a prescriptive "beyond 10k LOC, convert all literals to constants"

As a bonus, here's an example (there are a lot of raw zeroes) in the Linux kernel :)

https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/kernel/sched/membarrier.c#L257
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/171#issuecomment-5593467838" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**30 :fas-comment:**
</div>

In practice, I think quite a few companies do use Jira / Linear on a broader scale for issue tracking because it (1) allows non-technical stakeholders to contribute to the roadmap (2) allows you to view issues across multiple repos (which is fine for here in iP / tP where we're working in a monorepo, but often in enterprise code we have many services in many repos). 

But I do know another very common workflow is kind of a "dual-tracking" system, where developers indicate a Jira / Linear issue key (e.g. `PROJ-123`) in their Github Issue title (or branch / PR title) and this is automatically tied to said issue by workflow, so any activity like merging the PR will update the status. So developers can stick to gh issues if they so prefer but it's synced to the main issue tracker too.

As for pure github issues, I think it really depends on the size of the company and whether you have any non-technical stakeholders who would be involved in issue tracking. 

Hope this helps 😃 
</panel>

