<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/209#issuecomment-5664795464" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**37 :fas-comment:**
</div>

> Alternatively it could also be suggested that students setup Github actions CI to automatically test their jar files on different platforms such that there is no need to actually run it on anyone's systems

The second half of this suggestion is already in Week 5's iP tasks, [iP task 4 "Add Increment: A-CI"](https://nus-cs2103-ay2627-s1.github.io/website/schedule/week5/project.html#4-add-increment-a-ci) us to set up GitHub Actions, and the recommended [`gradle.yml`](https://github.com/se-edu/addressbook-level3/blob/master/.github/workflows/gradle.yml) already runs the build on a matrix of `ubuntu-latest`, `macos-latest` and `windows-latest`.

So the cross-platform checking already does happen on GitHub's runners rather than on anyone's machine. It is marked `optional` for the iP though, so are you suggesting to maybe promote it from optional to required?

However, I think that may not be enough to cover all the possible failures (e.g. JavaFX platform-specific natives missing from the fat JAR, the window not opening, fonts/paths behaving differently), and getting CI to cover these needs a headless GUI setup (e.g. TestFX + Monocle), which is quite a fair bit more than is reasonable for iP probably.

On the security point itself, I believe a VM / container for running classmates' JARs seems like reasonable advice regardless. Since we need to run other teams' JARs during the PE, I think this is some good advice to keep in mind too. Maybe a one-liner somewhere could help. 

An additional point worth considering is that running in a sandbox is generally pretty easy when virtualising Linux / Windows, but macOS is particularly annoying because Apple's license only permits virtualising macOS on Apple hardware... so thanks Apple I guess...
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/248#issuecomment-5697556427" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**38 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Tested on **Linux**, Ubuntu 24.04.5 LTS (kernel 6.14.0-32, x86_64), OpenJDK 25.0.4 (Temurin/Ubuntu build 25.0.4+7). Ran from an empty folder with `java -jar mouse.jar` as instructed.

I'll chime in with a Linux data point since the thread so far covers Windows 11 and macOS. 

Everything on your checklist passes, and I can independently confirm aerodart's find-then-delete bug above, plus it is broader than just `delete`.

### Checklist results

| # | Check | Result |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Runs from empty folder via `java -jar` | Pass |
| 2 | Window title is `Mouse`, GUI opens, accepts input | Pass |
| 3 | All 11 commands work as per UG | Pass (but see bug below) |
| 4 | Bad command errors instead of crashing | Pass |
| 5 | Tasks persist across restart (`data/mouse.txt`) | Pass |

Launch was clean, and `help` output matches the User Guide exactly. All three task types added fine, and your date parsing handled every documented format, `2019-12-02` rendered as `Dec 02 2019`, and `2019-12-02 1800` as `Dec 02 2019, 6:00PM`.

The main bug is that index commands ignore `find` results and operate on the full list. `find` renumbers its output from 1, but `delete`/`mark`/`unmark`/`priority` interpret that number as an index into the full list. So any index command issued after a `find` silently hits the wrong task.

aerodart above already flagged this for `delete`, but their repro used a case where the find result and the real list overlapped at index 1, which makes it look like an off-by-context issue. I picked a case that isolates it completely, i.e. a find with exactly one result:

Starting list:
```
1.[T][X] read book
2.[D][ ] return book (priority: high) (by: Dec 02 2019)
3.[D][ ] pay fees (priority: low) (by: Dec 02 2019, 6:00PM)
4.[E][ ] meeting (from: Mon 2pm to: 4pm)
```

```
find fees
 -&gt; Crumbs matching that sniff:
    1.[D][ ] pay fees (priority: low) (by: Dec 02 2019, 6:00PM)

delete 1
 -&gt; Tossed this crumb:
      [T][X] read book          &gt;-- deleted the wrong task
    The stash now holds 3 crumbs.
```

Only one task was on screen, numbered `1`, and `delete 1` removed a task that wasn't even in the result. There's no error and no warning, the user just loses a different task than the one they were looking at. That makes it a silent data-loss bug, which I would treat as higher severity than a display quirk.

It is not limited to `delete`. Same mechanism with `mark`:

```
list
 -&gt; 1.[D][ ] return book ...
    2.[D][ ] pay fees ...
    3.[E][ ] meeting ...

find meeting
 -&gt; 1.[E][ ] meeting (from: Mon 2pm to: 4pm)

mark 1
 -&gt; Nibble done. Marked this crumb:
      [D][X] return book (priority: high) (by: Dec 02 2019)   &gt;-- wrong task
```

`unmark` and `priority` take the same index path, so they'll behave identically.

I agree with aerodart's suggestions for fixes, and I would personally lean towards the first for the smaller diff, i.e. have `find` print each task's real list index instead of renumbering from 1. Then `find fees` shows `3.[D][ ] pay fees`, and `delete 3` does what the user expects with no extra state to maintain. The mapping approach (displayed index -&gt; real index) is more work and adds a piece of stale state you have to invalidate whenever the list is mutated, which is an easy place for a second bug to creep in. Whichever way you go, the fix belongs in the shared index-resolution path so all four commands are covered at once, not just `delete`.

However, for error handling, this part is well done. Every malformed input produced a specific, styled error bubble. 

| Input | Response |
|---|---|
| `blah` | `Mouse no understand. Give cheese (or a real command).` |
| `todo` | `That crumb has no name` |
| `delete 99` | `That crumb is not in the stash` |
| `mark abc` | `That's not a valid crumb number` |
| `delete 0` | `Crumb numbers start at 1` |
| `deadline no by date` | `A deadline crumb needs a '/by' time` |
| `event party /from Mon` | `An event crumb needs both '/from' and '/to' times` |
| `priority 1 urgent` | `Priority must be high, low, or none. Mouse is picky` |

Distinguishing "not a number" from "out of range" from "zero index" is a nicer level of detail than most iPs bother with.

`data/mouse.txt` was created next to the JAR as documented. After `bye` and a relaunch, all four tasks came back with done-state and priorities intact. The JVM also exits cleanly on `bye`, so no orphaned process left behind.

I also fed it a deliberately corrupted save file:
```
T | 0 | none | valid task
GARBAGE LINE WITH NO PIPES
D | notanumber | high | broken deadline | 2019-12-02
X | 0 | none | unknown type
T | 0
```
It started normally and reported `Skipped 3 mouldy lines in the stash file.`, keeping the two recoverable entries. Refusing to quit on a malformed save file, and telling the user how many lines were dropped, is a nice touch.

Nice work overall!

