card capital of France /a Paris
cards
quiz
paris
quiz
wrong
delete 3
todo
mark abc
deadline bad /by 2026-02-30
event backwards /from 2026-09-27 /to 2026-09-25
nonsense
list
list
todo save probe
bye
list
cards
list
cards
list
cards
'''

- Adding, listing, case-insensitive searching, marking, unmarking and deletion worked. Enter and Send both worked.
- Special characters in `todo read A|B & "C" <D>` were displayed and restored unchanged after restart.
- Adding/listing a trivia card and answering a quiz correctly (`paris`) and incorrectly (`wrong`) worked.
- Missing descriptions, non-numeric task numbers, impossible dates, reversed event dates and unknown commands produced clear errors without crashing.
- Resizing to 700 × 500 kept messages and controls readable.
- `bye` displayed a farewell and closed Gud Goi. Reopening restored tasks, completion status, special characters and cards; the deleted event stayed deleted.

Storage checks (isolated test files only; both files backed up first):

- While the app was running, replaced `data/saved.txt` with `BROKEN_SMOKE_TEST_LINE`. `list` still showed the in-memory tasks. Adding `todo save probe` rewrote a valid file, replacing the external edit without warning. This matches the User Guide's documented limitation.
- With the app closed, repeated the corrupt-file edit and relaunched. Gud Goi quoted the bad line, stated that it had deleted the damaged file, and started with an empty agenda. The trivia card remained intact. This matches the documented behavior; see `101-corrupt-cards.png`.
- Closed the app, restored both backups, reopened, and verified the original three remaining tasks and trivia card. Both restored files matched their backups exactly. See `104-backup-restored-cards.png`.

Release-link issue: the forum points to `A-UserGuide`, but that release was unavailable (GitHub API returned 404). I tested the available [A-Release JAR](https://github.com/Bruhmiuz/ip/releases/download/A-Release/gudgoi.jar) instead. To reproduce, follow the forum's release link; expected a downloadable release, actual unavailable release. The originally named release therefore could not be verified.

Not verified: Linux, Unicode/emoji characters, and scrolling. Mouse-wheel automation did not produce visible scrolling, so this is not classified as an application defect.

The terminal printed a JavaFX native-access warning, but it did not prevent launch. No crashes encountered.

