I think you should be able to `git fetch --all --tags` first to update your remote-tracking branches.

If your local `master` has no additional commits and is simply behind `origin/master`, you can just fast-forward it without needing to resolve any merge conflicts.

I would probably avoid re-cloning since fetching should be enough to bring your local view of the remote up to date. You can use `git status` and `git log --oneline --graph --all` after fetching to check whether the branches have actually diverged before deciding whether a merge is needed.

Also actually I am quite curious how did this happen? Do you have a suspicion on why your remote is somehow infront? 🤔 
