<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/153#issuecomment-5556622308" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**15 :fas-comment:**
</div>

agree with @Aster0 @BoonHow97 

I think the second approach is generally the cleaner design. The GUI/controller should mainly handle presentation-related responsibilities, such as reading the user's input, passing it to the application logic, and displaying the result.

The actual command parsing and processing should ideally remain outside the JavaFX controller. This keeps the application logic independent of the UI, so the same logic can still be reused by the original text UI or tested without having to initialise JavaFX.

I think a useful way to draw the boundary is to ask: **“Would this logic still be needed if I replaced JavaFX with another interface?”** If yes, it probably belongs in the application/domain layer rather than the GUI.

For example, deciding what `mark 2` means and updating the task should be application logic, while deciding how the updated task is displayed in a chat bubble would be UI logic.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/151#issuecomment-5556649673" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**16 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Late to the show but here is my thoughts about UML implementation details after reading through the discussions: 

I think the clarification about omitting the attribute/method compartments entirely is useful. It also helps distinguish between **"this class has no methods"** and **"the methods are simply outside the scope of this diagram."**

From the discussion, my takeaway is that consistency does not necessarily mean every class must be shown with the same amount of detail. Rather, the diagram should be consistent with its **purpose and level of abstraction**.

For example, if `Parser` is the main focus of the diagram, it may make sense to show some of its internal methods, while other classes can be treated as a black box if its internal implementation is irrelevant to what the diagram is trying to explain.

So I guess the key question is less:

> **"Did I show the same amount of information for every class?"**

and more:

> **"Does every piece of information shown contribute to what this diagram is trying to communicate?"**

If there could still be ambiguity, omitting the method/attribute compartments or adding a UML note seems like a good way to make the intentional omission clear, as what prof mentioned. 
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/157#issuecomment-5557488979" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**17 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think introducing a `TaskStatus` enum or additional factory methods may be unnecessary abstraction for this case.

Since the task only has two states, `isDone` already models the concept naturally as a boolean. While a literal such as `false` at the call site may not immediately show what it represents, just like what you said. The constructor parameter name `isDone` makes the intended meaning clear once the API is understood.

I would consider introducing something like `TaskStatus` only if more task states are expected in the future. Otherwise, for a simple done/not-done state, I think `boolean isDone` is sufficient and avoids over-engineering.
</panel>

