<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/201#issuecomment-5658266898" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**24 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think @spencercdz @MarcusMa06-code covered most of what I want to convey, but just to add on a little: 

My understanding is that a **feature** refers mainly to a distinct user-facing capability of the MVP.

As such, things like **command-based navigation** seem more like an interaction/design choice rather than a feature by itself. Similarly, **automatic data persistence** would be an application-wide behaviour rather than a separate feature, unless there is a specific user-facing operation such as `backup`, `export`, or `restore`.

Hence, I think the feature list should mainly contain concrete user actions such as `add`, `delete`, `find`, etc.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/207#issuecomment-5673234412" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**25 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think the key is to decide on the **level of abstraction** based on what the sequence diagram is trying to illustrate.

For example, if the purpose is to show what happens when an `add` command is executed, it may be sufficient to show the main interactions such as `Parser → Logic → Model → Storage`. Internal helper-method calls do not necessarily need to be included if they do not add anything important to understanding that scenario.

However, if a helper method is important to explaining the interaction or behaviour being illustrated, then it can be shown as well (including self-invocations where appropriate).

So rather than showing every method call in the actual code, I would include the calls that are **relevant to the specific scenario and level of abstraction** of the diagram.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/211#issuecomment-5673249750" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**26 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think a design decision becomes architecturally significant when its impact is no longer confined to one component and it starts influencing other parts of the system.

Using your example, if changing the data representation inside `Model` only requires changes within `Model`, I would still consider it an implementation-level decision. However, if that change forces corresponding changes to `Logic`, `Storage`, `UI`, or the interfaces between these components, then it has a wider system-level impact and could be considered architecturally significant.

So perhaps one way to judge it is by looking at the **scope and consequences of the decision**, rather than simply whether the decision is made inside a component.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/216#issuecomment-5677163482" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**27 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I will refer to some week 5 content haha. 

I think a feature does not necessarily have to correspond to a command or even be something that the user directly triggers.

From W5.3, a **feature list** is simply described as a list of features of a product, while **user stories** describe a feature from the perspective of the person who wants the capability, usually in the form `As a ``{``user role``}``, I can ``{``function``}`` so that ``{``benefit``}```. 

So I would interpret a feature more broadly as some **capability or behaviour of the product that helps fulfil a requirement**, rather than specifically an action exposed as a command.

For example:
- **Saving the data automatically** can still be considered a feature, even though the user does not invoke a `save` command directly, because it is behaviour provided by the application.
- **Editing the data file manually outside the application** seems different, because that behaviour is not actually provided by the application itself. I would therefore be more inclined to describe this as a supported usage/instruction rather than an application feature.

The Week 5 notes also define a functional requirement as specifying **what the system should do**, which seems to support distinguishing application-provided behaviour from actions performed externally by the user. 

So my understanding is that **commands are one way of exposing features, but not every feature needs to be a command**.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/218#issuecomment-5677240199" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**28 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I would separate **user input errors** from **unexpected application/system errors**.

For something like:

`add n/John p/abc`

the invalid phone number is an **expected validation failure**. Since the application already detects it and gives the user an appropriate error message, I would usually **not log it as a `WARNING`**. Otherwise, normal user mistakes could make the logs unnecessarily noisy.

If there is value in tracing such interactions during debugging, it could be logged at a lower level such as `FINE`, but even then we should avoid logging unnecessary user-provided data.

I would use higher levels for issues that indicate something abnormal in the application itself, for example:
- `WARNING` for an unexpected but recoverable problem, such as failing to read a file but still being able to continue.
- `SEVERE` for a serious failure that prevents an important operation or causes the application to become unusable.

So I think the deciding factor is not simply **“was an error shown to the user?”**, but rather **“would this event be useful for a developer or maintainer when diagnosing an unexpected problem?”**

In short, expected validation errors can usually remain user-facing only, while unexpected internal failures are more useful to record in the logs.
</panel>

