<div slot="header">

**39 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I agree that the number of classes should not determine the component boundary. A useful rule of thumb is to ask whether the class has a distinct responsibility, exposes a clear interface, and can be understood independently from the component containing it.

If the class only implements an important decision within Logic or Model, I would keep it inside that larger component and explain it in a lower-level diagram. I would show it as a separate architectural component only when the rest of the system interacts with it as an independent unit. This also keeps the architecture diagram at a consistent level of abstraction.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/266#issuecomment-5717737565" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**40 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think loading the valid entries while clearly warning the user is a reasonable approach, especially since each task is stored independently. Rejecting the entire file because of one corrupted line could cause more unnecessary data loss.

However, the invalid line should not be ignored silently. The application could report how many entries failed to load, identify the affected line numbers, and preserve a backup of the original file before saving again. This gives the user a chance to recover or repair the missing data.

I would only stop loading completely if the corruption makes the file’s overall structure unreliable or if partially loaded records could create inconsistent state. For independent task entries, best-effort recovery with a visible warning seems like the better trade-off.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/215#issuecomment-5717790441" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**41 :fas-comment:**
</div>

If the object already exists when the interaction begins, its participant box and lifeline are shown from the top of the sequence diagram.

A newly created object is represented differently. As shown in the `Minefield` example, the create message points to the new object’s box, and its lifeline begins at that point rather than at the top. Therefore, the presence or absence of a create message and where the lifeline begins distinguishes an existing object from one created during the interaction.

<img width="387" height="202" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/bb2b4bfc-0c67-4c76-8634-b6b4dea6da76" />
</panel>

