<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/540#issuecomment-5786661375" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**45 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think the target audience should guide the glossary. Since this is a developer guide, I would assume readers know common terms like API and JSON, so I would not add them by default.

I would include a term if it has a project-specific meaning, could be interpreted in more than one way, or is essential to understanding the guide. For example, if “API” refers to one particular external service throughout our project, defining that usage could help. That keeps the glossary focused on terms readers actually need clarified.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/530#issuecomment-5786736755" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**46 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Since a method creates the Task with an empty description, I would check the invariant in `Task` itself, ideally in its constructor and any method that can change the description. That catches the programming error close to where the invalid state is introduced.

A boundary assertion can also be useful if a component receives a `Task` whose validity it cannot rely on. But if Task already guarantees its own invariant, `checking !description.isBlank() `at every component boundary would mostly repeat the same check.

So I would use both only when the boundary has an independent reason to distrust the object. Otherwise, the check inside `Task` gives more useful debugging information because it fails before the invalid object gets passed around.
</panel>

