## **Smoke Test Request - TaskTracker**

### Resources
* **Download:** [TaskTracker.jar](https://github.com/ykteo000/ip/releases) 
* **Documentation:** [TaskTracker User Guide](https://ykteo000.github.io/ip/)
* **Target OS Platforms:** Windows | MacOS | Linux

---

### Instructions for Testers
Please test the application following the instructions in the User Guide above. You can use standard commands such as:
`list`, `help`, `todo`, `deadline`, `event`, `fixed`, `mark`, `unmark`, `delete`, `undo`, `find`, `bye`

#### Priority Focus Areas
* **Date/Time Tasks:** Creation and formatting of `deadline` and `event` entries.
* **History State:** Deleting tasks and restoring them with `undo`.
* **CLI Recovery:** Entering `>` to restore and correct mistyped input lines.
* **Error Resilience:** System behavior when handling missing or corrupted records in `data/tasks.txt`.

---

### Feedback Guidelines
When reporting your findings, please include:
1. **Operating System:** Specify OS name and version (e.g., Windows 11, macOS Sequoia).
2. **Screenshots:** Attach visual captures demonstrating any unexpected behavior.
3. **Bug Reports:** Provide steps to reproduce, exact input strings, and expected vs. actual output.
4. **Suggestions:** Note any UI/UX or workflow improvements.

---

## Thank you for your time and testing support!

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/135" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3. :fas-info-circle: Abstracted Code Leading to Failed Checkstyle with DeclarationOrderCheck**
</div>

Basically I have abstracted all the message commands for errors and printing to its own separate class called Message.java, and within it I want to have some private methods that other public methods refer to to further build the string.

This leads to a chicken and egg problem because...

> 1. Java’s Compiler Rule: You _**cannot reference**_ a variable _**before it is declared**_
> 2. Checkstyle’s Rule: Every single _**public variable must appear physically higher**_ in the file than any private variable. Hence failing by DeclarationOrder.

Brief truncated code (the file is just basically a whole ton of syntax strings).
'''java
public class Message ``{``

    // --- Raw Command Syntax (private helpers) --- //
    private static final String SYNTAX_INDEX = "mark &lt;index> | unmark &lt;index> | delete &lt;index>";
    private static final String SYNTAX_TODO = "todo &lt;description>";
    private static final String SYNTAX_DEADLINE = "deadline &lt;description> /by &lt;due DT>";
    private static final String SYNTAX_EVENT = "event &lt;description> /from &lt;start DT> /to &lt;end DT>";
    private static final String SYNTAX_FIND = "find &lt;keyword>";
    private static final String SYNTAX_DATE_TIME = "yyyy-MM-dd HHmm(24-H clock)";

    /** This is where the conflict arises due to 1 and 2. */
    public static final String MSG_HELP = "Here are the available commands:\n"
            + "  - list : Views all tasks\n"
            + "  - " + SYNTAX_TODO + " : Adds a todo task\n"
            + "  - " + SYNTAX_DEADLINE + " : Adds a deadline task\n"
            + "  - " + SYNTAX_EVENT + " : Adds an event task\n"
            + "  - " + SYNTAX_INDEX + " : Manipulates task by index\n"
            + "  - " + SYNTAX_FIND + " : Finds event by keyword\n"
            + "  - bye : Exits the program\n"
            + "  - Date and Time (DT) Format: " + SYNTAX_DATE_TIME + "\n";
    
    // Many more definitions for messages that I will leave out

'''

This mutual exclusivity is what leads to issues with checkstyles. To me I feel that certain methods such as helpers can be made private but checkstyle is rather unhappy. A rather simple fix would just to make all the fields above public, or just make it package private (which will still fail checkstyle under VisibilityModifier).

Would like to hear some other workarounds to this. I do know fair share of people have their exceptions thrown directly with an assembled message in their respective error sites looking through the IP which I should have ideally done too.

My rationale was basically better abstraction by grouping related fields together. But frankly this is what my mind thought of (I have not automated any level of the iP using AI agents so far, so my idea here could indeed not be very optimal coding practices).


Thanks!



</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/67" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4. :fas-info-circle: 💎Remember to Activate 2FA in Github + Some (Easy) Online Safety Tips**
</div>

--- Remember to set up 2FA for Github ---

<img width="1269" height="76" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/7d5f57c3-8784-440e-865b-17e864a8629b" /> (Mine shows 09 October 2026, but depends on each user)

As of 2023, Github has mandated 2 Factor Authentication (2FA) for all developers who contribute code to Github.  Some users (including me) may not receive updates till now because of activity history on this site.

Please do ensure you have 2FA set up before that deadline if the notification pops up on your account, failure to do so may cause revocation of Github services.


--- Basic Cybersecurity Tips (which everyone here should know of) ---
As more of us are using Agentic AI, there is definitely an increasing number of malware and vulnerability exploitation, reason being most people dont put as much emphasis onto the security aspect at the expense of convenience. So here are some general tips that are good practices.

1) Do be wary of fake impersonation emails/ popups, most users are honestly fine and nothing will happen unless you have done other stuff on your PC, but still do be wary.

A simple tip is to check the Tune/ Page Info/ Site Control icon on the left next to the address bar. Shown below is how it looks like in Chrome. You may view the certificate information to verify that the certificate ID and public key matches from the source (most websites publicly list the certificate ID and public key, else a google search will probably get you the results).

<img width="335" height="239" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/3091e3a8-b8e1-4d36-8fc6-66e4e5af792b" />

<img width="313" height="210" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/fa0f83b3-821e-4eb3-8fb1-39ac508ef5b8" />

<img width="395" height="488" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/7e030208-fb60-4817-abc8-4a4816af0d2f" />

2) When browsing websites and downloading files, you may want to use these links to verify the safety and security of sites and files. https://nordvpn.com/file-checker/ https://nordvpn.com/link-checker/ https://www.virustotal.com/gui/home/upload https://www.virustotal.com/gui/home/url https://transparencyreport.google.com/safe-browsing/search?hl=en
Yes I do use multiple sites just to cross verify, once again do check the certificates and site status with the Tune icon.

3) Recommend having a antivirus running on your device, Microsoft Defender (if you are a windows user) is free and has pretty good core features.

4) Do not reuse the same passwords, or choose easy passwords. 2FA sites that are important for you. And oh yes having password stored on browsers is a bad idea. Not to scare and fearmonger too much but https://www.reddit.com/r/hacking/comments/rmyhe3/extracting_browser_passwords_is_it_really_this/

5) Please do not upload all your personal data to AI. I know its convenient. But at least blur out your personal data. And on the subject of "generate a photo of what you know about me" etc trends, at this point its a glorified data mining scheme lol. Try not to.

6) Make backups of your data. Well in this course we have git commit and pushes. But for the rest of your device? 

If there anything that is important and I miss out feel free to add on, but otherwise these are just some of the most generic tips. So long as you are well protected and dont try to do too dumb things, you should be fine.

Cheers!
</panel>

