Ok I think the answer to this question is basically up to how much you want to learn.

I do agree with you that there is an increase in complexitiy if we have to keep doing this iterative process.

But the syllabus is probably designed to make you do it iteratively, make sure it works, then add features and redo.
And ah yes, to learn the horrors of the working world by simulating the demands of your superiors.
Which is indeed true in the real business world (I do stuff outside of academic life so... experiences lol).

For me I work based on mostly OOP principles, so I tend to create classes to make objects that work together.
That also sort of helps make the iterative refactoring less of a pain (except for changing time from tring to Java date time util).
Good OOP principles will make it much easier to add in more features in the future because you pass objects.
The implementation within can always be changed and tweaked much easier.

But @BoonHow97 is also right, you dont need to do a lot of OOP for the iP, frankly we are doing A LOT in a short time.
Keep It Short & Simple ie KISS. If there is no need to abstract it because its rather trivial, then dont abstract it.
Only abstract it if you were hard taught to do it this way or you are basically used to this method.
And so long as you meet the coding style and other project specifications.

Cheers!





</panel>

