Reading everyones comments I would like to chime in some of my thoughts.
Once again, a human ie me fully wrote this comment. No ~clanker~ AI was used.

Lets abstract everything away and use a human as an analogy.

At the toddler stage [no I currently do not have kids but thats what my brain thought of]:
>You cant get a toddler to solve differential equations (unless like IQ 180+ genius).
>But he/she may understand shapes, and which shapes match (tbh everything goes into the square hole iykyk).
>He/she may also understand phonetics, or simple pictures, or "Mom" or "Dad".
>Same for food, he/she may hate broccoli and will just spit it out.

Think of your iP like how you would interact with a Toddler.
The Toddler probably wont know how it would respond to certain things you give it.
As a parent you have to guess what your toddler is trying to do.

- you could reject everything that is definitely invalid commands
- you could make it such that it parses regardless of capitalisation for the keyword (like toddlers with phonetics/ pictures)
- you could throw a guide case on what is the expected format, either after a wrong input or when help is used

Now a toddler slowly grows up to a child, then a teen, then an adult.
> He/she will learn more things, and what used to be unobtainable can be done
> Much smarter in identifying what is supposed to be done and can jump in to correct

If you want to think of your iP as a child/ teen/ adult (goes byeond CS2103T scope tbh).

- Add more varied methods to handle more responses
- Try to figure out what incomplete input are suppo.sed to refer to

For the purposes of CS2103T, I would say having a rather strict block on whats allowed is reasonable.
Its a introductory SWE and for the outcomes I think making sure its within a set of bounds is ok.
Anyways you can always refactor and add even more features if you did decent OOP.

If you decide not to handle a specific error, you can just throw an error message I suppose.
Have you heard of the term Ostrich Algorithm? If the bug is very very uncommon, just handle it decently well.
