Hmmmm this else-if chain can be replaced by a switch statement to reduce the nesting and brackets, while improving readability.

<img width="503" height="455" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/42f30cf6-6578-41b1-a8e5-a21e30b08642" />

Take a look at your own implementation within your if-else blocks. Could something be brought out of the method instead?
Eg if you have detected a deadline event is to be added, then the whole process to add a deadline can be handleAddDeadline(variables).

Maybe a simple analogy I can give you is a factory production line (no AI was used, my brain just thinks of random stuff lol)
Each machine on the conveyor belt is meant to do something.

Eg a glass bottled drink production factory needs 
1) Empty glass bottles --> How were these bottles made? Probably from another production line that handled the glass making.
2) The beverage --> How was the drink made? Probably from another production line that handled the drink making.
3) The metal cap --> How were the caps made? Probably from another production line that handled raw metal processing.
4) Glass bottles get filled up then is capped.
5) Packing the bottles for export --> We need a machine to help pack them in a carton of dozen/ pack of 6.
6) Shipping --> We need to handle logistics to make sure all the bottles get delivered properly.

Same process in your code. Think of it in steps. Each method accomplishes a task. And once we finish the method and class, we abstract the idea to high functioning level, ie what its supposed to do. So thinking of your if-else or switch as the machine that helps coordinate all of these separate processes.

Hopefully this helps you a little bit with your code. Cheers!

</panel>

