<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/75#issuecomment-5449454769" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**25 :fas-comment:**
</div>

> [@Aster0](https://github.com/Aster0) hi i was really curious, would you explain more about your idea of generics being used. I was thinking that your right about making the developer narrow down their method to a more specific command and was curious what was your motivation for thinking of such a design pattern.

My main motivation comes from #69 when we were talking about parsing arguments into a command. I realised that the initial method of having a main `Parser.java` class with switch expressions and `parseMethods` could be a little messy. Even though it's currently an iP, I wanted to find a way to streamline parsing of arguments to each command and make it clear to us, the developers of the structure and design. Implementing this parser design system also produces clear responsibility for each parsers, giving a clear role in the system.

The reason for using generics is as mentioned in the main post.. Without generics (i.e., `Parser` that just returns Command), a developer could just return the generic `Command` type unless they consciously remember to narrow the type (e.g., to `TodoCommand`, etc). 

By defining `Parser&lt;T extends Command>`, the interface explicitly forces subclasses (e.g., `TodoCommandParser implements Parser&lt;TodoCommand>`) to specify & return that exact command type. So this reduces ambiguity and guarantees that the parser produces the intended command, and could make JUnit testing much cleaner without unnecessary castings. 
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/75#issuecomment-5450503110" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**26 :fas-comment:**
