For me, I feel like reading and understanding code is very important. The issue with using AI is that you're on "auto-pilot" & you lose the ability to read, debug and comprehend code.

The early days of coding is very important where you actually understand and enjoy the thought process of creating code, I would say try to use minimal AI at this level, so in the future you can read and comprehend code easily. This way, you have full control of your own code too, and understand where to improve in the future and whatnot. 

Also, from my experience of testing AI during the holidays, it's as good as the author of the code. Normally, especially for projects like this, you start small and ask for xxx features. The AI will then fulfil this request by making the most basic architecture after which when you scale, the design would not make sense. So everyone ends up with an unoptimised chunk of code that does not scale realistically when the project gets bigger.
</panel>

