<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/154#issuecomment-5551940031" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**31 :fas-comment:**
</div>

For me, I try my very best to practice the necessary OOP design rules whenever possible. However, if a project is strictly small that I know won't scale in the future or if I know I'm solely the one developing, over-engineering is not a good thing to do, as it just adds extra noise. But if I know that the project might scale into something bigger in the future, I would try my best set its foundation the right way. 

There's a few things I keep in mind regardless of scale however;
1) Where I can reduce duplications
2) Keeping my design modular so refactoring is simple
3) If needed, how would other developers use and understand my API

But ultimately, it also differs from people to people. As for me, adding abstractions & interfaces actually makes the maintainability of my code better; and as a result, I can read and understand my code better if I were to come back to it several days later. 

But I think in an academic standpoint, just like CS2030S which you're supposed to practice good OOP in-case the project evolves to something needed in the future, I treat this as a challenge; if I need to refactor less in an upcoming feature submission, I say that I did a good job with my OOP design if not I am able to learn from what mistakes I made. 

Ultimately.. I don't think there's a right or wrong answer. I think you should do what you feel you're comfortable in and understanding the trade-offs if any.


</panel>

