Great question! I believe that logically, multiplicities should hold before and after an operation is completed. As long as the invariant is restored by the time the operation finishes, I believe that this system should be perfectly valid and conforms to the UML diagram.

In a real life scenario that I thought of;

1) Person A transfers $50 to Person B.
2) Person A's Bank account is reduced by $50 via the bank system. (At this moment, for a split second, $50 is gone from Person A's account)
3) Person B receives the $50 into their bank account via the bank system

If we audit the books at point 2, it will not be balanced. But because the transfer is wrapped in a single operation, we should only see it after point 3 has completed. 

Typing this out reminds me of what I learnt during CS2102, about transactions where a database can enter an invalid state during a transaction but must be valid after the transaction.
</panel>

