<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/138#issuecomment-5522552764" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**21 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Hi I actually had the same question when I was doing my tutorial today, so I went to do a bit of research. The way I understand the difference between aggregation and composition is that composition represents a **stricter** `whole-part` relationship, where the part cannot exist if the whole doesn't exist. For example, if we were to destroy a particular book, we can also safely assume that all of that book's contents (eg chapters) will not exist as well as they can only be read if the book exists.

However, for aggregation, it more so represents a `container-containee` relationship. For example, even if we destroy a bookshelf that contained books, it doesn't mean that the books itself were destroyed because the books don't need a bookshelf to exist, they simply used to be stored in the bookshelf before it got destroyed. In a sense you could argue that this may also represent a `whole-part` relationship, but I like to think of it as books do not specifically need a bookshelf in order to exist, unlike chapters which specifically need to be in a book for them to exist.

I would like to hear others' opinions on this as well!

Side note: This is quite a common question that gets asked and I found this link of [Martin Fowler](https://martinfowler.com/bliki/AggregationAndComposition.html) expressing his opinions on this (I think the course website mentions him as well).
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/142#issuecomment-5531217850" expanded>
