I think this is standard practice even amongst professional teams because GitHub Issues exist independently of your commit history and memory, making it suitable to track bugs. In fact, if I am not mistaken most software teams use GitHub Issues to [track issues as a whole](https://docs.github.com/en/issues/tracking-your-work-with-issues/learning-about-issues/quickstart), not just bugs (Issues also include things like collecting feedback, planning tasks, etc), so common practice is to tag the issue with the appropriate tag (eg whether it is a bug fix, etc)

In our context of the iP, I would assume it is probably fine to use GitHub Issues just to track our bug fixes for now, as the other issues mentioned aren't really relevant to what we are doing right now. However, opening up a GitHub issue might be a bit overkill here (as was mentioned in forum post #57). In fact, I would recommend you to check out forum post #57 as well, as I believe the discussions there regarding "How to track technical debt" are highly relevant to your question. 

I personally think having a `//bug-tracker` comment in my code would be simpler and easier to implement and would be my go-to option in the **context of our iP**, but I would like to hear what others think as well.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/177#issuecomment-5614972630" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**26 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think we shouldn't treat a statement simply being wrapped in a function as a higher level of abstraction. To me the idea of abstraction is so that when I read the code, I know quickly at a glance what exactly each line is doing.

In this example, even though `moreThanFiveLateDeliveries` and `driver.numberOfLateDeliveries > 5` may appear to be different levels of abstraction because one is a helper and the other is a conditional, I actually think they are on the same abstraction level because both functions read the same to me. In fact, I think this is what inline functions help to target, where we can reduce unnecessary extraction into helpers to make the code more succinct.

The point of abstracting code is to not overload the readers with all the implementation details of a function, it simply tells the reader what output they can expect. In this case, when I read `moreThanFiveLateDeliveries` and `driver.numberOfLateDeliveries > 5`, in both cases I know exactly what the code is trying to say, which is that it is checking if there are more than 5 late deliveries. 

If we compare this to the example provided for SLAP 
```
printBanner();
salary = basic * rise + 1000;
tax = (taxable ? salary * 0.07 : 0);
displayResult();
```
it isn't clear to me as a reader what lines 2-3 are doing here, and I have to actually trace the code to know that it is trying to process data, so this is a suitable candidate to be extracted out into a `processData()` method.

I think the main takeaway is when you (or anyone else) read your code, you should be able to understand what each method/ variable is doing at a glance, and if you have to mentally calculate the output in order to figure it out, then it would be worth extracting out.
</panel>

