<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/343#issuecomment-5717080680" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**35 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Good points raised by @YujieJWang and @advaitio. Adding one more angle to this: another way to think about it is why we bother drawing component boundaries at all. Usually it's to protect the rest of the system from needing to know how something is done, so that that part can change independently later.

So a good test is whether this class's internals could be swapped out without anyone else caring? If yes, and other parts just call it and trust the result, that's a good sign it deserves its own box. But if it's tightly tangled with Logic/Model's internals, sharing data structures, assumptions, etc., pulling it out doesn't really achieve anything, since it still can't be understood or changed on its own.

Also, every component is one more thing a reader has to hold in their head. If the class's importance is really about what it does rather than how independently it can evolve, I think that can be better shown in a lower-level diagram like a class diagram or sequence diagrams (indirectly) instead.
</panel>

