<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/157#issuecomment-5560130642" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think boolean parameters are fine when the meaning is obvious from the surrounding code, but passing `true` or `false` directly can hurt readability. For example, when I see `new Task("read book", false)`, I know the task has some property set to false, but I cannot immediately tell that it refers to whether the task is done.

Personally, I would still keep `isDone` as a boolean because a task naturally has only two states: done or not done. Instead of introducing an enum just for those two states, I would avoid passing boolean literals directly where possible. For example:

'''java
boolean isDone = false;
new Task("read book", isDone);
'''

This keeps the simple boolean representation while making the call site easier to understand.

If the task status later becomes more complicated, such as having `IN_PROGRESS`, `CANCELLED`, or other states, then I think an enum would make more sense. So I don't think boolean parameters themselves are bad, but boolean literals as arguments can sometimes make code less readable.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/158#issuecomment-5560258955" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think in this case it is better to follow the prescribed course environment rather than adding `mac-aarch64` ourselves.

Since the requirement is only for the JAR to work on Macs using the prescribed JDK+FX distribution, supporting a normal ARM JDK without JavaFX seems to be outside the expected scope. Adding another JavaFX dependency might make the JAR more portable, but it also introduces something that is not required by the course instructions.

I would therefore keep the dependencies as given in the tutorial and use the prescribed `25.0.3.fx-zulu` setup for Apple-silicon Macs. If the requirement were instead for the JAR to work on any Mac with just a normal JDK installed, then I think including `mac-aarch64` would make sense.

</panel>

