</div>

I would probably go with CSS as well if the background is static. It feels cleaner to keep visual styling separate from the controller logic, especially when the Java version requires quite a bit more code just to set the background.

I also found the point about `cover` useful because I initially assumed the cropping was a scaling issue. Knowing that it is expected behaviour makes `contain` seem like a better option if I want the whole image to stay visible. I would only use the Java approach if I needed the background to change dynamically during runtime.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/148#issuecomment-5573600448" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**6 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I ran into this same issue when following the JavaFX tutorial and initially thought I had configured something wrongly in SceneBuilder.

Checking the `fx:id` directly in the FXML file was the easiest workaround for me, since I could just compare the IDs there with the corresponding `@FXML` fields in the controller. I also did not realise that the ID can be checked from the Code section in the Inspector, so that is useful to know as well.

Good to know that this seems to be a SceneBuilder 26.0.0 UI issue rather than an issue with the FXML itself.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/163#issuecomment-5581901147" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**7 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I agree that CSS is probably the cleaner choice for static styling, while Java makes more sense if the background needs to change dynamically. The point onseparating design from logic is a good way to think about it 👍 

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/165#issuecomment-5582186497" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**8 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Thanks for the tip 👍 I had the same issue where the GUI closed too quickly for the goodbye message to be seen. `PauseTransition` seems like a much cleaner solution than using `Thread.sleep()`.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/166#issuecomment-5582560308" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**9 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think the main “cost” is the extra complexity and effort introduced by the refactor. If extracting code makes the intent clearer or reduces duplication, it is probably worth it, but if it just creates more methods to jump between without improving understanding, I would rather keep the code inline.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/181#issuecomment-5651896130" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**10 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I agree that keeping separate diagrams for expected and actual behaviour is clearer. During debugging, the actual sequence helps show where the program diverges, while the expected one still documents what the system is supposed to do.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/177#issuecomment-5651999224" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**11 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Hi, think the clearest way to distinguish them is:

Use SLAP / extract a helper when the parent method is mixing a high-level task with lower-level implementation details. The goal is for the parent method to read at one conceptual level.
Use Inline Function when the helper adds little or no abstraction and merely renames an expression that is already easy to understand.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/218#issuecomment-5691883385" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**12 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I agree that normal validation errors probably should not be `WARNING`s. If the program correctly rejects bad input, that is expected behaviour, so I would only log it at a lower level if it is useful for debugging. Higher levels should be reserved for unexpected application or system problems.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/343#issuecomment-5717112248" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**13 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think whether something should be shown as a component depends more on its responsibility and interface than on how important the class is. A class might make an important systemwide decision, but if it is still just part of the work done inside `Logic` or `Model`, I would probably keep it within that larger component

And I would only show it as a separate component if it represents a clearly distinct responsibility that the rest of the system interacts with through a well defined interface. Otherwise putting too many individual classes into the architecture diagram could make it look more like a class diagram and lose the higher-level view that an architecture diagram is supposed to give.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/344#issuecomment-5719136608" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**14 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I don’t think every invalid command needs to be logged, especially if the program already handles it properly and tells the user what went wrong. Otherwise, the logs could get filled with normal user mistakes and make actual problems harder to spot.

I’d save `WARNING` or `SEVERE` for things that aren’t expected, like failing to save or read data, corrupted files, or unexpected exceptions. If normal user input is useful for debugging, it could still be logged at a lower level instead.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/484#issuecomment-5729355887" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**15 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I also found this a bit confusing at first since `Remark` does not explicitly extend anything. The key point is that every Java class implicitly extends `Object`, so `hashCode()` is still being overridden.

Using `value.hashCode()` also makes sense because `Remark` compares equality based on its `value`, so equal `Remark` objects should produce the same hash code too.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/531#issuecomment-5762428164" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**16 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I would use the debugger first if the bug is reproducible, since a breakpoint or watchpoint can show exactly when the value changes and the call stack tells you what caused it.

But for something like `taskList.size()`, I think  it’s important to remember that the list can change internally without the `taskList` field itself being reassigned. In that case, breakpoints on methods like `remove()` or `clear()` would be more useful.

If the bug only happens occasionally then logging the important state changes seems better because you can inspect what happened after the fact.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/530#issuecomment-5763053842" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**17 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Seems the main assertion should be as close as possible to where the invalid state can actually be created or changed. That makes the failure happen earlier and gives a more useful clue about the real source of the bug.

I wld only add another assertion at a component boundary if that boundary has its own separate assumption. Repeating the exact same invariant everywhere would probably just add noise. Also, if the bad value can come from normal user input or stored data, I think that should be handled with normal validation rather than relying only on assertions.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/519#issuecomment-5763230832" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**18 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Really useful tip, especially for getting a quick overview of an unfamiliar repo. good idea of using it alongside the Developer Guide, since the generated diagram can show how the code is currently connected while the DG explains how the system is intended to be structured.

Also a good point that it is better suited for public repos unless you are sure the repo can be shared with a third party service.

</panel>

