<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/211#issuecomment-5809597777" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**28 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I look at whether the decision creates a dependency that other parts of the system have to care about.

For example, using an `ArrayList` inside `Model` by itself feels like detailed design. But if other components start relying on things like insertion order or duplicate behaviour, then those assumptions become part of the wider system design.

So IMO the important part is not just how many components are affected, but whether the decision changes interfaces, assumptions, or long-term constraints across the system. If it can be changed locally without anyone else noticing, its probably still detailed design.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/215#issuecomment-5809740480" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**29 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Easiest way is prob to read it is that a lifeline starting at the top just means the object already exists when the scenario begins. It doesn’t mean the object was created at that point.

If the object is actually created during the scenario, then you would show a create message and start its lifeline from there instead. So for something like a delete command, Model and the existing Person can simply have their lifelines from the top, since they were already around before the interaction started.
</panel>

