<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/177#issuecomment-5627727337" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

i had the same confusion tbh

I try to think SLAP more like "is this method thinking at one level only" instead of strictly trying categorize different scenarios  into extract or inline 

in my iP `Parser.parse` only decides which command it is. the messy parsing goes into helpers like `parseDeadlineDetails` / `parseTaskId`. if i put everything inside `parse` it starts mixing "which command" with "how to split `/by`" which feels weird

'''java
public static Command parse(String fullCommand) throws InputException ``{``
    String[] parts = splitCommand(fullCommand);
    return switch (parseCommandType(parts[0])) ``{``
        case DEADLINE -&gt; ``{``
            String[] details = parseDeadlineDetails(parts);
            yield new AddDeadlineCommand(details[0], details[1]);
        ``}``
        case MARK -&gt; new MarkCommand(parseTaskId(parts));
        // ...
    ``}``;
``}``
'''

but extract can also be too much. i have these tiny wrappers that basically just rename the call. It doesnt really help SLAP, only improve the readability

'''java
protected Task getTask(TaskList tasks, int taskId) throws InputException ``{``
    return tasks.getTask(taskId);
``}``

protected Note getNote(NoteList notes, int noteId) throws InputException ``{``
    return notes.getNote(noteId);
``}``
'''

So for me I extract when the parent stays cleaner at one level, and the helper actually does something. if its just a rename i leave it inline. not 100% sure this matches the notes perfectly but this is how i been reading it
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/181#issuecomment-5644833471" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think it depends what you are using the diagram for.

When explaining expected scenario, the sequence diagram should show **intended behaviour**. 

When debugging the bug where **save()** runs before **validate()**, i would not replace that intended diagram with the buggy order as if it is the design. 

more useful is to keep the intended diagram, then compare it with what actually happens, and then the mismatch will alert us to pay attention and see if we need debugging
</panel>

