<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/154#issuecomment-5651847621" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**7 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I agree with the latest comments. For iP, highly extensible is not always better. Since we know some future features maybe never come, I think forcing abstraction makes more classes and harder to read.

I had a similar issue when one of the weekly tasks was to make iP more OOP. Back then I wanted to explore ways to easily enable more commands without adding too much complexity to the project. So i came up with the following `Command` hierarchy + parser that returns a command object, so that I only need to add more cases if i want  to add more commands.

'''java
public static Command parse(String fullCommand) throws InputException ``{``
    String[] parts = splitCommand(fullCommand);
    return switch (parseCommandType(parts[0])) ``{``
        case TODO -&gt; new AddTodoCommand(parseDescription(parts));
        case FIND -&gt; new FindCommand(parseFindKeyword(parts));
        case NOTE -&gt; new AddNoteCommand(parseDescription(parts));
        case UNDO -&gt; parseUndo(parts);
        // ...
    ``}``;
``}``
'''
This might not be the most elegant solution but i think its a good balance between overcomplicating and being extensible.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/201#issuecomment-5657687357" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**8 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think you are right that those don't really belong as separate "features" in the MVP list. We already agreed tP is a CLI based app, so I would take "command-based navigation" for granted because it is the core requirement of the product.

This also applies to "data persistence". To me CLI and data persistence are more like the basis you build the MVP on. the actual features need these basis to perform on.
</panel>

