<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/166" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4. :fas-info-circle: When do benefits no longer justify the cost when doing code refactoring?**
</div>

When reading through Week 5's topics, on the section of refactoring, section 5.5 quotes:

> It is too much refactoring when the benefits no longer justify the cost. The costs and the benefits depend on the context.

They then go on to compare two refactoring methods, which are opposites of each other.

The extract method refactors from:

'''function printOwing(invoice) ``{``
  printBanner();
  let outstanding  = calculateOutstanding();

  //print details
  console.log('name: $``{``invoice.customer``}``');
  console.log('amount: $``{``outstanding``}``');  
``}``
'''

To:

'''
function printOwing(invoice) ``{``
  printBanner();
  let outstanding  = calculateOutstanding();
  printDetails(outstanding);

  function printDetails(outstanding) ``{``
    console.log('name: $``{``invoice.customer``}``');
    console.log('amount: $``{``outstanding``}``');
  ``}``
``}``
'''

Which is the inverse of the inline method from:

'''
function getRating(driver) ``{``
  return moreThanFiveLateDeliveries(driver) ? 2 : 1;
``}``

function moreThanFiveLateDeliveries(driver) ``{``
  return driver.numberOfLateDeliveries > 5;
``}``'
'''

To:
'''
function getRating(driver) ``{``
  return (driver.numberOfLateDeliveries > 5) ? 2 : 1;
``}``
'''

All blocks of code were quoted directly from https://refactoring.com/catalog/extractFunction.html and https://refactoring.com/catalog/inlineFunction.html.

My question is what exactly do these benefits and costs refer to? And how should I justify the benefits and costs when refactoring, and when exactly does the cost outweigh the benefits?



</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/157" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**5. :fas-info-circle: Are boolean parameters like true and false bad for readability?**
</div>

While looking through iPs for this week's tutorial preparation, I noticed one user has its `Task` class use boolean constructor parameter to represent the task's status:

'''java
public Task(String description, boolean isDone) ``{``
    this.description = description;
    this.isDone = isDone;
``}``
'''

This is appropriate since `isDone` clearly communicates the `Task`'s status

'''java
boolean isDone = parts[1].equals("1");
new Todo(description, isDone);
'''

But then I thought about what if a boolean like `true` or `false` was passed in directly:

'''java
new Task("read book", false);
'''

At the call site, `false` doesn't clearly communicate its purpose unless I check the constructor and method definition.

Would alternatives such as:

'''java
Task.incomplete("read book");
'''

or even:

'''java
new Task("read book", TaskStatus.INCOMPLETE);
'''

be better for readability and understanding? Or is that unnecessary abstraction for such a simple boolean representation. To me the main issue with boolean parameters is that `true` and `false` appears without context sometimes, causing the code reader (me) to look for the method definition again, disrupting the flow.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/151" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**6. :fas-info-circle: How much implementation detail to include in a UML class diagram?**
</div>

We are taught that a UML class diagram doesn't need to show every attribute and method in the actual implementation. This means we can omit some details in the diagram and diagrams to be studied also normally omit some details.

However, I was wondering what is considered "relevant", especially for methods.

For example I have
```
public class Parser ``{``
    public Command parse(String input) ``{``
        String commandWord = getCommandWord(input);
        return createCommand(commandWord, input);
    ``}``

    private String getCommandWord(String input) ``{``
         ... 
    ``}``

    private Command createCommand(String commandWord, String input) ``{``
         ... 
    ``}``
``}``
```

If I were to draw a class diagram, would it be better to only show the `+parse(input): Command` since the two private methods are implementation details? This also applies to constructors, should they normally be shown if they don't communicate anything important? And what if these private methods and constructors are actually essential to understand the diagram, should they be included?

So what's the rule of thumb to decide the appropriate level of detail in a class diagram or does it depend on the purpose of the diagram?

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/112#issuecomment-5482009158" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**7 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think this question is more than just "AI can make mistakes". AI can generate code that looks correctly, but actually inconsistent with what the user was trying to implement with the rest of the project. It might invent methods/APIs that don't exist, or fully misunderstand a class' responsibility if JavaDoc comments were missing, or solve one issue to pass a testcase but unknowingly fail another.

Therefore project context becomes even more important when using AI to code, as well as endlessly testing, reviewing code and doing QA before accepting changes to push out the final code.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/116#issuecomment-5482143736" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**8 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Gnanes99 covered this point well but to add on to his point, I think one way to differentiate the two is to look at whether there's already an existing product which has behaviours and constraints you are expected to maintain, instead of whether there is already existing code.

Having starting code doesn't necessarily make a project brownfield if that code is mainly skeleton. We are still creating the actual product, deciding the behaviour and features, and setting the limitations ourselves. I would consider that greenfield. This also seems to fit the iP where some starting code and skeleton was provided but we are still expected to personalise it and build it ourselves.

For tP, we have an existing address book and limitations are given to maintain it as an address book, only allowing us to add features to it but not morph to another whole different product. That seems closer to brownfield development.

I would think the distinction is whether you are expected to build a whole new product (greenfield) or expected to maintain and improve the existing product (brownfield), and not looking at whether code is provided or not.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/117#issuecomment-5482350609" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**9 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I believe we should not deliberately choose a vague name just in case the method changes later. If the responsibility of that previous method changes significantly enough for its old name to become inaccurate, that might itself is a sign that the method's expected behaviours has changed and an entire change in method is appropriate.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/124#issuecomment-5494017723" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**10 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I also struggled with understanding the dependency arrow at the start, but one way that helped me understand the direction is to relate the dependency arrow back to code.

If class A uses class B, for example:

```
class A ``{``
    void doSomething(B b) ``{``
        b.run();
    ``}``
``}``
```

then changes to B could potentially affect A, because A is using B. So I would read this as A depends on B, and the dependency arrow goes:

A --> B

Perhaps the teaching team could consider adding an example like this in the notes for better understanding.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/127#issuecomment-5504110537" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**11 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I agree with @zikiai that the variable declaration alone does not enforce the UML multiplicity. Java only guarantees the type of the reference via declaration, but not that the multiplicity or whether it is non-null. The implementation in code must then enforce this constraint when creating constructors and setters after the engineer has understood the UML diagram. 

If the association should not change, the field can also be made final to ensure so.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/139#issuecomment-5527228761" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**12 :fas-comment:**
