I think greenfield project doesn't always necessarily mean having complete freedom over every technical decision. Even in an actual greenfield project, there could be possible limitations such as the project's/company's tech stack, deployment environment, compatibility issues and what libraries are approved for usage. However it is still greenfield in the sense that we are creating a new product instead of developing an existing one.

Therefore I don't think requiring Java makes the iP less greenfield. The greenfield aspect means having freedom over our class design, implementation and additional features, but Java is one of the project constraints.

However, I still do like the idea of additional experimentation after meeting the learning objectives.
