<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/138#issuecomment-5527363279" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**13 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Yes, these two ideas are very alike. For the purposes of this course, I would keep to the terminology used by the textbook. From what I understand the textbook describes **composition** as a strong whole-part relationship and **aggregation** is described as a weaker container-contained relationship. For me the key difference is whether the object contained is an integral part of the other object.

For a simple example, a `SportsClub` can have `Person` members, but I wouldn't say a `Person` is a "part" of the `SportsClub` unlike how `Chapter` can be described as part of a `Book`. The `Person` is not dependent on the club.

So calling aggregation a "weak whole-part relationship" is a broader way to describe it, while `container-containee` is a useful distinction used by our course to avoid confusing it with composition.

I think for our UML diagrams/exams I would follow the course's terminology unless stated otherwise.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/133#issuecomment-5527492098" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**14 :fas-comment:**
