Nice tip. I think one thing worth noting is that auto-formatting doesn't necessarily mean the indentation follows the project's coding standard, since the IDE formatter follows whatever configuration the IDE currently has. If two teammates have different configuration settings, pressing the both auto-indentation shortcut could produce different formatting.

For tP, might be useful for everyone to make sure their IDE uses the same project code-style settings if you plan to utilise this shortcut!

Also you are right that Checkstyle checks whether code violates specified rules while formatter changes code according to indentation and spacing rules. So running the formatter can save a lot of time but Checkstyle is still useful and not a replacement.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/146#issuecomment-5545412848" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**15 :fas-comment:**
</div>

The alias sure is convenient but do be slightly careful about combining `add`, `commit` and `push` into a single command.

In particular, using `git add .` means everything that was modified would be staged without us consciously checking. If there were debugging or unrelated modifications that we forgot about, we would be accidentally entering everything into the commit.

One benefit of keeping perhaps `git add` separate is the chance to review which exact files is going into the commit. I think this is especially important for project work. The few extra commands actually helps as a useful review checkpoint.

Just some food for thought!
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/151#issuecomment-5549845772" expanded>
