<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/154#issuecomment-5557511235" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**17 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think the number of files to be changed doesn't correlate with how extensible the design is but more of what we are changing in each file.

Like adding a new command might require changing parser, UI and tests. This technically requires making changes to a few files, but if each change is small and makes sense for that class's responsibility, it doesn't mean that the design is extensible.

I feel like a bigger problem is when we add one feature which forces us to change the same few logical conditions in other classes, which could show that some form of abstraction is missing. So the problem to me isn't how many files needs to be changed but whether there is a consistent pattern of changes to be made in those files to control the same behaviour.

This way we can avoid adding extra interfaces or classes just to reduce the number of file changes, even though the original design was already easy to maintain.

</panel>

