<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/177#issuecomment-5630115474" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**20 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Maybe we can trying looking at whether the helper hides an implementation or is just repeating the method in plain English.

Example for:

`moreThanFiveLateDeliveries(driver)`

isn't hiding the implementation since number 5 is a magic literal and is exposed in the method name. If the requirement changes from lets say 5 to 3, the method would be obsolete and needs to be renamed. I can see why the inline function is usable here to remove it, since the helper only adds another obsolete method.

The method like:

`isFrequentlyLate(driver)`

is a more useful abstraction since `getRating()` only needs to know whether the driver satisfies that rule, but not what the exact threshold currently is.

And the lower-level method:

`return driver.numberOfLateDeliveries > LATE_DELIVERY_THRESHOLD;`

Here, extracting a method makes more sense since its name introduces some meaning at the caller level. It also doesn't contain magic literals. Otherwise inlining probably makes the code easier to follow.

I guess SLAP and inline don't really contradict. SLAP says statements should be roughly the same level of abstraction, while inline says remove helpers that don't provide a useful abstraction and only add extra methods.

So a possible rule to follow is to extract when helper hides a lower-level rule behind a meaningful concept, inline when the helper is basically another non-useful level of abstraction.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/175#issuecomment-5630167968" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**21 :fas-comment:**
</div>

On top of discussing about bugs, there might be future extensibility to worry about here.

Suppose `list 67` behaves like `list` because everything after `list` is ignored.

But later on if we add a feature like `list 10` to intentionally display the first 10 tasks only, the previous input of `list 67` would now have a different meaning. Because of this I would lean toward rejecting certain arguments that logically doesn't makes sense instead of ignoring them. This can make accepted command grammar clearer, and prevent unintentional design flaws if we want to add new features later.

Of course every strange input doesn't need its own special error handling. Maybe unknown arguments can fall under just one generic invalid-format error.


</panel>

