<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/211#issuecomment-5667260545" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**29 :fas-comment:**
</div>

One possibility is that multiple changes across components means that abstraction boundary is flawed, instead of the original implementation being architectural.

For example, if `Model` internally stores tasks using an `ArrayList`. If we change it to another collection and `Logic`, `Ui` and `Storage` all need changes because they relied on `ArrayList` behaviour, then maybe the bigger issue is that those components knew too much about how Model stores its data.

I think this is a matter of representation. Whether `Model` uses `ArrayList` or another collection data type can be detailed design, but decisions like "tasks preserve insertion order" or "duplicate tasks cannot exist" start to become architectural if multiple components depend on those.

So whether a design decision is architecturally significant is not determined by the number of affected components but also whether they depend on intentional system-level contracts, or accidentally depends on an implementation detail that should be abstracted.
</panel>

