### 12. GOEL..RTIN `@Krtin888` (30 posts) <span class=text-warning>:octicon-eye:</span>
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/213" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1. :fas-info-circle: 💡 Java 25 native-access warning does not necessarily mean startup failed**
</div>

While smoke-testing a JavaFX JAR on Java 25, I saw this warning:

'''text
WARNING: A restricted method in java.lang.System has been called
WARNING: java.lang.System::load has been called by com.sun.glass.utils.NativeLibLoader in module javafx.graphics
WARNING: Use --enable-native-access=javafx.graphics to avoid a warning for callers in this module
'''

This warning by itself does **not** mean the app failed to start. In JDK 24 and later, the default `--illegal-native-access=warn` mode still allows the native-library operation and prints a warning. So if the GUI does not open, look for a later exception or another cause rather than treating these lines as the error.

For this specific warning, the command can be run as:

'''bash
java --enable-native-access=javafx.graphics -jar your-app.jar
'''

I would use the module named in the warning rather than `ALL-UNNAMED`, as it grants access more narrowly. This is also worth addressing before a future JDK changes the default from warning to denial.

Oracle's Java 25 migration guide explains the behaviour and options: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/migrate/migrating-jdk-8-later-jdk-releases.html#GUID-C31C88DF-9F6B-4B17-9FB0-65B8EDEFD2CA

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/158" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2. :fas-info-circle: Should the Week 5 fat JAR include mac-aarch64 JavaFX artifacts?**
</div>

## Context

The [JavaFX tutorial](https://se-education.org/guides/tutorials/javaFxPart1.html#setting-up-java-fx) asks us to include the `win`, `mac`, and `linux` JavaFX 17.0.7 artifacts and says all of them are needed for the app to work on all three operating systems. Week 5 also says the fat JAR should bundle JavaFX so that it works on computers where JavaFX is not installed.

I am using an Apple-silicon Mac (`uname -m` reports `arm64`). While testing my Level-10 app with a regular ARM OpenJDK 25 installation, `./gradlew run` loaded `javafx-graphics-17.0.7-win.jar` and then failed because the extracted native library was `x86_64` rather than `arm64`:

'''text
libprism_es2.dylib: mach-o file, but is an incompatible architecture
(have 'x86_64', need 'arm64e' or 'arm64')
'''

Using the `mac-aarch64` JavaFX artifacts allowed the same application code to run. OpenJFX also lists `mac-aarch64` as a separate supported target from `mac`: https://github.com/openjfx/javafx-gradle-plugin#4-cross-platform-projects-and-libraries

I understand that Mac users are required to use the prescribed `25.0.3.fx-zulu` JDK+FX distribution, and I will switch to that for the course setup. My question is specifically about the **distributed Week 5 fat JAR**:

1. Is the JAR expected to run on Apple-silicon Macs that have a normal JDK but no separately installed JavaFX?
2. If yes, should we add the `mac-aarch64` JavaFX dependencies alongside the exact `win`/`mac`/`linux` list in the tutorial?

I am hesitant to change the dependency list without confirmation because the Week 4 instructions specifically tell us to follow the sample `build.gradle` closely.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/126#issuecomment-5535428616" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I tried another variation in my iP while keeping the CLI and GUI on the same command-processing path.

I retained one `executeCommand(String input, Ui outputUi)` method for parsing, task updates, storage, and error handling. The CLI passes its console `Ui`. For the GUI, `getResponse(input)` creates a temporary `Ui` backed by a `ByteArrayOutputStream`, calls the same method, and returns the captured response text. The JavaFX controller therefore only calls `chris.getResponse(input)` and renders the result.

This avoided duplicating command logic and kept `Parser`, `TaskList`, and `Storage` independent of JavaFX. It also made switching between the CLI and GUI straightforward.

One trade-off is that the application layer still produces console-formatted text. If the application later needed richer responses—such as different message types, styling, or attached task data—I would replace the returned `String` with a small `Response` object. For this iP’s synchronous command-response flow, though, injecting the chatbot into the JavaFX controller seemed simpler than introducing a queue or asynchronous listener layer.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/157#issuecomment-5558020704" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I took a third option in my iP: the normal constructor accepts only the description and always creates an incomplete task. When loading saved data, `Storage` uses that same constructor and calls `markAsDone()` only when the stored status is `1`.

That keeps the common call site clear:

'''java
new Todo("read book");
'''

and confines the raw `"1"`/boolean interpretation to the persistence boundary instead of exposing it throughout the model API.

There is a trade-off: loading a completed task briefly creates it as incomplete and then mutates it. That is harmless in my current iP, but if construction later triggers listeners or stricter invariants, I would switch to an explicitly named factory such as `Task.fromStorage(description, isDone)`. For exactly two stable states, I don't think an enum adds much; the more useful distinction is between a normal user-created task and reconstruction from storage.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/163#issuecomment-5573215421" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**5 :fas-comment:**
</div>

CSS can handle the resizing choices here without moving the background into the controller. The JavaFX CSS reference lists three useful values for `-fx-background-size`:

'''css
/* preserve aspect ratio and crop overflow */
-fx-background-size: cover;

/* preserve aspect ratio and show the whole image, possibly leaving empty bands */
-fx-background-size: contain;

/* fill both dimensions, but allow distortion */
-fx-background-size: stretch;
'''

So the current scaling problem may be the expected result of `cover`, rather than a limitation of CSS: it deliberately crops whichever dimension overflows. If showing the entire image matters more, I would try `contain` together with a suitable background colour for any exposed area. If filling the pane matters more, keep `cover` and use an image whose important content is near the centre.

Since this background is static, I agree that CSS keeps the controller simpler. I would use Java only if the image or sizing policy needs to change based on application state.

Reference: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/java-components/javafx/26/docs/javafx.graphics/javafx/scene/doc-files/cssref.html

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/158#issuecomment-5573216031" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**6 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Thanks for the clarification. I understand the required compatibility target now: the JAR needs to work on Macs using the prescribed JDK+FX, not on an ordinary ARM JDK without JavaFX.

The `./gradlew run` failure under my regular ARM OpenJDK therefore isn't evidence that I should change the tutorial's dependency list. I'll keep the specified dependencies, switch to `25.0.3.fx-zulu`, and test the generated fat JAR directly with the prescribed setup. Closing this as answered.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/166#issuecomment-5580878626" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**7 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think the apparent contradiction disappears if we treat extraction and inlining as tools for different code smells, rather than rules that one form is always better.

The benefits I would look for are:

- a meaningful name replaces low-level details, so the caller reads at one level of abstraction;
- the extracted behaviour can be reused or tested independently;
- a likely future change is localised in one place;
- the original method no longer has several responsibilities.

The costs include another place a reader must jump to, parameters/state that must be threaded through the new method, a larger API if the method has to be exposed, and the risk/retesting effort of changing working code. A tiny extracted method whose name merely repeats its implementation can therefore cost more than it helps.

For example, in an iP parser, extracting deadline/event parsing became useful once the input loop was also handling command dispatch, index validation, and several date delimiters. It gives those parsing rules a clear home and makes edge cases testable without driving the GUI. In contrast, extracting a one-off condition such as `taskIndex &lt; 0` into `isLessThanZero(taskIndex)` would add a hop without adding domain meaning; that is a good candidate to keep inline.

My practical test would be:

1. Identify the specific problem first (mixed responsibilities, duplication, unclear intent, or a change that is difficult to make).
2. Apply the smallest refactoring that addresses it while keeping behaviour unchanged.
3. Run the existing tests, then reread the caller and extracted code together.
4. Keep it only if the expected maintenance/readability gain is greater than the extra indirection and change risk.

So the threshold is contextual, but the justification can still be concrete: name the present code smell, the likely reader/change being helped, and the new complexity introduced. If those cannot be stated, the refactoring is probably speculative.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/171#issuecomment-5595111185" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**8 :fas-comment:**
</div>

One practical benefit is that an issue can record the evidence needed to reproduce and verify a bug, while a commit message alone usually cannot. For an iP/tP bug I would keep the issue small but include:

- exact steps or command input that reproduce it;
- expected and actual output;
- the version/commit and relevant environment details;
- a short acceptance check for deciding that it is fixed.

Then the implementation branch/PR can reference the issue. If the PR description contains `Fixes #123`, GitHub links the discussion to the code and closes the issue when that PR is merged. I would avoid closing the issue merely when the first fixing commit is written: until the fix is reviewed, tested, and integrated into the default branch, the bug still exists in the product's main line.

For a tiny solo iP, recording every typo as an issue may indeed be more ceremony than value. My rule of thumb would be to open one when the bug might be forgotten, needs investigation, has a useful regression case, or needs coordination. A fix that is obvious and completed immediately can stay as one focused commit. In the tP, the same record becomes more valuable because another teammate can reproduce, assign, review, and verify it without relying on the original reporter's memory.

GitHub documents the closing-keyword workflow here: https://docs.github.com/en/issues/tracking-your-work-with-issues/using-issues/linking-a-pull-request-to-an-issue

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/168#issuecomment-5608240829" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**9 :fas-comment:**
</div>

One workflow that helped me with the Week 5 parallel increments was giving each branch its own Git worktree. For example:

'''bash
git worktree add ../ip-assertions -b branch-A-Assertions master
git worktree add ../ip-codequality -b branch-A-CodeQuality master
git worktree add ../ip-streams -b branch-A-Streams master
'''

This gave the coding agent a separate directory for each increment, so changes for Assertions, Code Quality, and Streams did not get mixed together or require repeatedly stashing files. I could also run the tests and inspect the diff for each branch independently.

The main caveat is that worktrees do not remove the need to understand the merge. After merging the first PR, I still need to merge the updated `master` into the remaining branches as required by the course workflow. If there is a conflict, I would not let the agent accept one side automatically; the resolved code has to preserve the intent of both increments.

`git worktree list` is useful for checking which directory belongs to each branch, especially because Git prevents the same branch from being checked out in two worktrees at once.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/181#issuecomment-5644166740" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**10 :fas-comment:**
