<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/297#issuecomment-5701427798" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**13 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Your interpretation matches what I found when I reproduced this on Zulu OpenJDK 25.0.3. In JDK 24 and later, the default behaviour is `--illegal-native-access=warn`: the native-library operation is still allowed, and the JVM prints one warning for the module. Therefore, these lines alone do not mean the JAR failed; we should look for a later exception if the app does not start.

Using the module named in the warning is the narrow fix:

'''bash
java --enable-native-access=javafx.graphics -jar YOUR_JAR_NAME.jar
'''

I would not use `--illegal-native-access=allow` merely to hide it, as that grants access without identifying the module. The warning also matters for future JDKs because Oracle says denial will become the default later, but on the prescribed Java 25 setup it is currently a warning rather than an execution failure.

Oracle's Java 25 migration guide documents the default modes and `--enable-native-access` option: https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/migrate/migrating-jdk-8-later-jdk-releases.html#GUID-C31C88DF-9F6B-4B17-9FB0-65B8EDEFD2CA

</panel>

