Sorry I missed this mention earlier. Yes, I meant that a small refactoring can be tried and then discarded if the resulting code is not actually clearer.

The time already spent making the change is a sunk cost; keeping it only because it has been completed can leave permanent indirection or complexity in the code. Sometimes the trade-off is also easier to judge from the concrete diff than from a hypothetical version. Since refactoring should preserve behaviour, I can compare both versions after running the same tests and revert the change safely if it did not produce the expected benefit.

I would still identify a specific problem before starting rather than refactoring randomly. The sequence I intended was: form a hypothesis such as “extracting this block will make the caller read at one level”, make the smallest reversible change, compare the result, and keep it only if that hypothesis holds.

</panel>

