I hit the overwrite risk while updating my iP today. My earlier load path caught a malformed saved line, displayed an error, then started with an empty in-memory list. The dangerous part was **the next successful `todo`**: saving that list would replace the original file, including all its valid records.

I chose a conservative alternative to partial recovery for now: if any saved line cannot be parsed, Chris reports its line number and starts in **read-only mode**. `help`, `list`, and exit still work, but commands that change tasks are refused until the user backs up/repairs the file and restarts. This sacrifices convenience (you cannot work with the other 19 tasks immediately), but it makes the no-silent-data-loss guarantee simple.

I added a regression test with a valid first line and malformed second line: attempt `todo`, then assert that the original file's bytes are unchanged. If I later implement partial recovery, I'd keep that same test and add the explicit backup/confirmation step others suggested before the first save. The important distinction seems to be not just *how many records load*, but *what the next save is allowed to do*.

</panel>

