The error is likely a spelling/capitalisation mismatch rather than an inheritance problem. Java is case-sensitive, so the method and annotation need to be exactly:

'''java
@Override
public int hashCode() ``{``
    return value.hashCode();
``}``
'''

In your comments I noticed `hasCode` (missing the `h` in `hash`) and `@OverRide` (capital `R`). Either would cause an error: `hasCode()` does not override `Object.hashCode()`, and the annotation is named `Override`, not `OverRide`. Also check that the method is directly inside the `Remark` class rather than accidentally nested inside another method. I would keep the annotation, because it lets the compiler catch exactly this kind of mismatch.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/542#issuecomment-5782113601" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**24 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think a more useful replacement would be: **"Precondition: The machine is idle and no other transaction is in progress."**

That precondition identifies a specific system state required before this use case can begin. In contrast, "all hardware is working" is too broad and applies to practically every use case.

I would avoid making "the card is valid/readable" a precondition, because checking the card can reasonably be part of the main flow, with an invalid or unreadable card handled as an extension. Likewise, a total power or hardware failure is not a useful extension because the system cannot continue with meaningful behaviour.

If the machine being idle is already assumed globally, then omitting the precondition entirely may be cleaner.
</panel>

