<panel type="info"  collapsed>
<div slot="header">

### 13. SPEN..HENG `@spencercdz` (25 posts) 
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/126#issuecomment-5511708465" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think the event handler is a reasonable boundary here. handleUserInput() does not need to contain the chatbot logic. It can act as the GUI controller: read the text field, call a method on the chatbot/application layer, then render the returned response.

For example, the flow can be:
`GUI event handler -&gt; Duke/Logic.process(input) -&gt; response -&gt; GUI renders response`

That keeps the GUI responsible for JavaFX controls and display, while Duke or Logic owns parsing commands, updating tasks, and producing a response. You do not need to simulate two servers or use a BlockingQueue unless you have a separate long-running task to move off the JavaFX UI thread.

As the project grows, you can extract the handler’s work into a small controller method, but the callback itself is still the normal way for JavaFX to pass a user action into the application.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/125#issuecomment-5511764903" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

A repository-level `.gitattributes` is a good way to give the whole team one predictable policy. Your example is a sensible starting point:

`* text=auto`
`*.java text eol=lf`
`*.gradle text eol=lf`

It works at Git's checkout and commit boundary, so it should not conflict with IntelliJ, Gradle, or a teammate's local `core.autocrlf` setting. The important practical point is to add it in its own commit, then have one person run `git add --renormalize .` and commit any line-ending-only changes separately. That avoids burying a large mechanical diff inside a functional pull request.

Afterwards, `git ls-files --eol` is useful for checking the tracked files' effective line-ending settings. The course progress tools look at repository history and refs, so a consistent line-ending policy should not affect them.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/132#issuecomment-5511767125" expanded>
