<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/139#issuecomment-5524272220" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I can see the appeal of allowing different languages and interfaces. It would make the project feel more genuinely greenfield and let students explore tools they may use beyond the course.

The trade-off is that iP is also a shared learning sequence. Java, Gradle, JavaFX, the coding standard, the progress tracker, and peer-help examples give everyone a common baseline. Supporting multiple languages would make the learning resources, testing expectations, and peer feedback much harder to keep comparable.

A middle ground could be to keep the assessed iP in Java, but offer an ungraded implementation variant or showcase track where students can rebuild a later level in another stack. That preserves the common foundation while still encouraging exploration.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/151#issuecomment-5548724590" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**5 :fas-comment:**
</div>

A useful rule is to make the diagram support its intended discussion, rather than mirror the implementation.

For a high-level design diagram, showing `+parse(input): Command` is likely enough because it communicates the class’s responsibility and the public operation other classes rely on. The private helper methods can be omitted if they only describe how `Parser` performs that work internally.

Include a private method, field, or constructor when leaving it out would hide an important design idea, such as a meaningful dependency, an invariant, a distinct creation path, or a collaboration that the diagram is meant to explain. Constructors are usually omitted unless construction itself is relevant, for example when different constructors establish different object states.

It is also fine to use separate diagrams at different levels of detail. A broad class diagram can show the public-facing structure, while a focused design diagram can include selected private details for one complicated area.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/84#issuecomment-5548797094" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**6 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think there is one important caveat to the idea that AI can generate the code and then generate tests for all of it: the tests are not an independent check if the same interpretation produced both the implementation and the tests.

An AI can consistently misunderstand a requirement, implement that misunderstanding, and write tests that confirm it. Passing tests then only show consistency with its own interpretation, not necessarily correctness against the actual requirements.

A stronger workflow is to derive acceptance cases from the task specification before reviewing the generated code, then use those cases as an independent oracle. The key comparison is not simply “write 10,000 lines” versus “understand 10,000 lines”, but the cost of validating the requirements, boundaries, and later changes when the original design decisions were made in one large step.

This does not make one-shot generation useless. It just moves much of the work from implementation to specification, review, and validation.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/75#issuecomment-5548797095" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**7 :fas-comment:**
</div>

One nuance about the generic `Parser&lt;T extends Command>` idea: it is useful when code that holds the parser also needs to retain the concrete result type. But the central dispatcher ultimately treats every result as a `Command`, so its registry naturally becomes `Map&lt;String, Parser&lt;? extends Command>>` and the concrete type is no longer visible there.

Also, Java already permits a covariant return type:

'''java
interface CommandParser ``{``
    Command parse(String arguments) throws ParseException;
``}``

final class TodoCommandParser implements CommandParser ``{``
    @Override
    public TodoCommand parse(String arguments) throws ParseException ``{``
        // ...
    ``}``
``}``
'''

A test that works directly with `TodoCommandParser` can still receive `TodoCommand` without a cast. So I would start with the non-generic interface unless a real caller benefits from preserving the concrete type through an abstraction.

The more important design choice is keeping parsing rules close to the command grammar while leaving the central parser responsible only for splitting the command word, dispatching, and producing a consistent unknown-command error. A `switch` and a registry are both reasonable ways to do that.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/155#issuecomment-5552759323" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**8 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Spotless and Checkstyle can coexist, because they solve different problems:

- Spotless is primarily a formatter. It rewrites code into a chosen layout.
- Checkstyle verifies code against a rule set and reports violations.

For the A-CheckStyle increment, I would keep Spotless if you like its formatting workflow, but also add the course Checkstyle configuration and run it as the course guide requires. That gives you an independent check that your code follows the expected SE-EDU rules.

The main thing to watch for is formatter conflict. If Spotless and Checkstyle disagree about imports, whitespace, or wrapping, avoid repeatedly reformatting until the tools fight each other. Configure Spotless to use compatible rules where possible, and treat Checkstyle as the final source for rules required by the increment.

You should not need to remove Spotless just to install Checkstyle. Add Checkstyle, run the Gradle task, and address any actual violations it reports.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/160#issuecomment-5578098977" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**9 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think there is a middle ground between replacing the tutorial’s beginner path and leaving a confusing behaviour undocumented.

The tutorial can keep its current main flow, while adding a short troubleshooting note that the `vvalueProperty()` binding may prevent manual scrolling in some JavaFX setups. The note could point to the listener-based alternative for students who encounter that behaviour, without requiring everyone to understand `Platform.runLater` immediately.

That would be useful because the current symptom looks like a broken `ScrollPane`, not an intentional trade-off. Even if the alternative remains optional, documenting the cause and workaround would save students time and give them a clear path forward.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/166#issuecomment-5585261770" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**10 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think trying a small refactor and then discarding it can still be worthwhile. Tests only show that behaviour was preserved. They do not tell us whether the resulting design is actually easier to read or change.

A small, isolated refactoring commit makes this cheap: compare the before and after versions, keep it if the name or separation clarifies a real responsibility, and amend or revert it if it only adds indirection. That is not wasted work, because the comparison gives concrete evidence about whether the proposed abstraction helps in this codebase.

The key is to keep the experiment small and behaviour-preserving, rather than changing structure and functionality at the same time.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/169#issuecomment-5585261769" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**11 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Extracting repeated assertions into a private helper can be a good idea when they together express one named invariant, such as “an entry is valid” or “the task list is internally consistent”.

One important caveat: Java `assert` statements are normally disabled unless assertions are explicitly enabled at runtime. They are therefore suitable for programmer assumptions and internal invariants, but not for validating user input or other conditions the application must always enforce. For those, use an explicit check and exception instead.

I would avoid extracting assertions merely because the syntax repeats. The helper should describe a meaningful rule, and its call sites should make sense as places where that rule must hold.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/168#issuecomment-5586686962" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**12 :fas-comment:**
</div>

A workflow I find useful is to treat AI answers as claims that need evidence, rather than accepting them based on how confident they sound.

For example:

- If I ask whether a course increment requires something, I ask the AI to link the exact course-guide section instead of relying on a summary.
- If I ask about an API behaviour, I ask it to point to the relevant Javadoc or reproduce the behaviour with a minimal example.
- Before allowing code changes, I ask it to list the files it plans to inspect and why. Afterwards, I review `git diff` and run the relevant tests myself.

This turns vague follow-ups like “are you sure?” into checks that are easier to verify. AI is still useful for exploring options and explaining unfamiliar code, but the developer should be able to trace important decisions back to requirements, source code, or test results.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/171#issuecomment-5595110409" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**13 :fas-comment:**
</div>

One practice that makes an issue tracker especially valuable is linking the bug report to the change that fixes it. For example, include `Fixes #12` in the pull request or commit message where appropriate. Then a future reader can trace the bug, the reasoning, and the code change together.

For a small iP, a lightweight issue is enough: a short reproduction step, expected versus actual behaviour, and a `bug` label. You do not need to turn every small to-do into an issue, but issues are helpful when a problem needs prioritisation, investigation, or a record of how it was resolved.

This keeps the tracker useful without creating unnecessary process overhead.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/172#issuecomment-5596489210" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**14 :fas-comment:**
</div>

There are two separate things to check here: the actual file size and whether the JAR is self-contained.

First, 11,304 KB is roughly 11 MB, so it is not below 5 MB. If the CS2103T requirement has a 5 MB limit, it is worth checking that requirement and the units before changing anything else.

For JavaFX, make sure you are testing the built JAR itself, not Gradle’s development classpath:

'''sh
./gradlew clean shadowJar
java -jar build/libs/&lt;your-jar-file>.jar
'''

If that command starts the GUI successfully, it is strong evidence that the JAR includes the JavaFX runtime files it needs. `./gradlew run` is not the same test because Gradle supplies dependencies separately while developing.

You can inspect the archive directly too:

'''sh
jar tf build/libs/&lt;your-jar-file>.jar | grep '&hat;javafx/'
'''

Entries such as `javafx/application/Application.class` show that JavaFX classes have been bundled. The larger file size can be expected when a self-contained JavaFX application includes its runtime dependencies.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/189#issuecomment-5645327955" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**15 :fas-comment:**
</div>

One practical way to read the difference is through the missing path. In an `alt` fragment, each operand represents a possible path and has a guard; one applicable operand is selected. With only one operand, the other outcome is implicitly “no interaction”, which is why it has the same effect as `opt`.

So use `opt [condition]` when the false case genuinely does nothing, and use `alt` once you need to show a distinct alternative, such as `[valid] process()` versus `[else] showError()`.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/192#issuecomment-5653604983" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**16 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Smoke-tested the updated Jarvis v0.2 release on macOS using Zulu OpenJDK 25.0.3. I downloaded the JAR into an isolated empty folder and verified its SHA-256:

`9134b54924c7242372dd6a882ef018466cc1fcfd7eeebeb24d823657b227281b`

`java -jar jarvis.jar` started the desktop application successfully without an application exception.

