<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/196#issuecomment-5653801240" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**17 :fas-comment:**
</div>

## MVP scope

The course page does not prescribe a fixed number of MVP features. Its FAQ says to aim for the **smallest set of features the product cannot do without**, and that even the most basic version of those features is enough initially.

So a five-feature MVP can be sufficient if those five features together allow your intended users to complete the product’s main purpose. The target is not to match AB3’s number of features or to include every useful idea from the start.

A practical way to decide is to assess each proposed feature against these questions:

1. What is the main job our target user needs this product to do?
2. If we remove this feature, can the user still complete that job using the remaining features?
3. Is the feature essential, or does it mainly make the workflow faster or more convenient?

If users can still achieve the same outcome with a reasonable workaround, it is likely a nice-to-have. The course page gives a similar example: an `add` feature is essential, while `edit` may be deferred because users can delete and re-add an item instead.

Therefore, commands such as `add`, `delete`, and `find` are not automatically required just because they exist in AB3. They should be included only when they are needed for your own target users and product direction. Likewise, filtering can be a must-have if your users cannot realistically use the product’s core workflow without it, but it can be postponed if it only improves convenience.

It may help to list the features as must-have and nice-to-have. Start with the smallest coherent set of must-haves, then add the nice-to-haves only if time permits. The feature specification is assessed based on whether it covers the smallest set of features the product cannot do without, rather than whether it reaches an arbitrary feature count.

## UI mock-ups

The requirement is for **relevant UI mock-ups**, and the course page says these can be low-fidelity and temporary. I would not create a separate mock-up for every possible state or every combination of errors.

Instead, include:

- The normal, intended flow for each feature.
- Any distinct state that materially changes what the user sees or understands, such as an invalid input message, duplicate warning, empty result, or confirmation screen.
- Enough detail to make the intended GUI behaviour clear to your teammates.

The written feature specification should still define the precise expected output for both successful and failed commands, even if there is no separate mock-up for every individual error case.

In short, five features may be enough. What matters is whether they form the smallest complete feature set for your target users, and whether the behaviour of each feature is thought through clearly.

Source: [Week 6 tP instructions](https://nus-cs2103-ay2627-s1.github.io/website/schedule/week6/project.html#tp-define-the-mvp)
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/201#issuecomment-5658075521" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**18 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Adding a nuance to the earlier reply: I would separate a feature from both implementation details and product constraints. A feature should describe user-visible behaviour that fulfils one of your selected user stories. So command-based navigation is likely a constraint or interaction style for this CLI product, rather than a feature by itself.

Data persistence depends on its role. If your target users need their records to survive restarts, it can be a feature such as “save and reload records”. However, it should still be described in user-facing terms: when saving and loading happen, what the user sees, and how cases such as missing or invalid data are handled. It should not be listed only as an internal decision like “store data in a file”.

The Week 6 page frames the task as translating selected user stories into a feature list, then asks for a precise command format and expected outputs for each feature. That suggests using user needs and observable behaviour, rather than internal mechanisms, to decide what belongs in the list.

Source: [Week 6 tP instructions](https://nus-cs2103-ay2627-s1.github.io/website/schedule/week6/project.html#2-draft-the-feature-specification-sun-sep-20th-23-59)
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/207#issuecomment-5663439682" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**19 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Start with one concrete scenario, such as “the user adds a valid task”, and include only the messages needed to explain how that scenario produces its observable result. Show calls that cross participants or represent an important decision, validation, creation, or state change.

You do not need to show every helper method. Omit a helper if removing it does not make the main interaction or outcome harder to understand. Include it when it explains a non-obvious step, an alternative path, or an important responsibility boundary.

For an `add` flow, a useful diagram would normally begin with the user action and end with the updated response or view. Include parsing, model updates, and persistence only to the extent that they are part of the behaviour you are explaining. A simple test is: if removing a call leaves the reader unable to understand why the outcome occurs, keep it; otherwise, leave it out.

The Week 6 material describes sequence diagrams as models of interactions between entities in a specific scenario, rather than exhaustive execution traces: [W6.1 Sequence Diagrams](https://nus-cs2103-ay2627-s1.github.io/website/schedule/week6/topics.html#w61-modeling-sequence-diagrams).
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/211#issuecomment-5666898566" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**20 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I would treat cross-component change as evidence, not proof, that a decision is architectural. If the representation is genuinely private to `Model` and its interface contract stays the same, `Logic`, `Storage`, and `Ui` should not need to know or change. In that case, the representation choice remains detailed design.

If changing it forces the other components to change their interfaces, assumptions, or behaviour, the significant decision is no longer merely which internal data structure `Model` uses. It has become part of the agreement between components, and is therefore architecturally significant.

A useful practical test is to ask whether the decision:

1. Changes component responsibilities or interface contracts.
2. Constrains implementation choices or quality attributes across multiple components.
3. Is expensive to reverse once other parts rely on it.

If so, it is likely architecture. However, change spreading across components is not automatically an architectural failure. Some changes genuinely span responsibilities, such as adding a new property that affects the UI, rules, model, and storage.

Source: [W6.2 Architecture Diagrams](https://nus-cs2103-ay2627-s1.github.io/website/schedule/week6/topics.html#w62-architecture-diagrams).
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/215#issuecomment-5676157474" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**21 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Yes. If an object already exists when the scenario begins, draw its lifeline from the top of the sequence diagram. A create message only appears when the scenario itself creates that object.

For a `delete` command, the model and the existing `Person` can therefore both have lifelines that start at the top. Show the relevant calls that locate and remove the `Person`, then use a destruction marker only if the scenario actually destroys that object.

The top of the diagram marks the start of the interaction being modelled, not necessarily the moment each object was constructed. This keeps the diagram focused on the command scenario rather than unrelated earlier setup.

Source: [W6.1 Sequence Diagrams](https://nus-cs2103-ay2627-s1.github.io/website/schedule/week6/topics.html#w61-modeling-sequence-diagrams).
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/213#issuecomment-5676227996" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**22 :fas-comment:**
</div>

One practical caveat for smoke tests: try the expected `java -jar your-app.jar` command first. The extra `--enable-native-access=javafx.graphics` option can suppress the warning, but it should not hide whether the distributed JAR behaves as intended for a normal user.

If the GUI starts normally, the warning is informational. If it does not, report the later exception or symptom as the actual failure. For a packaged application, the developer can also consider declaring the required native access in the JAR manifest rather than asking every user to add an option.

Oracle’s JavaFX guide confirms that `javafx.graphics` can produce this warning and shows the option as a way to suppress it: [JavaFX 25 guide](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/java-components/javafx/25/javafx-users-guide/compiling-running-javafx-application.html).
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/218#issuecomment-5676929806" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**23 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Your distinction is sensible. The key question is whether a future maintainer would need the record to diagnose a problem. An invalid command that is expected, validated, and explained clearly to the user usually does not need a `WARNING`. It can be omitted from the log, or recorded at `FINE` when tracing a specific interaction would be useful.

Reserve `WARNING` for an unexpected but recoverable problem, such as failing to read or save a data file. Use `SEVERE` when the failure prevents the program from continuing a necessary operation safely. The level should reflect the diagnostic value and severity of the situation, not merely whether the UI displayed an error.

Also avoid logging raw user input when it may contain personal data. Log only the context needed to diagnose the fault.

The course describes logs as records for troubleshooting later and illustrates routine processing at `INFO`, with a processing error at `WARNING`: [W6.5 Logging](https://nus-cs2103-ay2627-s1.github.io/website/schedule/week6/topics.html#w65-logging).
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/219#issuecomment-5677047612" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**24 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Keep the main diagram focused on one scenario, usually the successful `add` flow. Add an `alt` fragment only when an alternative is important to the interaction you want readers to understand. It is not necessary to show every validation failure.

A separate error diagram is useful when the error path has a meaningfully different sequence of calls or participants. For example, malformed input may end within the parser, while a duplicate may reach the model before being rejected. If including both would obscure the main flow, separate diagrams are clearer.

The goal is to model a specific scenario, not produce an exhaustive execution trace. Source: [W6.1 Sequence Diagrams](https://nus-cs2103-ay2627-s1.github.io/website/schedule/week6/topics.html#w61-modeling-sequence-diagrams).
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/216#issuecomment-5677052444" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**25 :fas-comment:**
</div>

A feature need not be a direct command. Automatic saving is a feature because it is observable product behaviour that fulfils a user need: data remains available after a restart.

Editing the raw data file is slightly different. It is best described as a usage instruction or supporting capability unless the product deliberately commits to supporting a stable, editable file format. If it is supported, document its constraints clearly, because users can otherwise create invalid data.

For a feature list, name the product behaviour, such as “persist data automatically”, rather than the external action “edit the data file”. The course frames features as product designs that fulfil user needs, not as command formats only: [Week 6 project guidance](https://nus-cs2103-ay2627-s1.github.io/website/schedule/week6/project.html).
</panel>

</panel>


