Tested `kibo.jar` v0.2 on Ubuntu 22.04.5 with Zulu OpenJDK 25.0.4.1. On Linux x86_64, the JAR launched successfully from an otherwise empty folder. `list`, `find`, and `schedule 2026-09-18` worked, the GUI remained usable when resized, malformed inputs were handled without crashing, and tasks persisted after terminating and relaunching the application.

However, i found three issues:
1. `bye` displays the farewell message and disables the input, but it does not close the window or terminate the Java process. Both were still present after ten seconds.
2. An event whose end precedes its start is accepted and displayed in the schedule. For example, `event backwards /from 2026-09-18 12:00 /to 2026-09-18 10:00` is saved successfully.
3. On Linux ARM64, the JAR does not launch because its bundled JavaFX native library is AMD64. Startup fails while loading `libprism_sw.so` with a message indicating that an AMD64 `.so` cannot load on AArch64, followed by `No toolkit found`.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/218#issuecomment-5677871026" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**15 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Another thing I’d consider is where the error gets logged. If a validation failure propagates through several layers, we probably shouldn’t log the same event in the parser, command and UI layers, as that can make one user mistake look like multiple problems.

I think it’s cleaner to handle/log it once at the layer that has enough context to describe what happened. Lower layers can throw/return the error, while the appropriate boundary decides whether it is worth logging and at what level.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/219#issuecomment-5677874139" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**16 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Another option besides putting every error case into one large alt frame is to use a ref frame. If an error-handling interaction is substantial enough to deserve its own sequence diagram, the main diagram can reference that interaction instead of duplicating all the details.

That seems useful when the error path is relevant to the scenario, but showing its full sequence would make the main diagram harder to read.
</panel>

