Tested `laby.jar` from release `v0.2` on Ubuntu 22.04.5 x86_64 with Zulu OpenJDK 25.0.4.1.

The requested positive flow worked: I added a todo, deadline, event, and contact; listed them; found the deadline; marked it; deleted the event with `deletetask`; exited with `bye`; and confirmed the remaining tasks/contact persisted after relaunch.

I found two reproducible input-validation issues:

1. An impossible calendar date is accepted and silently changed. Running `deadline impossible /by 2026-09-31 12:00` adds and persists a deadline dated September 30, 2026 instead of rejecting the invalid September 31 date.
2. An event whose end is earlier than its start is accepted and persisted. Running `event backwards /from 2026-09-20 16:00 /to 2026-09-20 14:00` adds the event without an error.

I also noticed that recoverable validation messages begin with `System crashing...` even though the app remains running, and `deletecontact 0` asks for a valid task index rather than a contact index.

I did not observe an uncaught exception or application error. `bye` closed the window and application successfully.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/251#issuecomment-5691927469" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**23 :fas-comment:**
</div>

One small thing I'd add is that although the actual runtime object must be from a concrete subclass, the diagram does not always need to expose that concrete type if it is irrelevant to the interaction being modelled. Since a subclass object is also an instance of its superclass, you can sometimes show the participant using the more general type to keep the diagram focused on the relevant abstraction.

So I’d distinguish between the actual object being concrete and the type/detail we choose to show in the diagram.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/252#issuecomment-5691941681" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**24 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I don’t think there is a requirement to write a separate justification just because a method is ~40 lines long. The course guideline says that once a method goes beyond 30 lines, we should consider whether it can be shortened, rather than treating 30 lines as a strict limit.

I’d probably review the method mainly in terms of SLAP: if parts of it can be meaningfully extracted into well-named helper methods, that would improve readability. But if splitting it would only create artificial methods without making the code clearer, keeping it longer seems reasonable.

If the reason for the structure is non-obvious, a comment explaining why it is kept that way could help, but I don’t think a formal justification is required.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/250#issuecomment-5693647539" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**25 :fas-comment:**
</div>

<img width="430" height="600" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/d735fe29-5b55-40fb-a17d-9e94ee644680" />
<img width="430" height="600" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/58fcc0b2-e4b9-4816-8718-adfbfdf04b16" />
Tested `Atom.jar` from release `v0.2` on Ubuntu 22.04.5 x86_64 with Zulu OpenJDK 25.0.4.1.

The main features worked when I used the date format requested by the app: adding todo/deadline/event tasks, `list`, `schedule`, `mark`, `unmark`, `find`, `delete`, and persistence after restarting.

I found three reproducible issues:

1. The date formats in the User Guide do not work. The guide documents `dd-MM-yyyy HHmm` for deadlines/events and `dd-MM-yyyy` for `schedule`, but Atom rejects those formats and asks for `yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm` or `yyyy-MM-dd`. For example, the documented-style commands `deadline submit report /by 20-09-2026 2359`, `event project meeting /from 21-09-2026 1400 /to 21-09-2026 1600`, and `schedule 21-09-2026` are all rejected. The equivalent ISO-style commands work.
2. An impossible calendar date is accepted and persisted. Running `deadline impossible /by 2026-09-31 12:00` successfully adds the deadline. Atom displays it as September 30, 2026, but `data/atom.txt` stores `2026-09-31 12:00`, and the task remains after restarting.
3. Closing Atom's only window does not terminate the application process. The window disappears, but the Java process remained active with no visible Atom window until it was terminated manually.
</panel>

</panel>


