<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/103#issuecomment-5463482827" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think this does result in a cyclic dependency between class 1 and 2. Since class 2 has an association with class 1, class 2 has a natural dependency on class 1 (By maintaining reference of class 1 in the field). The dashed arrow is to supplement the fact that class 1 also has a dependency on class 2 (The example is meant to show an association from class 2 to class 1 doesn't imply dependency from class 1 to class 2). Hence, both classes depend on each other.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/154#issuecomment-5551484602" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

For me, I consider if the extra abstraction is actually makes future changes easier. If adding or changing a features forces you to edit code in many different places, it means the design could be split up better. But if adding another class/interface gives you more files to jump between without making anything easier to change, then it is unnecessary complexity.

For the iP, I don't think the goal should be making everything as extensible as possible. Keep is simple and structure it such that each part got their own distinct roles such that when a change is needed, it is mostly contained to one or two parts instead of the whole program. 
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/122#issuecomment-5552494647" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**5 :fas-comment:**
</div>

<img width="512" height="668" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/0e7ce0eb-687f-41bb-a359-9724f7dc1b63" />

My GUI design is beyond JavaFX part 4 and added a lot of ideas from part 5 like custom CSS, styled chat bubbles, avatars and background image. I used AI to make to fit the theme I have in mind. AI is useful in implementation, but I need to specify all the design directions.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/181#issuecomment-5652129417" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**6 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I agree that it depends on what scenario the sequence diagram is meant to represent. I think the important part is not really “intended vs actual” by itself, but that the diagram should be consistent with its stated purpose. If I am using the diagram to investigate a failing execution, I would show the actual observed order instead. In debugging, having both seems most useful because the point where the two diagrams first diverge gives a concrete place to investigate. 
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/177#issuecomment-5652158427" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**7 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think a useful distinction is that SLAP is about the conceptual level of the code, not whether a line happens to be inside another method. For example, moreThanFiveLateDeliveries(driver) does not necessarily abstract much because its name almost directly restates driver.numberOfLateDeliveries > 5. The reader still understands essentially the same low-level rule either way, so introducing another method may just add an extra jump.

On the other hand, if that rule represents a meaningful domain concept, then extracting it can make more sense because the caller now only needs to know what decision is being made, while the helper hides how something like a high risk driver is determined.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/166#issuecomment-5652175414" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**8 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think another “cost” of refactoring is the extra cognitive overhead introduced by indirection. Extracting code into another method may shorten the original method, but if the helper has a vague name or is only called once, the reader may now need to jump between two methods (potentially located in different files since utils file to hold helper functions isn't uncommon in some projects) to understand something that was previously obvious.

I would not use method length alone to decide whether to extract. I would ask whether the refactor actually improves one of these things: readability, duplication, testability, responsibility separation, or ease of future modification.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/163#issuecomment-5652229689" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**9 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I also ended up preferring CSS for most of my GUI styling. One thing I found useful is that even if the UI needs to change dynamically, it does not necessarily mean the actual styling has to move into Java. I think a useful separation is that Java decides when the UI state changes while CSS decides how that state looks. For a fixed chat background I would therefore still use CSS, and only move image handling into Java if I actually needed behaviour that CSS could not express cleanly.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/194#issuecomment-5652531836" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**10 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I definitely felt that it was a slog to go through W6.3 this week as compared to previous weeks where generally I can see the ideas being taught and what is written generally value-add to the learning experience. The TRAFFIC section feels overly long for the points each step of the process want to cover. I found myself losing track of what the section is covering because I am getting lost in the words.  I also feel trying to cover something broadly may be counterproductive in helping people understand it due to information overload; it might be better for the person to find out themselves when they encounter something specific. 

I do wish that future weeks could be less verbose so its easier to understand the main points being covered.
</panel>

