<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/177#issuecomment-5623424924" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**9 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Adding on to what @Gnanes99 said, I think it also has to do with the naming of the helper.

moreThanFiveLateDeliveries literally says what and how it's going to do, I might as well just read the one line of code, so I guess it doesnt make sense to abstract it.

But would it be better to have the helper be something like isLateDriver(driver)? the implementation can look something like

```
function getRating(driver) ``{``
  return isLateDriver(driver) ? 2 : 1;
``}``

function isLateDriver(driver) ``{``
  return driver.numberOfLateDeliveries > LATE_DRIVER_THRESHOLD;
``}``
```
I suppose this way we can also avoid a magic literal, while at the same time (I think) providing meaning to the abstraction.

But I suppose we could also still inline it and have the following:
```
function getRating(driver) ``{``
  return driver.numberOfLateDeliveries > LATE_DRIVER_THRESHOLD ? 2 : 1;
``}``
```


Im not sure which is truly better here, but the former seems better to me as it gives us the option of naming the rule, that is the driver being late frequently. I think it is in cases like the shown example where the mehtod is named exactly same as HOW it carries out the logic, like moreThanFiveLateDeliveries, that it may be better inlining it.
</panel>

