<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/204#issuecomment-5662609640" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**12 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Hi there, I looked through some of the commits, and a lot of the earlier commits seem to be violating the git standards quite heavily, small but important things like capitalisation, imperative tone, usage of fullstop, and more.

Your recent commits seem to be better, but I think the main reason for not getting the tag is because the bodies that you have added recently are not exactly following the standard either.

One commit that really stood out was [0334577](https://github.com/aahilma/ip/commit/033457754c7d84130eb4b519cccd2519f3e0d7b0), which has a super long commit message (looks longer than the body lol).

The [git convetions](https://se-education.org/guides/conventions/git.html) are quite strict I suppose, and looking back it makes sense why these strict conventions are to be used as they make reading through the commits a lot easier and consistent. A dev wouldn't have to spend time trying to decipher your intention or understand HOW code works (if how was used) instead of WHAT and HOW.

tldr I think you just need to maybe clean your recent commits and perhaps push 5 new commits that strictly follow the conventions with body as well. You can ask AI to refer to the git conventions and explain to you the mistakes you made in your commits as well if you want to find your mistakes more precisely!

<img width="1305" height="595" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/4337c7a4-15af-42b6-85c8-57fd75858854" />
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/222#issuecomment-5696290821" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**13 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Hello, I found two bugs!

Bug 1: The year 0000 is displayed as year 0001.
Bug 2: I have added a task that matches one that is already in my list, although the user guide claims Kiwi does not.

**Bug 1: Wrong display of year**
<img width="381" height="210" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/91173c4c-1756-41ec-8956-2b9cd0fabfe3" />

**Steps to reproduce:**
1. Launch Kiwi.
2. Enter `deadline dl /by 0000-01-01`

**Expected:** The deadline is shown as `(by: Jan 01 0000)`, or the date is rejected.

**Actual:** The deadline is shown as `(by: Jan 01 0001)`, which is a different date from the one entered.

**Bug 2: Duplicate tasks added**

The user guide states:
> **Adding a deadline:** "Adds a task that is due on a date."
> **Adding a to-do:** "Kiwi will not add a task that already matches one in your list (same type, description, and dates)."

<img width="1021" height="629" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/40eb5c00-b7ed-40fb-91ed-e0cecd870690" />

However, by using the year 0000, I was able to create a task that matches an already existing task in my list.

**Steps to reproduce:**
1. Launch Kiwi.
2. Enter `deadline dl /by 0000-01-01`
3. Enter `deadline dl /by 0001-01-01`
4. Enter `list`

**Expected:** Step 3 is rejected with `That task is already in your list.`, since the task looks identical to the one added in step 2.

**Actual:** Both tasks are added. `list` shows two identical entries:

<img width="377" height="454" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/4584cf76-73e0-476d-b890-d07ea0e4f204" />

This seems to be caused by Bug 1: the two dates are different internally, but both are displayed as `Jan 01 0001`, so the list shows a duplicate that the user guide says cannot exist.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/222#issuecomment-5696551766" expanded>
