<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/166#issuecomment-5634352781" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**11 :fas-comment:**
</div>

From my understanding, the “cost” does not only refer to performance. It can also refer to the time needed to refactor and test the code, the risk of introducing bugs, and the extra effort needed to navigate between different methods. The benefits would include better readability, less duplicated code, and making the code easier to modify in the future.

I do not think Extract Function and Inline Function contradict each other. For the first example, `printDetails()` gives that block of code a clear purpose and allows `printOwing()` to be read at a higher level. However, in the second example, `moreThanFiveLateDeliveries()` does not explain much more than the condition itself. maybe  keeping it as a separate function may just make the reader jump elsewhere to understand a simple line.

I guess there is no exact point where the cost outweighs the benefit. A practical way to decide is to compare both versions and ask which one would be easier for another developer to understand and modify. If extracting a function gives the code a meaningful name, removes duplication, or isolates logic that may change, it is probably useful. If it only creates another very small function without adding clarity, then inlining may be better.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/148#issuecomment-5634495690" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**12 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Hi, I think I found the cause. It seems to be a visual bug in SceneBuilder 26.0.0 where both columns get stuck at their minimum widths.

with SceneBuilder from source, open:

'''text
kit/src/main/java/com/oracle/javafx/scenebuilder/kit/editor/panel/info/InfoPanelController.java
'''

Under `performInitialization()`, add this after the lines that initialise `leftTableColumn` and `rightTableColumn`:

'''java
final TableView&lt;IndexEntry> tableView = leftTableColumn.getTableView();
leftTableColumn.prefWidthProperty()
        .bind(tableView.widthProperty().multiply(0.35));
rightTableColumn.prefWidthProperty()
        .bind(tableView.widthProperty().multiply(0.65).subtract(2));
'''

Then rebuild and launch it using:

'''bash
./mvnw clean install -pl app -am -DskipTests
./mvnw javafx:run -f app
'''

The full `fx:id` should then be visible, and you should also be able to resize the columns manually.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/207#issuecomment-5674809681" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**13 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Yep, agree with the rest! I think the participants chosen can also indicate the intended level of detail. If the lifelines represent broad components such as `Logic`, `Model`, and `Storage`, I would mainly show calls that cross those boundaries. If they represent specific parser or command objects, more internal calls may be relevant.

But I also think thatt mixing both levels could be confusing. For example, showing every parser helper method while representing the entire Model interaction with one call may give a misleading sense of importance. So besides asking whether a call contributes to the outcome, we could also ask whether it matches the diagram’s overall level of abstraction.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/211#issuecomment-5674829748" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**14 :fas-comment:**
</div>

One more angle is to consider the impact across versions, not just across components. For example, if `Model`’s representation is reflected in saved user data, changing it may require data migration or backwards compatibility handling in `Storage`, even if the interfaces used by `Logic` and `Ui` remain unchanged. I would consider that architecturally significant because the system has made a long term commitment to that representation.

A useful test could be whether the decision can be changed through a local refactoring without coordinating with other components or migrating existing data. If it can, it is probably detailed design. If it cannot, it is more likely to be an architectural decision.

</panel>

