### 18. ANAN..ANDA `@ananyakharbanda` (23 posts) <span class=text-warning>:octicon-eye:</span>
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/554" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1. :fas-info-circle: How should we handle a feature that needs to change a shared interface?**
</div>

Suppose two people are working on separate features and both depend on `Model`.

Feature A needs to add:

'''java
void addRemark(Person person, Remark remark);
'''

while Feature B also needs to change the `Model` interface for something unrelated.

If both branches modify `Model.java` and `ModelManager.java`, there is a pretty high chance of conflicts even though the actual features are independent.

Would it be better to make a small PR containing just the shared interface change first, merge that into `master`, and then have both feature branches build on top of it?

Or is that discouraged because the interface change by itself doesn't provide any user-visible functionality?

Im wondering how we should balance incremental integration with avoiding PRs that only contain plumbing needed by future features.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/531" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2. :fas-info-circle: How do you debug a value that becomes wrong long before the program fails?**
</div>

Suppose I know that a field eventually has the wrong value, but I don't know which method changed it.

For example:

'''java
taskList.size()
'''

should be `5`, but by the time the UI displays it, it has become `4`.

Putting a breakpoint where the wrong value is displayed tells me that it is already incorrect, but not where it first changed.

Would the usual approach be to use something like a watchpoint/data breakpoint on the field and stop whenever it is modified, rather than adding logging to every method that could change it?

And if the bug only happens occasionally and I can't reproduce it reliably in the debugger, would logging those state changes then be the better option?

I'm trying to understand how to choose between debugger features and permanent logging when the main problem is finding the first point where the program state becomes incorrect.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/530" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3. :fas-info-circle: Should assertions be placed where an invalid state is created or where it is first used?**
</div>

From the debugging topic, I understand that a defect can create an incorrect program state, and the actual failure might only happen much later.

Say a method accidentally creates an invalid object:

'''java
Task task = new Task("");
'''

but nothing crashes there. The task gets passed around for a while and the failure only happens later when some other component tries to display or save it.

If I want to use assertions to catch this earlier, where would be the better place to put them?

One option is to assert inside `Task` itself that its state is always valid:

'''java
assert !description.isBlank();
'''

Another is to assert at component boundaries whenever another component receives a `Task` and assumes that it is valid.

The first seems better because it catches the bad state closest to where it is created, but the second also documents the assumptions between components.

Would it make sense to do both in some cases, or would that just be duplicating the same invariant everywhere?

I'm mainly wondering how we decide where an assertion gives the most useful debugging information without filling the code with repeated checks.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/286" expanded>
<div slot="header">

