<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/266" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**5. :fas-info-circle: What should happen if only part of the saved data is corrupted?**
</div>

I was thinking about how we should handle corrupted storage files.

Say the file has 20 tasks, but one line in the middle is invalid because the user edited the file manually or something went wrong with an older version.

Would it be better to:

- stop loading completely because the file is no longer valid, or
- skip that one bad line and still load the other 19 tasks?

Skipping the bad line seems more user-friendly, but at the same time silently ignoring data feels a bit dangerous because the user might not realise a task was lost.

Would a reasonable approach be to load whatever is still valid, but also show/log a warning that some entries couldn't be loaded?

Just wondering what the usual tradeoff is here between recovering as much data as possible vs being strict about data integrity.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/262" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**6. :fas-info-circle: When is extracting command handling actually better than a long if-else chain?**
</div>

I got the automated code quality feedback that my `getResponse()` method is quite long, and most of the length comes from dispatching different commands.

It's roughly something like:

'''java
if (command.equals("list")) ``{``
    ...
``}`` else if (command.startsWith("find ")) ``{``
    ...
``}`` else if (command.startsWith("mark ")) ``{``
    ...
``}`` else if (command.startsWith("delete ")) ``{``
    ...
``}``
'''

I can shorten the method by extracting each branch into another method, but the actual command selection still has to happen somewhere.

Another option would be to have something like a `Command` abstraction and let the parser return the correct command object, but for a small application that feels like quite a lot more structure just to remove an if-else chain.

So when applying SLAP/refactoring, is a long dispatch method necessarily something we should try to remove, or can the dispatch itself reasonably stay in one place as long as the actual command logic is separated out?

I'm mainly wondering where the line is between improving abstraction and just moving complexity around.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/219" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**7. :fas-info-circle: How many error cases should we put in one sequence diagram?**
</div>

When drawing a sequence diagram for something like an `add` command, there can be quite a few different paths.

For example:

* invalid command format
* invalid field
* duplicate entry
* normal successful add

I know we can use `alt` fragments for different cases, but if we show every possible error the diagram can get quite messy.

Would it be better to keep the main sequence diagram focused on the normal flow and only include important alternative paths, or are we expected to represent most of the possible error cases as well?

At what point would it make more sense to use a separate sequence diagram for an error scenario?

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/218" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**8. :fas-info-circle: Should we log normal user input errors?**
</div>

For logging, I'm a bit confused about what is actually worth logging.

Say the user enters something invalid like

add n/John p/abc

and the program already catches it and shows an error message because the phone number is invalid.

Would we still log this? I feel like using WARNING for every invalid command would make the logs pretty noisy, since this is expected user behaviour rather than something going wrong internally.

Would it make more sense to only log these at something like FINE, and reserve WARNING / SEVERE for unexpected things like file errors or states that shouldn't normally happen?

Basically, how do we decide between an error that should just be shown to the user vs something that should also go into the logs?
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/154" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**9. :fas-info-circle: Is a highly extensible design always better for the iP?**
</div>

While implementing the iP, I've noticed that we can often make a design much more extensible by introducing additional abstractions/classes/interfaces.

However, for a relatively small application, these abstractions can also increase complexity without providing an immediate benefit.

When evaluating code quality, how should we balance extensibility against simplicity?

For example, is it generally better to design specifically for the requirements we currently have, and refactor when new requirements appear, rather than designing upfront for hypothetical future features?
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/153" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**10. :fas-info-circle: How much application logic should be inside JavaFX GUI classes?**
</div>

After adding JavaFX for Level-10, I noticed that it can be quite tempting to put more logic directly inside event handlers or controller classes.

For example, when the user enters a command, the GUI could either:

- parse and process the command itself, or
- simply pass the input to the same application logic that was previously used by the text UI and display the returned result.

The second approach seems cleaner because it keeps the GUI mostly responsible for presentation, and it would also make the underlying logic easier to test without JavaFX.

Is this generally the preferred design?

More broadly, where should we draw the boundary between UI logic and application/domain logic in a small application such as the iP?
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/110" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**11. :fas-info-circle: Creating a separate folder for explanations**
</div>

Are we allowed to create folders for our personal explanations, ie reasonings for certain style choice, it seems helpful to refer back to after a while.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/139#issuecomment-5550370171" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**12 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think another consideration is that iP seems to serve as preparation for the tP, rather than only being a standalone greenfield project.

Even if the same functional requirements can be implemented in Rust/C++/Go, the learning experience may be quite different. For example, concepts such as inheritance, interfaces, exceptions, dependency management, testing frameworks and even what constitutes "OOP" do not map one-to-one between languages. Rust in particular would encourage fairly different design patterns from Java.

So if everyone eventually has to work on the tP in Java, having the iP in Java also gives us a smaller environment to practise the same tooling and design decisions before entering a much larger codebase.

Maybe a compromise could be to give more freedom over the architecture/UI/features of the iP while keeping Java as the language constraint. That still leaves a lot of greenfield decision-making without losing its role as preparation for the tP.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/152#issuecomment-5560071562" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**13 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think another useful distinction is whether naming the literal actually adds semantic meaning.

For example, replacing `1` with `ONE` probably doesn't make the code much clearer, because `ONE` tells us exactly the same thing as the literal. But something like `MAX_RETRIES = 3` or `DEFAULT_WINDOW_WIDTH = 480` explains *why* that value exists.

So I would probably name a literal when the name communicates some domain meaning, constraint, or relationship that the raw value itself does not. For universally understood values such as `0` when initializing a loop counter, introducing a constant might actually add more indirection without improving readability.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/157#issuecomment-5560077557" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**14 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think part of the readability problem here is also specific to Java not having named arguments.

If the call site could look something like `new Task("read book", isDone: false)`, the boolean would be much less ambiguous. But with Java we only see `new Task("read book", false)`, so the reader has to know the parameter order.

This makes me think boolean parameters are probably fine when the surrounding method name already makes their meaning obvious, but become more problematic in constructors or methods with multiple parameters. If we ever ended up with something like `new Task("read book", false, true, false)`, that would be a much stronger sign to redesign the API.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/154#issuecomment-5644166330" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**15 :fas-comment:**
</div>

A technical way to explore this in the iP could be to compare two implementations of command handling. In the simpler design, the parser returns a `CommandType` enum and the main controller uses a `switch` statement to execute the corresponding logic. In a more extensible design, the parser acts as a factory that returns a polymorphic `Command` object, with concrete classes such as `AddCommand`, `DeleteCommand` and `FindCommand`, each implementing an `execute(TaskList, Ui, Storage)` method.

The second design follows the Open–Closed Principle more closely because adding a command mainly involves introducing a new subclass instead of modifying a large execution `switch`. It can also improve cohesion by keeping parsing and execution logic for each command together, while enabling individual commands to be unit-tested independently. However, it introduces additional indirection, object creation and dependencies between the command layer and components such as `TaskList`, `Ui` and `Storage`. If every command requires all three dependencies, the `execute` interface may also become too broad, suggesting unnecessary coupling.

I think this is where experimenting beyond KISS and YAGNI becomes useful. We can compare the designs based on change amplification, cyclomatic complexity, coupling, cohesion and testability. If adding a new command requires fewer edits to existing classes and fewer unrelated tests to change, the abstraction is providing a genuine extension point. If it only distributes a small `switch` across many almost-empty classes, it is likely over-engineering. This makes the boundary between useful abstraction and speculative generality something we can observe through the code rather than decide purely in advance.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/168#issuecomment-5644256685" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**16 :fas-comment:**
</div>

One AI workflow I have experimented with in some of my personal projects, although not yet for the iP, is a **context-aware PR review agent** that reviews the semantic impact of a change instead of sending the entire repository to an LLM.

A possible implementation pipeline is:

'''text
git diff
   ↓
identify changed lines
   ↓
parse changed files into an AST
   ↓
find the enclosing classes and methods
   ↓
retrieve directly related methods, interfaces and tests
   ↓
send this context to the LLM
   ↓
produce structured review findings
'''

For example, `git diff --unified=0 origin/master...HEAD` gives the exact changed line ranges. A parser such as JavaParser or Tree-sitter can then map each changed line to its enclosing method or class. Instead of passing every file to the model, the program constructs a smaller dependency graph containing:

* the changed method
* interfaces or parent classes it implements
* methods directly called by it
* classes instantiated by it
* tests that reference the changed class

The model can then be required to return structured findings:

'''json
``{``
  "findings": [
    ``{``
      "file": "src/main/java/Parser.java",
      "line": 42,
      "severity": "warning",
      "category": "correctness",
      "claim": "This branch accepts an empty argument.",
      "evidence": "substring(...).trim() can produce an empty string.",
      "suggested_test": "parse_emptyArgument_throwsException"
    ``}``
  ]
``}``
'''

Requiring a file, line number and evidence helps filter out vague comments such as “this code could be improved.” The pipeline can reject findings that reference nonexistent lines or files outside the diff. Deterministic tools such as the compiler, Checkstyle and JUnit should also run separately, with their output supplied to the model as evidence rather than asking it to guess whether the code builds.

The most interesting problem is context selection. Too little context leads to incorrect reviews, while sending the whole repository increases cost and introduces irrelevant information. Using an AST and dependency graph makes context selection a software-engineering problem rather than simply relying on a larger context window.

I have not tried integrating this into my iP workflow yet, but experimenting with similar ideas in personal projects made me realise that AI code review becomes much more reliable when it is combined with traditional program analysis and structured, verifiable outputs.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/219#issuecomment-5683930673" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**17 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Thank you everyone, much clearer now!
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/252#issuecomment-5693339136" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**18 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think another thing worth checking is whether the length is coming from one responsibility or several different responsibilities.

For example, a 40-line method that is one fairly linear algorithm may still be easy to follow, while a 25-line method that parses input, validates it, modifies state and formats output is arguably doing too much.

So I wouldn't use line count itself as the main signal. I'd look at whether you can describe the method with one clear responsibility, and whether extracting something gives you a meaningful abstraction rather than just making the original method shorter.
</panel>

