This is pretty useful. One thing I'd probably keep in mind though is that the generated graph is showing code/package dependencies, which isn't necessarily the same thing as the intended architecture.

I think it could be especially useful for spotting unexpected coupling though. If two parts of the repo that are supposed to be fairly independent suddenly have a lot of connections in the graph, that gives you a good place to inspect the imports/dependencies more closely.

So maybe good for "what currently depends on what", then compare that against the DG/architecture diagram to see if the implementation is actually following the intended design.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/540#issuecomment-5778929885" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**21 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think familiarity with a term and the need to define it are slightly different things.

Even if the reader already knows what something like `API` or `JSON` means generally, it could still be worth putting in the glossary if the term has a specific meaning within our system.

For example, `API` could refer to any interface between software components, but in our DG we might use it specifically to mean an external service that the application depends on. In that case, defining it helps fix the meaning we intend rather than teaching the reader what an API is.

So I wouldn't include common technical terms by default. I'd include them when:

* the term is ambiguous or overloaded,
* the project gives it a narrower/special meaning, or
* misunderstanding it would affect how someone reads the requirements or design.

That seems more useful than deciding purely based on whether the target audience is technical.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/531#issuecomment-5813297783" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**22 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Thanks everyone, this clears it up.

My main takeaway is that if the bug is reproducible, it makes more sense to use the debugger and narrow down the mutation point, then inspect the call stack there. For a mutable object like `taskList`, a watchpoint on the field itself may not catch changes such as `remove()` or `clear()`, since the reference hasn't changed.

If the bug is intermittent, targeted logging around the actual mutation points, like the operation plus the before/after state, seems more useful than logging every read.

The idea of first narrowing down *when* the state becomes wrong, then choosing the tool based on reproducibility, was the part I was missing. Thanks!

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/530#issuecomment-5813312018" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**23 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Thanks everyone, this makes the distinction much clearer.

What I was mixing up was validation vs assertions. If an invalid value can come from normal user input or stored data, that should be handled with proper validation rather than relying on assertions.

For internal invariants, it seems better to check them close to where the state can actually become invalid, since that makes the failure easier to trace. Then an assertion at a component boundary is more useful when that boundary introduces a new assumption, rather than just repeating the exact same invariant everywhere.

So I think the main takeaway for me is that assertions should document/check assumptions that are supposed to already hold, while validation handles inputs that can legitimately be invalid.

Thanks for the explanations!
</panel>

</panel>


