<panel type="info"  collapsed>
<div slot="header">

### 24. FANG.. XIN `@pinxinfang` (20 posts) 
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/73" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1. :fas-info-circle: Handling old saved dates after Level 8**
</div>

Before Level 8, my chatbot stored deadline dates as strings, so values such as `next Friday` were accepted. After changing them to `LocalDate`, those existing tasks can no longer be loaded.

Should we maintain compatibility with data saved in earlier levels, or is it acceptable to treat the old data as invalid? How are others handling this after the Level 8 refactor?
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/69#issuecomment-5434170189" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think command classes are useful, but reflection may be overkill here. A short `switch` or a `Map&lt;String, CommandFactory>` can handle parsing, while each `Command` class contains its own execution logic.

The map may still be long, but it is just a list of mappings rather than complex control flow. `Class.forName()` also introduces runtime errors if a class is renamed or has the wrong constructor. For an iP with a fixed set of commands, an explicit switch or map seems simpler and easier to test.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/96#issuecomment-5461525139" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I took a look at the error file and it seems like an architecture mismatch. The JavaFX files are built for `x86_64`, but your Java is trying to run them on `arm64`.

Could you run `uname -m` and `java -XshowSettings:properties -version 2>&1 | grep os.arch`? If they show ARM64/aarch64, you may need an ARM-compatible version of the JAR, or use an x86_64 JDK through Rosetta. Installing another ARM JDK probably won’t fix the mismatch.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/135#issuecomment-5519709616" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

  I think the first suggestion is probably the simplest. You can make MSG_HELP private and provide a public getter:
  
  private static final String MSG_HELP = ...;
  
  public static String getHelpMessage() ``{``
      return MSG_HELP;
  ``}``
  
This keeps the constants encapsulated while avoiding the Checkstyle declaration-order issue. Since MSG_HELP is already static final, it is only initialized once, so I don’t think an extra cache is needed.

If Message.java eventually becomes too large, it might also be worth splitting the constants into smaller classes based on their purpose, such as command syntax and error messages.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/164#issuecomment-5642747888" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**5 :fas-comment:**
</div>

For the iPad jump, it may help to record the browser (for example Safari), iPadOS version, orientation, and viewport width, then check whether the same jump happens in a private window. Responsive layouts can recalculate the focused row's height when the details panel opens, so a fixed height, delayed scroll target, or an element being inserted above the current anchor could cause this.

For the overlapping red label, the screenshot suggests the focused-view container may not reserve enough vertical space for the details section. A responsive layout rule such as allowing the label and details panel to wrap, or adding a small breakpoint-specific margin, might avoid the overlap without changing the desktop layout.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/229#issuecomment-5711496325" expanded>
