<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/501#issuecomment-5738825317" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**10 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Tested **Ruby-V0.2** on **macOS 15.7.3 (Intel), Azul OpenJDK 25.0.3**. These were AI-assisted tests against the released JAR: a normal `java -jar ruby.jar` launch, console checks, and an automated GUI driver exercising the release's Enter/Send handlers.

The main flows worked:

- The JAR launched. The greeting, avatars, text and chat bubbles rendered correctly at the default size. The input and Send button remained usable at the tested 300px and 800px window widths; the header text became truncated at 300px.
- Adding todo/deadline/event tasks, `list`, case-insensitive `find BOOK`, `mark`, `unmark`, and deletion worked. Contact add/list/delete also worked, including supplying optional fields in a different order.
- Duplicate tasks/contacts, `deadline Homework /by 2026-02-30`, `mark 99`, an event ending before it starts, an invalid phone number, and `nonsense` were rejected without closing the window.
- Tasks and contacts survived reloads, including checking `list` and `contact list` in a fresh Java process. Normal lowercase `bye` closed the GUI window.

**One reproducible inconsistency: uppercase `BYE` closes the window even though it is rejected.**

1. Launch Ruby.
2. Enter `BYE` and press Enter.

Expected, following the User Guide's lowercase-command rule: show an invalid-command error and keep the window open.

Actual: Ruby produces `Hold on — That's not in my repertoire. Try a command I know.`, but the window closes immediately, so the user cannot read the error.

The GUI driver recorded the error response followed by `WINDOW_SHOWING=false`. In the release source, [`Parser.parse`](https://github.com/Rubyherp/ip/blob/Ruby-V0.2/src/main/java/ruby/command/Parser.java) accepts only exact lowercase `bye`, whereas [`MainWindow.isExitCommand`](https://github.com/Rubyherp/ip/blob/Ruby-V0.2/src/main/java/ruby/MainWindow.java) uses `trim().equalsIgnoreCase("bye")`. Making the GUI exit condition agree with the parser would fix this; ideally, close only after a successfully parsed exit command.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/498#issuecomment-5739066134" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**11 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Tested **Pip A-Release** on **macOS 15.7.3, Intel, Azul OpenJDK 25.0.3**, from a separate test folder. These were AI-assisted automated GUI tests against the released `pip.jar`, plus a console check of storage recovery.

The flows I checked passed:

- The GUI launched, and both Enter and Send processed commands. Todo, deadline, event, list, find, mark, unmark and delete worked.
- `find REPORT` matched `submit report` without changing its full-list index (`2`).
- `todo BUY   MILK` was rejected as a duplicate of `todo buy milk`. An impossible date (`2026-02-30`), an event ending before its start, `mark 0`, `mark 999`, `todo` without a description, and `list extra` all produced errors.
- `bye` displayed the farewell and disabled input, as documented. After closing and launching a fresh Java process from the same folder, all three tasks were restored, and `undo` correctly reported `No steps to retrace yet.`

I specifically checked that read-only/failed commands don't consume undo:

'''text
delete 2
list
find meeting
mark 999
undo
list
'''

The deleted deadline was restored at position 2, between the original todo and event. Undoing a successful `mark 2` also restored its uncompleted state.

For recovery, in a separate disposable folder I started with:

'''text
T | 0 | keep first
BROKEN LINE
T | 1 | keep second
'''

Pip reported **line 2**, loaded both valid tasks with their completion states intact, and created a `.bak` file that matched the original file byte-for-byte. Adding `todo after recovery` then saved all three valid tasks successfully. I didn't find a functional failure in these checks.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/502#issuecomment-5739066234" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**12 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Tested **Hu9o A-Release** on **macOS 15.7.3, Intel, Azul OpenJDK 25.0.3**, using AI-assisted automated GUI tests against the released `hu9o.jar` in a separate folder.

The requested core flow worked: the GUI opened, Enter and Send both worked, the dog typing indicator appeared, and the supplied todo/deadline/event commands succeeded. `list`, `mark`, `unmark`, `find BOOK`, and `delete` worked. `progress` showed **1/3 (33%)** after marking one task, then **0/3 (0%)** after unmarking it. All four supplied invalid inputs produced red error bubbles. Exiting with `bye` saved and closed the app; the remaining tasks reappeared in a fresh Java process.

I found two date-input problems:

**1. The documented hour-and-minute form is rejected.**

'''text
deadline minute test /by 20/9/26 630 PM
'''

Expected: add a deadline at 6:30 PM, using the guide's `h[mm] a` format.

Actual:

'''text
Invalid Date...Use day/month/year 12 hr time
    i.e 12/8/26 330 pm
'''

The whole-hour example `20/9/26 6 PM` worked in the same session.

**2. An impossible date is silently changed and saved.**

'''text
deadline impossible /by 31/2/26 6 PM
'''

Expected: reject the nonexistent date.

Actual: a task was added as `[D][ ] impossible (by: 28 Feb, 6 PM)`. It was still February 28 after `bye` and relaunch.

A possible fix is in [`DateTime.INPUT_FORMAT`](https://github.com/vishnuroxx/ip/blob/A-Release/src/main/java/hu9o/task/DateTime.java): try separate `d/M/uu h a` and `d/M/uu hmm a` formatters, keeping case-insensitive AM/PM, and apply `ResolverStyle.STRICT` to both. I checked those two formats separately on Java 25: they accepted `6 PM`, `630 PM`, `330 pm` and `1230 AM`, rejected February 31 and February 29 in 2026, and accepted February 29 in 2028. The [`ResolverStyle` documentation](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/time/format/ResolverStyle.html) explains why SMART resolution can adjust an invalid day to the month's last valid day.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/504#issuecomment-5740670500" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**13 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Tested **Amadeus v1.0** on **macOS 15.7.3, Intel x86_64, Azul OpenJDK 25.0.3**, using AI-assisted automated GUI checks against the released JAR in a separate folder.

The GUI opened, and both Enter and Send worked. `todo`, `deadline`, `event`, `list`, `mark`, `unmark`, `delete`, and case-insensitive `find REPORT` worked. Missing descriptions, an out-of-range task number, an unknown command, and February 30 were rejected without crashing. The ordinary tasks and `#study` tag survived a fresh-process restart.

For your specific date-search check: `find 2026-09-20` returned no matches, while `on 2026-09-20` found the deadline. This matches the User Guide's description-only rule for `find`.

**One persistence issue: a pipe in a task description becomes a tag after restarting.**

1. Enter `todo compare A | B`.
2. Run `list`: the task is displayed as `[T][ ] compare A | B`.
3. Enter `bye`, relaunch the JAR from the same folder, then run `list`.
4. It now displays `[T][ ] compare A [B]`.

Expected: preserve the accepted description, or reject unsupported characters before adding the task.

The saved line is `T | 0 | compare A | B`. In [`Storage`](https://github.com/Polar1605/ip/blob/v1.0/src/main/java/amadeus/storage/Storage.java), the extra field is interpreted as the optional tags field. Rejecting the storage delimiter in user input is a small fix; escaping it in both encoding and decoding would preserve support for literal pipes. A save/load round-trip test for this input would catch the regression.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/503#issuecomment-5740670615" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**14 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Tested **Murphy v0.3** on **macOS 15.7.3, Intel x86_64, Azul OpenJDK 25.0.3**, using AI-assisted automated GUI checks against the released JAR outside the repository.

The GUI opened with the requested title, both Frenchie avatars, and the sidebar cheat sheet. Enter and Send worked. The supplied todo, deadline, event, list, find, mark, and delete examples worked; unmark also worked. The malformed event, February 30, an invalid task number, an unknown command, and a duplicate todo all produced errors without crashing. After closing the window and launching a fresh Java process, the remaining deadline and event were restored.

**`bye` does not exit the GUI.**

1. Launch the JAR and enter `bye`.
2. Murphy displays its farewell, but the window stays open.
3. Enter `list`: it still responds normally.

The GUI check recorded `WINDOW_SHOWING=true` after the farewell. [`MurphyService.respond`](https://github.com/aanya007/ip/blob/v0.3/src/main/java/murphy/MurphyService.java) returns a goodbye string, but the submit handler in [`MurphyApplication.createInputArea`](https://github.com/aanya007/ip/blob/v0.3/src/main/java/murphy/MurphyApplication.java) never closes the stage or calls `Platform.exit()`. Returning an exit flag from the command layer and handling it in the GUI would let both interfaces agree on successful exit.

One resize observation: at the minimum window size of 760×520, several command buttons are reduced to `...` and the sidebar cheat sheet is clipped. The input and Send button remain usable. A scrollable sidebar and a wrapping command-button layout could help at that size.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/505#issuecomment-5740670721" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**15 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Tested **Carlo A-Release** on **macOS 15.7.3, Intel x86_64, Azul OpenJDK 25.0.3**, using AI-assisted automated checks of the actual GUI forms in the released JAR.

The window, image, and three task columns rendered. Adding a todo, deadline, and event placed them in the correct columns. Mark done, Unmark, Delete, Find, Show date, and Show all worked in the tested flows. `next week` produced a readable error without crashing. Tasks were written to `data/carlo.txt` under the launch folder and restored after closing the window and launching a fresh Java process.

**One date-validation bug: February 30 is silently changed to February 28.**

1. Choose **Deadline**.
2. Enter description `bad date` and due date `2026-02-30`.
3. Click **Add deadline**.

Expected: reject the nonexistent date.

Actual: the app says the deadline was added and shows `bad date (by: Feb 28 2026)`. February 28 is also saved and restored on restart.

The input formatters in [`CarloDateTime`](https://github.com/winstonmulyadinus/ip/blob/A-Release/src/main/java/carlo/task/CarloDateTime.java) use the default SMART resolution. Java's [ResolverStyle documentation](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/time/format/ResolverStyle.html) explains that this can move an invalid day to the month's last valid day. Consider applying `ResolverStyle.STRICT` to all date/date-time input patterns and using `uuuu` for the year, for example:

'''java
DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("uuuu-MM-dd")
        .withResolverStyle(ResolverStyle.STRICT);
'''

A regression check should reject `2026-02-30` and non-leap-year `2026-02-29`, while still accepting `2028-02-29`.

</panel>

