<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/163#issuecomment-5653471837" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**6 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Late to this but on the "sometimes the image doesn't show" part with the Java approach, that's usually because the FXML controller extends `AnchorPane` while the actual root node loaded by `FXMLLoader` is a different `AnchorPane`, so the background ends up on a node that isn't in the scene graph. Sounds like that's what you hit given your fix was setting it on the root directly. Using `fx:root` in the FXML (like in the JavaFX tutorial part 4) also avoids that mismatch. Agree CSS is the cleaner choice for a static background though.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/177#issuecomment-5653536933" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**7 :fas-comment:**
</div>

One thing that helped me was realising Extract Function and Inline Function are literally opposites of each other, so they can't both be "rules" that apply at the same time. They're just the two directions you can move in depending on what the code currently looks like. So the question isn't really "SLAP or inline", it's "does this helper's name tell the caller something the body doesn't already say". `moreThanFiveLateDeliveries` fails that test because the name is just the body in English, so you inline it. Something like `isFrequentlyLate` passes because `getRating` no longer needs to care what the threshold is, so you'd keep it extracted. SLAP is satisfied either way as long as `getRating` reads at one level. It's the name that decides whether the extra method is earning its keep.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/206#issuecomment-5664889728" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**8 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Agree with @XieKaiwen  that an OS upgrade is a lot to ask just to get a JDK. For the alternative path, the guide could just reuse what the Mac guide already does, which is SDKMAN:

```
curl -s "https://get.sdkman.io" | bash
source "$HOME/.sdkman/bin/sdkman-init.sh"
sdk install java 25.0.3.fx-zulu
```

This pulls the JDK from the vendor directly so it doesn't matter which Ubuntu version (or distro) you're on. It also happens to be the Zulu FX build, so JavaFX comes bundled, which the plain `openjdk-25-jdk` apt package doesn't give you. Would make the Linux and Mac guides consistent too.
</panel>

