<panel type="info"  collapsed>
<div slot="header">

### 29. JOEL..HENG `@Antelyuu` (18 posts) 
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/207" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1. :fas-info-circle: How detailed should a sequence diagram be for an iP feature?**
</div>

From W6.1, I understand that sequence diagrams describe interactions for a specific scenario. When we start drawing them, how do we decide which calls to include? For an `add` command, should we show every Parser/Logic/Model/Storage call, or only the calls needed to explain the scenario and its outcome? I’m wondering whether helper methods should usually be omitted unless they affect the interaction being illustrated.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/139#issuecomment-5538649968" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think an alternative-language iP could work only if there is a clear equivalent for things like testing, linting, and CI. But that would be a lot to support and grade fairly. Keeping Java for the assessed iP, with an optional ungraded port later, seems like a reasonable middle ground.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/153#issuecomment-5557469044" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I found it helps to keep the controller dependent on one narrow method, e.g. `logic.getResponse(input)`, rather than on individual command classes. Then if you later change parsing or add commands, the UI code does not need to change. The only extra UI-side logic should be presentation choices, such as styling an error message differently.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/161#issuecomment-5570918516" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Until there is one, I think keeping a small checklist alongside `git log --oneline --decorate` is enough to spot missing commits or tags before the deadline. It does not replace the teaching team’s checks, but it makes it easier to verify that your fork has the expected history.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/171#issuecomment-5599487662" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**5 :fas-comment:**
</div>

There is also a small advantage during release testing: you can scan the remaining open issues and check that none were simply forgotten. For an iP, I’d create issues only for bugs with a useful regression test or that I cannot fix immediately; otherwise the tracking itself can get in the way.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/175#issuecomment-5614671121" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**6 :fas-comment:**
</div>

One thing worth checking is whether the behaviour is clear from your user guide. Whatever you choose for `list 67`, it should not surprise someone who only follows the documented command format. I would lean towards rejecting unused arguments with a short usage hint, since silently ignoring them can hide a typo.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/179#issuecomment-5631720361" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**7 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Since the requirement says “at least one”, I would keep the extra work unless the extension introduces bugs or conflicts with the project. You could mention the additional C-Extension items in your README or report so it is clear they were intentional, but the teaching team’s answer should take priority if you are worried about grading. I think as long as you have the tag it should be fine.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/181#issuecomment-5645874286" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**8 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think it helps to keep the two diagrams separate: one for the expected flow and one for the failing flow. For your example, the expected diagram would show `validate()` before `save()`, while the observed diagram shows the reverse. This makes the mismatch obvious without changing the diagram of the intended design.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/154#issuecomment-5651717653" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**9 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think an abstraction is most useful when a new feature starts duplicating the same logic in multiple places. If it only makes the current code look cleaner but introduces generic interfaces or extra classes for features that do not exist yet, I would probably defer it until there is a concrete need.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/166#issuecomment-5653198971" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**10 :fas-comment:**
</div>

For me, the main test is whether the new method makes the caller easier to understand. If I still need to jump into the helper to understand a one-line condition, I would probably leave it inline. If the helper represents a real concept or a group of steps, the extra method is usually worth it.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/207#issuecomment-5675525495" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**11 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Thanks everyone, this clears things up. I’ll focus on one scenario and include the interactions that explain the outcome instead of trying to show every method call. I also didn’t realise a `ref` frame could be useful for hiding details that are still important, so that was helpful.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/215#issuecomment-5718567589" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**12 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think the key distinction is between the start of the scenario and the lifetime of the object. Starting a lifeline at the top just means the object participates from the scenario’s beginning; the create message should only appear if this scenario constructs it. Likewise, an `X` destruction marker should only be used if the command actually destroys the object.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/484#issuecomment-5730573917" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**13 :fas-comment:**
</div>

This also matters when `Remark` is used in a `HashSet` or as a `HashMap` key. Since `equals()` compares the `value`, `hashCode()` must be based on the same value; otherwise two equal `Remark` objects could produce different hash codes and collection lookups may fail.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/510#issuecomment-5742883984" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**14 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I’d first compare the debugger configuration with the Gradle configuration that works. In a Windows IntelliJ + WSL setup, check that the Run/Debug configuration uses the WSL JDK and the same project and working directory as Gradle, since IntelliJ can otherwise launch the debugger in a different environment even when `./gradlew` works.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/543#issuecomment-5795034782" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**15 :fas-comment:**
</div>

For a quick free demo, keep the Java app running on your laptop, expose a small HTTP endpoint, and open the laptop’s local IP from your phone on the same Wi‑Fi. For access from anywhere, you’d need a deployed backend or tunnel; GitHub Pages only hosts static files and can’t run the Java program. I’d keep the CLI core and add a thin web layer instead of rewriting the whole app.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/211#issuecomment-5831995439" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**16 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I don’t think the number of files changed is enough by itself. If `Model` can swap its `ArrayList` internally without changing callers, it’s probably detailed design; if callers depend on ordering, duplicate handling, or the saved-data format, those assumptions have become architectural contracts.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/547#issuecomment-5847992802" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**17 :fas-comment:**
</div>

A partial PR seems meaningful when it has a clear “done” boundary that can be reviewed and tested on its own. For example, a model class plus tests is fine if it has a stable API and doesn’t leave broken callers or placeholder code; the PR description can link the follow-up that will connect it to the UI. That makes the increment useful even though the feature isn’t end-to-end yet.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/554#issuecomment-5892803289" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**18 :fas-comment:**
</div>

A shared-interface-only PR can be worthwhile if the method signature is agreed on and the project still builds and passes its tests. It reduces conflicts for the feature branches, but I’d avoid merging speculative methods before the owning feature is understood, otherwise the interface can accumulate unused API. In practice, agree on the smallest signature first, merge it, then rebase both branches and implement the features separately.
</panel>

</panel>


