<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/531#issuecomment-5759406518" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**17 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think for the `taskList.size()` example, a modification watchpoint on `taskList` would detect the field being reassigned, but not an item being removed from the existing list. So `taskList.remove(...)` could reduce the size of the list without triggering it. You can put breakpoints around the code that removes or clears items and then inspect the call stack to see what triggered it.

In general, theres two things:
First, a field changing value where watchpoints would help. 
Second, the object the field refers to changing internally for which breakpoints around the operations that modify the obj are better.
</panel>

