Tested DUDE v1.0.1 on **macOS 15.7.7 (Apple Silicon)** with **Zulu OpenJDK 25.0.3 + JavaFX**.

The following worked in my checks:

- Adding a todo, deadline and event; `list`, case-insensitive `find`, `on`, `mark`, `unmark` and `delete`.
- `undo` restored a deleted task even after using `list`, `find` and `on`. It also reversed duplicate additions and edits.
- The duplicate dialog's Cancel, Add and Edit options behaved as expected. Edit updated an existing deadline's date without adding another task.
- Unknown commands, missing todo details, an impossible date (`2026-02-30`), a reversed event time range and an out-of-range task number were rejected with helpful messages.
- The sidebar showed the correct task numbers, types, status and dates. User messages appeared on the right, DUDE replies on the left, and the conversation scrolled to the latest response. Enlarging and restoring the window kept it readable.
- Typing `de` filtered the commands; Up/Down navigation and Enter/Tab completion worked. The v1.0.1 usage hint stayed visible after typing `deadline submit report` too.
- After `bye` and a restart from the same folder, all three tasks, their dates and the deadline's completed status were preserved. `bye` closed the app cleanly.

No functional issues found in those checks. One small cosmetic observation: at the default window size, the avatar beside some longer error replies displayed `…` instead of 😎. Widening the window restored the emoji; the error text remained readable (screenshots below). The task sidebar and persistent usage hints are helpful!

Testing setup: I checked `java -jar dude.jar` startup, then used a temporary macOS launcher containing the unchanged release JAR and the same JDK for the interactive checks above. This doesn't verify the full GUI workflow through plain `java -jar`, or packaging on a JDK without JavaFX.

