<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/540" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1. :fas-info-circle: tP question - Should the glossary include common technical terms?**
</div>

In this week's tP tasks, we need to [update the glossary of the developer guide](https://nus-cs2103-ay2627-s1.github.io/website/schedule/week7/project.html#3-update-the-dg-user-stories-glossary-nfrs-use-cases). The textbook states that it could be useful to include "domain-specific/technical term[s] that may not be known to all stakeholders, to indicate its definition".

However, this is the developer guide, so I would expect the reader to be familiar with basic technical terms, regardless of who the tP targets. In the glossary, should we still include terms such as "API" and "JSON"? Would this depend on the target audience of our own tP?
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/512" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2. :fas-info-circle: tP question - Must use cases cover all user stories?**
</div>

[In the DG](https://nus-cs2103-ay2627-s1.github.io/website/schedule/week7/project.html#3-update-the-dg-user-stories-glossary-nfrs-use-cases), should we aim to cover all user stories with the use cases?

If yes, should we do so by having more user stories, or by having less but longer/broader user stories?
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/511" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3. :fas-info-circle: [Suggestion] Reduce meta-commentary on course website**
</div>

The course website is our main source of information. However, it often feels overwhelming to read, sometimes unnecessarily so because it is cluttered with other information, such as the teaching team's rationale.

For example:

- On [Admin Info > Participation Marks](https://nus-cs2103-ay2627-s1.github.io/website/admin/participation.html)

> Quizzes open around the weekly briefing time and stay open until the next briefing starts. In some weeks, there will be two quizzes **(because two smaller quizzes are easier for you to manage than one big quiz)**.

> Condition 1: submitted early i.e., within four days of the briefing (briefing day (Friday) + four more days -&gt; Tuesday 23:59) **(reason: to encourage learning the weekly topics before doing the weekly project tasks)**

- On [Week 7 > Project](https://nus-cs2103-ay2627-s1.github.io/website/schedule/week7/project.html#1-do-a-workflow-practice-session-thu-oct-1st-23-59)

> Congrats! You are done with this workflow practice session. **It might have felt like a lot of 'jumping through hoops' for very little gain, but as you go through the tP, this 'workflow overhead' is expected to feel progressively less 'draggy'.** Hopefully, it will be almost second nature to you by the time you finish the tP.

Some of these sections are dimmed, following one of the "layering" techniques mentioned on [Admin Info > Using this Website > Information Layers](https://nus-cs2103-ay2627-s1.github.io/website/admin/usingThisWebsite.html#information-layers). However, it still contributes to large chunks of prose, which makes skimming through for quick reference difficult, at least for those who prefer to read themselves and not through an LLM.

A possible solution would be to hide them into tooltips ([Why?](# "It can fit a long, long, long, long, long chunk of text, without adding too much text on the surface")). In fact, this is already done in sections such as [Week 7 > Adding a photo](https://nus-cs2103-ay2627-s1.github.io/website/schedule/week7/project.html#adding-a-photo).

> Note the need for lowercase **(why lowercase?)**

The course has a lot of material and information that we need to digest. I wonder if this could slightly improve readability.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/424" expanded>
