<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/542#issuecomment-5782265611" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**16 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Perhaps the notes had used hardware as a precondition because different use cases of the EZ-Link machine depend on different hardware, and hardware failure may not always be catastrophic. However, I agree that such a general precondition (everything is in order) does not seem useful, since it could easily be applied to every use case.

More useful "working hardware"-related preconditions could be something like
- For UC03 process NETS payment: The NETS POS terminal is in working order
- For UC02 cash payment: The cash machine is in working order; there is enough cash as change

For our tP, this is perhaps less relevant. Perhaps GUI-only use cases might require a working mouse/touchpad/TrackPoint/touchscreen, whereas other use cases should only require the keyboard?

However, can we omit these universal preconditions entirely, or should we still specify them somewhere?

For example, the EZ-Link machine probably needs the EZ-Link card reader to be working for every use case, yet we never mention that, but only mention other components like the POS terminal, which feels misleading, as if only the POS terminal needs to work, and not the EZ-Link card reader.
</panel>

</panel>


