<div slot="header">

### 34. PHAD..EJAS `@advaitio` (16 posts) 
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/226" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1. :fas-info-circle: Request to help with smoke testing JAR file**
</div>

Hello! I would appreciate help smoke-testing **Nudge**, my task-management assistant.

- **Release page:** [Nudge v0.1](https://github.com/advaitio/ip/releases/tag/v0.1) — download `nudge.jar` under **Assets**.
- **Link to the User Guide:** https://advaitio.github.io/ip/
- **Which OSes to test on:** Windows and Linux (macOS has been tested locally)
- **Things to check specifically:** Cross-platform launch, core commands, deadline sorting, error handling, and data persistence

## Preparation

1. Install **Java 25** and confirm it using `java -version`.
2. Open the release page linked above and download `nudge.jar` from **Assets**.
3. Put the JAR in an otherwise empty folder.
4. Open a terminal in that folder and run:

'''bash
java -jar nudge.jar
'''

## Testing

Please refer to the User Guide and choose your own realistic task descriptions, dates, and command order. Feel free to explore Nudge as a normal user. In particular, it would be helpful to check that:

- The GUI launches and displays correctly.
- Todos, deadlines, and events can be added and managed using the documented commands.
- `list` and `find` show the expected tasks.
- `sort` orders multiple deadlines by date.
- Invalid input produces a helpful error without crashing the app.
- Tasks persist after closing and reopening Nudge from the same folder.
- `bye` closes the application.

## Guide for testers

- Please post screenshots from your smoke test.
- Please mention the OS and version, processor architecture, and output of `java -version`.
- If anything fails, please include the command used and the complete error message.

Thank you for helping test Nudge!
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/175#issuecomment-5631160842" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I don't think every unusual input needs to be handled separately. I'd consider it a major bug if the program crashes or handles a valid command incorrectly.
For unspecified cases like `list 67`, either behaviour seems reasonable as long as it is consistent. I would reject it because accepting unused arguments can make the command syntax less clear to the user.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/168#issuecomment-5645902663" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

The course already suggests asking AI for several possible approaches before implementation. One follow-up I’ve found useful is asking it when its recommendation would change.
So after receiving the options, I might ask:
- What assumption does your recommendation depend on?
- What problems could this cause in the future?
- What change would make another approach preferable, and how difficult would switching be?

This usually gives more nuanced responses rather than simply claiming that one option is “best”. I find it particularly useful for software-design decisions where there isn't a clear better option.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/192#issuecomment-5652262178" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

