<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/211#issuecomment-5667402270" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**5 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I agree with @spencercdz and @Brendan-Lim that the number of affected components alone does not determine whether a decision is architectural. It also matters whether other components depend on that decision as part of the system design, or whether they are depending on an implementation detail that should have stayed within `Model`.
I think the context also matters. For example, using an `ArrayList` would normally be a detailed design decision. But if the way data is represented is important for meeting a system quality such as response time when handling a very large amount of data, then that representation decision could become architecturally significant.
So the same kind of decision may be detailed design in one system but architecturally significant in another, depending on how important it is to the overall system.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/218#issuecomment-5677438152" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**6 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think the logging level itself helps with this distinction too. W6.3b shows that if the logging level is set to `WARNING`, lower-level messages such as `INFO` are simply not written to the log. So keeping a lower-level log message for validation does not necessarily mean every normal user mistake has to appear in the logs during normal use.

I would also distinguish between the user entering an invalid value and the validation code itself failing. If `p/abc` is correctly rejected, the program is behaving as expected, so a user-facing error message may be enough, with a lower-level log message only if it would be useful for troubleshooting. But if the validation code throws an unexpected exception or causes the program to enter an incorrect state, that seems like a much stronger reason to log it at a higher level.

If we do log the rejected input, I think recording the reason for rejection should usually be enough rather than storing the entire command or phone number.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/219#issuecomment-5677616580" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**7 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think it makes more sense to separate error cases based on whether they actually change the sequence of interactions instead of giving every validation rule its own branch. If two invalid inputs are handled in basically the same way, one alternative like `[input is invalid]` should be enough. 

A separate diagram would make sense when an error case has a noticeably different flow and if including it would make the main diagram harder to follow.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/231#issuecomment-5709852049" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**8 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Smoke-tested the Clarry v1.1.0 prerelease on macOS 26.5.1 (Apple Silicon/arm64) using Zulu OpenJDK 25.0.3.

The downloaded `clarry.jar` launched successfully from a fresh writable folder using `java -jar clarry.jar`.

I tested the following:

- GUI rendering, shark mascot, text wrapping, scrolling, and resizing/maximising.
- `help`, `todo`, `deadline`, `event`, `list`, `find`, `on`, `mark`, `unmark`, and `delete`.
- Unknown commands, a missing todo description, a duplicate task, an out-of-range task number, an invalid date, and an event whose end precedes its start.
- Persistence after closing and reopening the app from the same folder.

All of the above behaved as expected, and the invalid inputs produced clear error messages without crashing or changing the task list. The remaining tasks were still present after reopening the app.

<img width="935" height="981" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/f4d694d8-d3ea-4cfc-a2e0-054c5eac0e70" />
<img width="1580" height="1035" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/c201a1d9-1326-44ed-ab54-1dffbda38463" />
<img width="686" height="879" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/70ebe394-61cd-4b68-8bd4-296ea6ee85c6" />
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/266#issuecomment-5709903522" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**9 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think it helps to separate what happens while reading the file from what happens when the program next saves. Recovering the 19 valid tasks in memory does not have to mean immediately rewriting the original file and permanently removing the corrupted line.

One possible approach is to start in a partial-recovery state, clearly tell the user which lines could not be loaded, and leave the original file untouched. Before saving again, the program could preserve the original as a backup or ask the user to confirm that it should be replaced with the recovered version. This allows the user to continue using the valid data without losing the chance to inspect or repair the rejected entries.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/343#issuecomment-5710021707" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**10 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think the main consideration is whether the class represents a distinct responsibility with a clear interface, rather than whether that responsibility happens to be implemented by one class or several. W6.2c describes a component as a major part of the system with one coherent responsibility, so a class would not automatically become a component just because it makes an important decision within Logic or Model.

At the same time, that decision can still be architecturally important without needing its own box. 

I would show the class as a separate component only if the rest of the system treats it as a distinct responsibility through a clear interface. Otherwise, keeping it within the larger component would make the diagram’s level of abstraction more consistent.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/344#issuecomment-5710195800" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**11 :fas-comment:**
</div>

This feels quite similar to #218. One distinction I found useful there was that the user-facing error message and the log serve different purposes. The error message should help the user understand what went wrong and what to do next.

So just because an error is shown to the user does not mean it also needs to be logged. A normal invalid command might only need a clear error message, while something like a save failure would probably be useful to both show to the user and record in the logs. An internal problem that the program recovers from without affecting the user might only need to be logged.

W6.3b also shows that when the logging level is set to WARNING, lower-level messages such as INFO are not written to the log. So events that are occasionally useful for debugging can still be logged at a lower level without appearing when a higher logging level is being used.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/450#issuecomment-5739885897" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**12 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think the diagram is mainly showing the interaction at runtime, rather than how `Parser` originally got access to `ParserFactory`. As Prof clarified, a reference to the factory could have been passed when the `Parser` was created, for example with `new Parser(this)`, so `getInfo()` can still be a callback even though that earlier step is not shown.

Showing something like `new Parser(this)` or adding a short note could make that clearer. I would be less inclined to change it to `Parser(getInfo)`, because that would suggest the method itself is being passed, which is a more specific implementation choice. So I think the original diagram is still reasonable, but a bit more context would remove the ambiguity.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/484#issuecomment-5739928823" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**13 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think it helps to see `@Override` as a check rather than something that makes the overriding happen. `Remark` already inherits `hashCode()` from `Object`, so defining `public int hashCode()` with the same signature is what overrides it. The annotation just lets the compiler check that the override is valid.

So if removing `@Override` makes the error disappear, I would be careful about leaving it out. It could mean the method is not actually overriding `Object.hashCode()` as intended, for example because of a spelling or capitalisation mismatch. In that case, keeping `@Override` is useful because it exposes the problem instead of hiding it.

If the correctly written version still gives an error, the exact compiler message would probably make the cause clearer.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/530#issuecomment-5789315313" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**14 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think the main check should go wherever the responsibility for keeping a `Task` valid belongs. If every `Task` must have a non-blank description, the constructor and any method that can change the description seem like the best places for it. That catches the invalid state before it gets passed around and makes the source of the problem easier to locate.

I would only repeat the assertion at a component boundary if that component has a separate assumption—for example, if it only accepts completed tasks even though incomplete tasks are valid elsewhere. In that case, the assertion documents something new. If it is just the same `!description.isBlank()` check at every boundary, it would probably be unnecessary duplication.

Also, if a blank description can come from normal user input or stored data, it should be handled through validation rather than relying on an assertion.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/531#issuecomment-5789410538" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**15 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I’d start by narrowing down when the value becomes wrong rather than logging every possible method. For example, I could check the size after loading, after processing a command, and before updating the UI. That would give me a smaller part of the execution to investigate.

Since `taskList` is mutable, a watchpoint on the field might not catch an item being removed from the existing list. If the bug is reproducible, I’d use breakpoints around the relevant mutations and inspect the call stack. If it only happens occasionally, targeted logging of the operation and the size before and after it would probably be more useful. Once the cause is fixed, a focused test could help catch the same bug in future.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/540#issuecomment-5789518530" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**16 :fas-comment:**
</div>

The glossary seems most useful as a place to clarify how terms are used in the project, rather than as a list of technical acronyms. A developer can easily look up the general meanings of API or JSON, so repeating those definitions may not add much.

For example, instead of defining JSON itself, it may be more useful to define “project data file” if that refers to a particular JSON file used by the application. One way to decide could be to ask whether two developers might interpret a requirement or design differently without the glossary entry. If so, the term is worth including; otherwise, it can probably be left out.
</panel>

</panel>


