<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/519" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1. :fas-info-circle: 💡Neat trick to quickly visualise a repo**
</div>

## Change `github` → `gitdiagram` in URL to visualise a repo

If you take any GitHub repo URL and change **`github` → `gitdiagram`**:

`https://github.com/owner/repo` → `https://gitdiagram.com/owner/repo`

It automatically generates an interactive diagram of the repository, showing the main parts of the system and how they relate to each other. You can also click the components to jump back to the relevant files/directories.

For example:

`https://github.com/``{``your_username``}``/tp` → `https://gitdiagram.com/``{``your_username``}``/tp`

This has been particularly handy for tP now as it gave me a quick mental model of the architecture of the tP codebase before diving deeper into the Developer Guide and tutorials. As I work on the repo, I can always refer back to the diagram to see the new changes between each components/modules.

GitDiagram: `https://github.com/ahmedkhaleel2004/gitdiagram`

</panel>

