<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/135#issuecomment-5519223762" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Instead of referencing the members of the class as is, try using a nested Syntax class. Calling Syntax.TODO is a qualified reference and so there is no forward reference issue as in problem 1.

```
public class Message ``{``

    public static final String MSG_HELP = "Here are the available commands:\n"
            + "  - list : Views all tasks\n"
            + "  - " + Syntax.TODO + " : Adds a todo task\n"
            + "  - " + Syntax.DEADLINE + " : Adds a deadline task\n"
            + "  - " + Syntax.EVENT + " : Adds an event task\n"
            + "  - " + Syntax.INDEX + " : Manipulates task by index\n"
            + "  - " + Syntax.FIND + " : Finds event by keyword\n"
            + "  - bye : Exits the program\n"
            + "  - Date and Time (DT) Format: " + Syntax.DATE_TIME + "\n";

    private static final class Syntax ``{``
        private static final String INDEX = "mark <index> | unmark <index> | delete <index>";
        private static final String TODO = "todo <description>";
        private static final String DEADLINE = "deadline <description> /by <due DT>";
        private static final String EVENT = "event <description> /from <start DT> /to <end DT>";
        private static final String FIND = "find <keyword>";
        private static final String DATE_TIME = "yyyy-MM-dd HHmm(24-H clock)";

    // Many more definitions for messages that I will leave out
```

Another alternative is just to make `msg_help` return from a method instead of a constant. To prevent multiple calls incurring overhead you can cache the String value in a seperate private field.
```
public class Message ``{``
    public String cache = "";
    //your private statics
    
    String getHelp() ``{``
        if(!cache.empty()) return cache;
        else ``{``
            cache = "Here are the available commands:\n"
            + "  - list : Views all tasks\n"
            + "  - " + Syntax.TODO + " : Adds a todo task\n"
            + "  - " + Syntax.DEADLINE + " : Adds a deadline task\n"
            + "  - " + Syntax.EVENT + " : Adds an event task\n"
            + "  - " + Syntax.INDEX + " : Manipulates task by index\n"
            + "  - " + Syntax.FIND + " : Finds event by keyword\n"
            + "  - bye : Exits the program\n"
            + "  - Date and Time (DT) Format: " + Syntax.DATE_TIME + "\n";
            return cache;
        ``}``
    ``}``
//Many more definitions for messages that I will leave out  
```

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/135#issuecomment-5526386320" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

> [@yeoznc](https://github.com/yeoznc) I think the cache may be unnecessary here. If MSG_HELP is made private static final as suggested above, it is only initialized once anyway, so getHelpMessage() can just return it without introducing mutable state.

Apologies for the confusion, I meant that the cache could be declared public and placed above the private statics, following our CheckStyle rules while not violating Java Compiler Rules. I have changed the code accordingly.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/139#issuecomment-5526541747" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**5 :fas-comment:**
</div>

One thing to take note is that the course highly recommends code reuse. By having the ability to read other people's code of the same language _after_ developing yours, you can quite easily identify differences in implementation and learn from that. On the other hand, if people started using esoteric languages for their programs (which is quite easy with AI nowadays) I'm not sure you would learn much.

This course isn't results oriented. If it was, people could easily game the system by having Claude Fable code the entire iP, with all the required commits etc, in any way they liked, without caring about how they even got there in the first place. We're looking to slowly refine our skills alongside our project by learning from others (including some AI code), and when the time comes, apply these skills in the workplace. In my opinion, it's less about the outcome of the project and more about the process you went through when developing it.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/160#issuecomment-5570885840" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**6 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Also faced this problem and the solution works for me. 👍
I would recommend submitting a PR as the old code's functionality is not aligned with what people normally expect of a scrollable chat. 
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/154#issuecomment-5571160333" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**7 :fas-comment:**
</div>

When we add interfaces / abstract classes _in anticipation_ of extending it for future classes, we are essentially guessing what methods the new requirements may have, and this could backfire heavily if it requires a very different type of implementation, or in some cases, not require any at all.
On the other hand, if we are in the iterative process of developing new features, we can first understand the classes we should add, giving us insight as to what interfaces / abstract classes we can make to simplify our design. This overall makes coding the feature much more obvious and straightforward as compared to trying to force-fit the new feature into the old interfaces we initially had.
</panel>

