<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/168#issuecomment-5598128646" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**11 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I currently use AI in two ways that I have found useful.

First, I use AI as a code reviewer. I keep an AGENTS.md file containing instructions on what the AI should check, such as coding conventions, code quality, testing requirements, and project-specific constraints. The AI reads these instructions before reviewing my work, which helps it give more relevant and consistent feedback. Many of the general instructions can also be reused across different projects, while project-specific rules can be added when needed. However, I still verify its suggestions myself because AI reviews can miss issues or produce incorrect advice.

Second, I use AI as a tutor while learning React through a side project. Instead of asking it to build the entire project for me, I ask it to break each feature into smaller steps, explain the relevant React concepts, and guide me through the implementation one step at a time. This allows me to make progress on the project while understanding what the code does, rather than simply copying generated code.
</panel>

