<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/144" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1. :fas-info-circle: 💡Shortcut to reuse commands**
</div>

I often find myself using the same commands like `git log --oneline --graph --all` to monitor commits. If you're in the same boat, you can create a shell alias to save time:

**1. Open your shell configuration file**

'''bash
vim ~/.bashrc
'''

> Use `~/.zshrc` if you're on macOS or using Zsh.

**2. Add an alias line**

'''bash
alias &lt;shortcut>='&lt;command>'
'''

For example:

'''bash
alias gall='git log --oneline --graph --all'
'''

**3. Reload the config**

Restart your terminal, or run:

'''bash
source ~/.bashrc
'''

Now instead of typing the whole command, i just have to type the alias gall into the CLI. 
Some other aliases I created:
alias cdip='cd /path/to/cs2103t/ip'
alias gp='git push'

Keep Calm & Software Engineer. 
</panel>

