<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/119#issuecomment-5534998063" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Great question which got me thinking. My view is that the UML diagrams only describe stable, observable states. 
If the intermediate state where the Task object is transitioning from one TaskList object to another (and is in Neither in that moment), it violates the UML diagram if it is exposed to the user. 

This seems to have high relevance to Parallel/Concurrent programming, where a thread/process could observe that intermediate state. This can be prevented by enforcing atomicity or locks (yay CS2106).
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/94#issuecomment-5535435127" expanded>
