My view is that there are a few guidelines when one decides when to name their literal. For one, when a literal seems to appear out of nowhere (hence "magic"), naming it can provide meaning and convey the origin of it. This aligns with the principle of making our code being self-explanatory. 

Adding to that, in certain situations where the meaning of the literal is obvious, it may not be necessary to define it.


</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/168#issuecomment-5586489111" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**7 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Prompting tips:
When I get stuck on certain questions, I like to explain my thought process and ask AI to complete/explain what is missing or wrong. This helps me extrapolate from my current knowledge.

Moreover, if I still do not understand their explanation, I like to reply with "You are wrong" to 1) test if the AI is hallucinating, and 2) see if they are able to defend their answer, which often solidify their explanation or exposes gap in their reasoning.


Learning tip:
It feels inevitable that a computing major has to resort to AI, else he/she loses out. This breeds over-reliance on AI. A rule of thumb that I stick to is that for every few prompts, I try to learn a new concept, however small it may be.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/166#issuecomment-5586675059" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**8 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I strongly agree with the part where it says "depends on the context".

Tight deadlines to meet:
Say you want to create a prototype of an app to pitch to investors in a few days, where they will just look at the design and not at the code. Deciding to refactor at this stage will incur time that could have (subjective) been better spent getting feedback from colleagues/teammates. Moreover, if the code base is already large, refactoring may result in bugs which incur more time.

Code that no one reads:
Suppose u have worked on a project which now u or anyone have no use of. Do you spend a few hours tidying it up despite the fact that you won't use it anymore? Unless one finds tidying code therapeutic, it is not worth spending a lot of time refactoring.

To confuse potential hackers/exploiters:
"To confuse the enemy, one must confuse him/herself"
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/171#issuecomment-5596724825" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**9 :fas-comment:**
</div>

In my view, for a solo project, the main benefit of using GitHub issues to track bugs and fixes is the ease of searchability. Rather than grouping your bug fixes together with all the commits, you have a separate section storing it. 

You would be able to review the bug and the fixes so that you can replicate the fixes should you encounter the same/similar situation again. 

Personally, I occasionally GitHub issues to keep track of dangerous bugs and the corresponding fixes, or fixes for bugs that are common. I also add  a note on why the fix works.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/175#issuecomment-5603259842" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**10 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Bugs can be subjective; a certain output from a input can be defined to be a bug if it deviates from the intended behaviour. For example, 'list 67' can be intended to just return the whole list, return the first 67 element, or return an error message, and the developer ought to implement such that it exhibit the correct behaviour according to the specification.

It was not specified how to handle 'list 67', so my view is that it is up to us to decide the behaviour; it is not the focus of CS2103T, which is focused on the software engineering process.

Part of the software engineering process would be deciding which of these output should be the intended behaviour. We as the implementors then have to think in terms of the user: "If I am a user, when would I want to use 'list 67' and what purpose does it serve?"

Personally, I would define 'list 67' to list the first 67 entries in the list, similar to 'head -n 67' in unix. This prevents the need of printing the whole list, which can be extended to be very long. A follow up question would be: Would list grow to be very large? For an average user, would there be 67 events/tasks that he/she balances at once?
</panel>

