<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/262#issuecomment-5693189567" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**6 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I ran into the same thing in my iP. I ended up keeping the command dispatch in one place instead of creating a separate Command class for every command.
My parser returns a command type and its arguments, then the dispatcher uses a switch where each case either returns a simple response or calls a helper such as addTask(), deleteTask(), or findTasks(). The dispatch still exists, but it stays at one level of abstraction and reads like an overview of what the program can do.
I think the long method becomes a problem when each branch also contains parsing, validation, storage and response formatting. If those details are already separated, a flat dispatch method seems reasonable. I would consider command objects only when the commands become complex enough to benefit from their own state or behaviour, rather than introducing a class per command just to remove the switch.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/260#issuecomment-5707438423" expanded>
<div slot="header">

