<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/147" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2. :fas-info-circle: Safest way to re-sync a local iP clone that has fallen behind the remote**
</div>

Hi, I have run into a situation where my local clone of my iP has fallen behind my remote, and I am not sure of the safest way to reconcile it.

On GitHub my remote has `branch-Level-10` and a few branches and tags that my local copy does not have, and my remote `master` is ahead of my local one. My local `master` is still sitting at the Level 9 merge, and my local `origin/master` is stale because this clone has not fetched in a while.

What is the safest way to bring the local copy back in sync without losing anything or messing up the level tags? Is a plain `git fetch --all --tags` followed by a merge or a fast-forward the right approach here, or is it cleaner to just re-clone the repo?

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/142#issuecomment-5537370423" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

From looking at AB3, they also do not seem to have a convention for this (roughly an equal split between capitalised and lowercase in `src/main`), so I doubt it is an explicit rule set by SE-EDU. The checkstyle config does not check it either, as there is no `JavadocStyle` module in `config/checkstyle/checkstyle.xml`, so both forms pass the build.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/145#issuecomment-5537572240" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Hi, I think you can create a branch for Level 10 first from master (`git branch branch-Level-10`) and use `git log` to find the hash of the last commit before Level 10 started. Then you can do `git reset --hard &lt;hash>`. (Make sure you have created the branch earlier, as otherwise you can lose track of your work.) Then merge it back with `git merge --no-ff branch-Level-10`. A plain merge will just fast-forward and leave you with a straight line again, so you need `--no-ff` to get the merge commit.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/146#issuecomment-5537870302" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**5 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Hi, you can try this:

```
git config --global alias.acp '!f() ``{`` git add -A && git commit -m "$*" && git push; ``}``; f'
```

which allows you to do `git acp <message>`. The `-A` is used to say add everything in the repo, not just your current directory and below, which is what `git add .` does.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/147#issuecomment-5537908498" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**6 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Thanks, I tried it and it has worked. I was working on two machines, so my local branch on one of them was stale.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/147#issuecomment-5537911580" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**7 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Closing as this is resolved. The fetch showed my local master was simply behind and not diverged, so a fast-forward was all that was needed.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/146#issuecomment-5538043742" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**8 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Hi, sorry for repeating Suvan's answer. I had the thread open while I was writing mine out and only saw theirs after I posted.

One thing worth adding though. With `$1`, if you typed something like `git acp test1 test2`, it would drop the other words and the commit message would only be `test1`. Using `$*` instead takes the whole thing, so multi-word messages work without needing quotes.

</panel>

