<div slot="header">

**7 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Hey @qqnoodle! I ran into this exact question on Level-10. What worked for me was to stop treating the GUI as the thing that "runs" the chatbot, and instead introduce one "connecting" method that both the CLI and the GUI call.

Add a single method to your main logic class:

'''java
public String getResponse(String input) ``{``
    // parse + execute ONE command, and RETURN the response text
    // (instead of printing it)
``}``
'''

That method is your boundary. It takes a raw input line and returns a String, it has no clue whether that String ends up in a terminal or a chat bubble. Your Parser/TaskList never import anything from javafx.*, so the logic layer stays completely GUI-agnostic.

Then both front-ends become thin callers of it:
- CLI: the run() loop reads a line → getResponse → prints the result.
- GUI: handleUserInput() reads the text field → getResponse → drops the result into a dialog box.

So handleUserInput ends up doing zero command logic, just wiring:

'''java
private void handleUserInput() ``{``
      String input = userInput.getText();
      String response = duke.getResponse(input);
      dialogContainer.getChildren().addAll(
          DialogBox.getUserDialog(input, userImage),
          DialogBox.getDukeDialog(response, dukeImage)
      );
      userInput.clear();
``}``
'''

the flow (GUI → main app → logic → back to GUI) is good. it constructs the logic object and injects it into the controller (fxmlLoader.getController().setDuke(duke)), so the controller just holds a reference and delegates.
</panel>

