One thing to note is that currently this is an individual project, in a team environment where others depend on a shared development database, a maintenance window should be announced where you can fix your database (convert parseable strings into the correct format / unparseable strings can be manually recovered etc, and update your scripts with valid datetime generators)
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/142#issuecomment-5537240812" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Based on the **SE-EDU Java Coding Standards**, `@param`:
1. The parameter name must match the exact identifier name in the method which follows standard Java naming conventions (camelCase, etc).
2. The first word following the description following the param name should be capitalized

E.g.
`@param x X coordinate of position.`
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/138#issuecomment-5537313884" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

My guess is that ChatGPT is quoting **formal UML theory** (where both are whole part but is weak vs strong). 

The course treats "Container-containee" explicitly for aggregation. So, I believe we should follow accept separate labels so as well to avoid confusion.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/218#issuecomment-5693370723" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**5 :fas-comment:**
</div>

As W6.3a explains, logs are mainly a "black box" for troubleshooting. So, I wouldn't normally log an invalid input such as `p/abc` at `WARNING` as the validator rejected it correctly, so the application is already behaving as expected. 

If recording validation failures would help with debugging, it could be logged at `FINE` without including the full user input. I think we can see that in W6.3b, lower-level messages can be excluded during normal use by setting logging level to `WARNING`

I would reserve `WARNING` and `SEVERE` for unexpected application or system problems. 
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/262#issuecomment-5693587210" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**6 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I don't think a long method is always bad. In W5.5d, the textbook says that code smells "don't always indicate a problem". So long-method warning may be something to investigate not always a problem to solve.

For SLAP, the main issue is if getResponse() mixes the abstraction levels. Personally, I would extract each command's parsing and execution logic into methods while keeping the selection in one place.

In my implementation, I did a command hierarchy: my parser identifies the command and returns a Command object, while each command class handles its own execution. The remaining switch only performs dispatch, so it stays at one abstraction level.
</panel>

