<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/344#issuecomment-5726035297" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**7 :fas-comment:**
</div>

A useful rule could be: log at `WARNING` only when a maintainer may need to take action.

A single malformed command is normally expected behaviour, so a clear user-facing error is enough. If input patterns are useful during development, logging a sanitised reason at `FINE`/`INFO` seems more appropriate than recording the full command, which could contain personal task details.

If repeated invalid input becomes relevant, an aggregated count or rate-limited message would be more useful than a warning for every attempt. This keeps the log focused on actionable events such as corrupted storage, failed saves, or unexpected exceptions.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/450#issuecomment-5726061488" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**8 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I agree that the call sequence is the key point. If `ParserFactory` invokes `Parser`, and `Parser` later invokes `getInfo()` on that caller before returning, then `getInfo()` acts as a callback.

Passing `factory::getInfo` explicitly could make the dependency clearer in Java, but it does not change whether the call is a callback. It only changes how `Parser` obtains the function reference.


</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/530#issuecomment-5758952224" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**9 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I would separate validation from assertions here. If an empty description can originate from user input or persisted data, the constructor should reject it with normal validation/an exception; assertions may be disabled, so they should not be the only protection.

For an internal invariant, the strongest assertion is usually closest to the state-changing operation owned by `Task`. Boundary assertions can still help when a boundary introduces a distinct assumption, but repeating the same “description is non-empty” assertion at every receiver becomes noise.

So I would put the main invariant check where `Task` is created or mutated, then add boundary assertions only for assumptions that are specific to that boundary.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/531#issuecomment-5758955214" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**10 :fas-comment:**
</div>

A data/watchpoint is a good first choice when you can reproduce the bug: it stops at the write, and the call stack tells you which path changed the value.

One refinement is to make the breakpoint conditional where the debugger supports it. For example, stop only when the size changes from `5` to `4`, rather than on every legitimate add/remove operation.

If the problem is intermittent, logging state transitions at the mutation points is more useful than logging every UI read. Include the operation and before/after size, then remove or lower the logging level once the defect is found so that the permanent log stays useful.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/519#issuecomment-5758957635" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**11 :fas-comment:**
</div>

This looks useful as a quick orientation tool. One caution is that the diagram is generated by a third-party service, so I would use it only for public repositories or repositories whose sharing policy permits it.

I also like the idea of comparing the generated dependency graph with the Developer Guide. The diagram can show current static dependencies, while the DG explains intended responsibilities and runtime relationships. A mismatch is not automatically a bug, but it gives a concrete place to inspect for accidental coupling.
</panel>

</panel>


