<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/148#issuecomment-5538629638" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

 You could try some of these to see if it works! You don't need the Assigned fx:id table to check the IDs. You can open the .fxml file directly and look for them there, for e.g.

&lt;TextField fx:id="userInput" ... />
&lt;Button fx:id="sendButton" ... />
&lt;ScrollPane fx:id="scrollPane" ... />
&lt;VBox fx:id="dialogContainer" ... />

You can also check that these names match the corresponding @FXML fields in your controller. Scene Builder generated controller skeleton should also show the full field names, that is another way to verify them without relying on the truncated table.

For the table, if the fx:id column won't resize, you can try widening the entire left-hand panel by dragging the divider between it and the page.

You could also test the Windows display scaling. Since the text looks clipped, you could temporarily set Settings → System → Display → Scale to 100%, restart Scene Builder, and see whether anything changes.

What I did was checking the FXML/controller skeleton first so that I could carry on with Part 4. Hopefully it works for you!
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/168#issuecomment-5585794132" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Came across a pretty interesting feature in GitHub Copilot CLI called Rubber Duck that might be useful for 2103T, especially for larger iP/tP changes. Rather than getting the same model to review its own solution, Rubber Duck uses a different model to critique the main session's plan, implementation or tests. It focuses on substantive issues such as logic errors, design problems and missed edge cases rather than formatting or style. 

For example, before starting a larger refactor, you could first come up with an implementation plan and then run:
/rubber-duck Critique this plan for unnecessary coupling, class responsibility issues and edge cases I might have missed.

I thought this was quite neat since it gives you a genuinely different second opinion before committing to an approach, rather than just asking the original session are you sure (which is what I always do haha).

Of course, two models agreeing still does not prove anything is correct, so I would still read the changes myself and rely on tests, Checkstyle and the actual requirements afterwards! 

GitHub docs: https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli/rubber-duck?utm_source=chatgpt.com 
</panel>

