<img width="1363" height="1528" alt="megatron griffin" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/0f6178be-3ed1-4361-97c7-f37c94cff211" />

AI was very useful in generating the images. Apart from that, it can give you the basic styling of the GUI elements.

However, it tends to make mistakes like grouping elements wrongly, and as others have mentioned, resizing it will cause weird unexpected behaviours. Describing the UI problem/behaviour you faced to the AI may not be enough for it to understand the problem, and I find that it is much easier to take a screenshot of the issue and give it a brief description instead.

But having said that, sometimes it was much easier for me to just go in and fix the problem rather than have to deal with the frustrating mess of describing a UI issue to the AI.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/157#issuecomment-5558526850" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Boolean parameters are not inherently bad for readability, but if I do not know what this iP is about, `new Task("read book", false)` might be interpreted as "this task is not visible" or "this task is not available" instead of the intended "this task is not done". Without checking the constructor, a reader cannot tell what the "false" refers to (which you did mention in your last para), and a named variable can help to clarify the meaning.

I did something like what you mentioned as your last alternative. I have an enum `TaskStatus` that is either `NOT_DONE` or `DONE`, which leaves little for guesswork/inference. It also contains the "marker" (the [ ] or [X]), so it could possibly help if I decided to do some of the category B extensions like RecurringTasks.

I guess ultimately the answer to your question (as with other engineering questions) is... **it depends**. As others have mentioned, for this iP where the task state is either done or not done, you might get away with using true and false. But if you want to be more clear to the reader and remove any guesswork (or tracing code), having an enum would certainly help.

</panel>

