<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/194#issuecomment-5652548444" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**6 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I strongly support this suggestion. As a student, I do not have any particular issue with the possibility that AI was used to help draft W6.3. AI can be useful for generating ideas, examples, and explanations. However, the final material still needs to be edited by someone who understands that students are trying to learn the topic, not admire the writing — or debug the prose before debugging the program.
The problem is especially noticeable in W6.3. The section introduces mistakes, defects, infections, and failures; explains how they form a chain; discusses non-code causes; presents a shopping-cart example; explains why debugging is difficult; lists several bad debugging approaches; introduces the TRAFFIC mnemonic; connects debugging to the scientific method; and then continues through tracking, reproducing, simplifying, finding origins, focusing, isolating, and fixing. These are all potentially useful ideas, but they are presented with so much detail that it becomes difficult to identify the main learning points.
The writing also has several very obvious AI characteristics: long sentences, repeated explanations, frequent em dashes, formal transitions, carefully balanced contrasts, and groups of three. Everything is “systematic, iterative, and evidence-based”. Every point is not only explained, but also qualified, expanded, and restated. The text repeatedly uses phrases such as “in other words”, “consequently”, “it is important to note”, and “in conclusion” — presumably because one conclusion is not enough when three conclusions can be provided.
A simple distinction such as “A defect is an error in the code. If the problem comes from configuration, data, dependencies, deployment, or requirements, it is a cause elsewhere” could be communicated directly. Instead, the reader is taken through multiple caveats about tests being wrong, requirements being mistaken, and the program potentially being correct while everything around it is incorrect. That information may be technically valid, but the amount of explanation makes a basic concept feel unnecessarily intimidating.
If you have read up to here, full disclaimer that all the other paragraphs apart from this were generated slop, but the point still stands that it is just so tiring to read through slop.
This is not a request to remove accuracy, nuance, or useful examples. It is a request to improve clarity, structure, and readability. Students need to know which concepts are central, which examples illustrate them, and which details are optional. When every sentence receives the same level of emphasis, students may assume that everything is equally important — which is a particularly effective way to make nothing feel understandable.
In conclusion, I support the original poster’s point. The problem is not AI itself; it is unedited AI-style writing that is verbose, repetitive, overqualified, and determined to explain a straightforward idea from every possible angle. Students should not have to perform debugging on the teaching material before they can learn how to debug their programs.
</panel>

