### 57. TAN .. BIN `@origami10004` (11 posts) 
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/209" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1. :fas-info-circle: [Suggestion] Security considerations for cross-platform JAR smoke testing**
</div>

We are recommended to ask for help from others to do smoke testing for out jar files to make sure that they run on different OS. However, a jar file is an executable, nothing is stopping someone from maliciously adding malware and get others to run. Even outside of malicious intent its entirely possible for a bug to impact someone's system.
I'm certain most of us would not be malicious enough to do such a thing but it could still be a nonzero chance that it could happen. Hence why I am reluctant to test anyone's jar files.

Would it make sense to advise students to setup a sandbox for running others jar files to ensure that it wont be affected?

Alternatively it could also be suggested that students setup Github actions CI to automatically test their jar files on different platforms such that there is no need to actually run it on anyone's systems
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/206" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2. :fas-info-circle: [Suggestion] Further information for installing Java 25 for linux**
</div>

On the page for installing Java 25 on linux systems, the guide is said to be written for Ubuntu 22.04 and steps for other Debian based Linux distros should be similar.

However, the commands to be run only works for Ubuntu 22.04 and above, older versions of ubuntu distros does not natively support installing java 25 with `sudo apt install openjdk-25-jdk`

