### 58. XIE ..IWEN `@XieKaiwen` (11 posts) <span class=text-warning>:octicon-eye:</span>
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/529" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1. :fas-info-circle: Request to help with smoke testing JAR file**
</div>

* [Link to JAR file](https://github.com/XieKaiwen/ip/releases)
* [Link to the User Guide](https://xiekaiwen.github.io/ip/)
* Which OS'es to test on: Windows | Linux
* Things to check specifically:
- The released JAR launches successfully and opens the Quackie GUI.
- Adding todo, deadline, and event tasks, e.g. `todo read book`, `deadline submit report /by 2026-09-25`, `event meeting /from 24/9/2026 1400 /to 24/9/2026 1600`.
- Dates in the `yyyy-MM-dd` and `d/M/yyyy HHmm` formats are shown in a friendly form (e.g. `Sep 24 2026, 2:00 PM`), while free-form values like `/by tomorrow` are shown as typed.
- Listing all tasks (`list`) and finding tasks by keyword (`find book`; matching ignores case and partial words work).
- Marking and unmarking tasks as done (`mark 1`, `unmark 1`).
- Updating a task in place (`update 1 deadline read book /by 2026-09-30`); it should keep its position and done status.
- Deleting tasks (`delete 2`), with the remaining tasks renumbered.
- Duplicate tasks are rejected.
- Tasks are saved to `data/quackie.txt` in the same folder as the JAR and load correctly after restarting Quackie.
- Invalid commands, dates (e.g. `2026-02-30`), task numbers (e.g. `mark 0`, `mark 999`), missing inputs (e.g. `todo`, `deadline report`), and events that end before they start show a helpful error message without crashing.
- The GUI displays messages correctly, long messages wrap, the window resizes sensibly, and the chat scrolls to the newest message.
- `bye` shows a goodbye message and disables the input box and **Send** button; the window can then be closed normally.

Guide for testers:
* Post screenshots from your smoke tests
* Mention which OS you used for testing


</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/206#issuecomment-5664552492" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Maybe the page can make the Ubuntu 22.04+ assumption more explicit, but I am not sure an OS upgrade should be presented as the default fix just to install Java. The course requires Java/JDK 25, using OpenJDK or an alternative, and points students to the SE-EDU installation guides: https://nus-cs2103-ay2627-s1.github.io/website/admin/programmingLanguages.html

For older Ubuntu releases, a note that their default repositories may not include `openjdk-25-jdk`, with a link to an alternative JDK-installation path, seems less disruptive than recommending a distribution upgrade.
</panel>

