<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/165#issuecomment-5698773035" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**8 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Your `stage.close()` version is valid when the goal is to close that particular window. With JavaFX's default implicit-exit setting, closing the last window will normally also end the app, so a single-window app may appear to behave the same.

`Platform.exit()` explicitly terminates the JavaFX application, which makes it the clearer match for a `bye` command, especially if the app later has another window or changes the implicit-exit setting. The OpenJFX lifecycle docs describe `Platform.exit()` as the preferred way to explicitly terminate an application: https://openjfx.io/javadoc/23/javafx.graphics/javafx/application/Application.html

Since the `PauseTransition` callback runs on the JavaFX Application Thread, either approach is fine here. I would use `Platform.exit()` for “quit the app”, and `stage.close()` when only that stage should close.
</panel>

