<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/510#issuecomment-5750653020" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**10 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Since the repository is in WSL, I would also verify that IntelliJ opened the project from WSL's Linux filesystem, rather than through a Windows-mounted path. JetBrains’ WSL guide uses the WSL network path for projects stored under a user's Linux home directory and notes that the project should use a JDK located in WSL.

In Settings → Build, Execution, Deployment → Build Tools → Gradle, check the Gradle JVM too: it can differ from the project SDK if Gradle settings or `org.gradle.java.home` overrides it. This gives a concrete comparison with the Gradle command that works.

If those match and the Remote Execution Agent plugin is enabled, the Build output from a Debug attempt and the breakpoint location would help narrow it down, as Prof Damith asked.
</panel>

