* Which OS'es to test on: Windows | Linux (already smoke-tested on Mac myself)                                                                                                
* Things to check specifically:                                                                                                                                               
    - Copy the jar into an empty folder and launch it from a terminal with `java -jar echo.jar` (not double-click) — should open a GUI window titled "Echo" with no errors.     
    - Try a few commands: `todo read book`, `deadline return book /by 2019-10-15`, `event meeting /from 2019-10-15 /to 2019-10-16`, `list`, `mark 1`, `find book`.              
    - Try an invalid command (e.g. `mark 99`) — should show a friendly error, not crash.                                                                                        
    - Close and reopen the app — your tasks from before should still be there (check that a `data` folder appeared next to the jar).                                            
                          

Guide for testers:
* Post screenshots from your smoke tests
* Mention which OS you used for testing

Thank you!

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/393" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2. :fas-info-circle: Does an already-implemented AB3 feature (e.g. add, delete) count toward our MVP automatically?**
</div>

Related to #196 and #201, but a slightly different angle: our tP inherits AB3's codebase, so features like `add` and `delete` are already fully implemented (command word, parser, execute logic) even though we haven't touched them for our own product direction yet.

Our question: does a feature being "already set up" from AB3 make it count toward our MVP by default, or should inclusion be decided purely by whether our target users need it - same as if we were building it from scratch?

E.g. if `delete` isn't essential for our specific target users' workflow, are we expected/allowed to treat it as out-of-scope for the MVP feature spec even though the code already exists and works? Or does "already implemented and working" mean it should stay in scope by default since removing it would mean extra work (hiding/removing a working command) rather than saving work?

Want to confirm before we finalize our feature spec due Sun Sep 20th. Thank you!
</panel>

