<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/165#issuecomment-5579528433" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I tried something similar, but used a `Timeline` because I wanted a couple of steps in the closing sequence.

After `bye`, my GUI shows the farewell message and disables the input field and Send button immediately. It then shows a closing notice after three seconds and exits five seconds later:

'''java
Timeline closingTimeline = new Timeline(
        new KeyFrame(Duration.seconds(3),
                event -&gt; showClosingNotice()),
        new KeyFrame(Duration.seconds(8),
                event -&gt; Platform.exit()));
closingTimeline.play();
'''

I found that `PauseTransition` works nicely for a single delayed exit, while `Timeline` is better if you want to show intermediate messages or create a countdown. Both approaches avoid blocking the JavaFX Application Thread and give the farewell message time to appear..

Hope this is useful to you and others as well!
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/168#issuecomment-5586772750" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**5 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I realised **git bisect** was already covered in the lecture 🤣 so am reposting to explain what I found out regarding combining it with a JUnit test and a coding agent to automate the debugging process.

Similar to the standard use of git bisect (which was explained in the lecture briefly), basically you first choose a test that passes in an older working commit but fails in the current commit. Then run:

'''bash
git bisect start
git bisect bad HEAD
git bisect good &lt;known-good-commit>
git bisect run ./gradlew test --tests "package.ClassName.testMethod"
'''

Git will check out each commit and run the test. A passing test marks the commit as good, while a failing test marks it as bad. It repeats this until it finds the first bad commit.

You can then ask your coding agent to inspect that commit’s diff and explain what likely caused the regression.

Then finally return back to your original checkout:

'''bash
git bisect reset
'''

This works best when the same JUnit test already exists throughout the range of commits being checked.

Link for more info on git bisect: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-bisect
</panel>

