<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/175#issuecomment-5600670466" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**6 :fas-comment:**
</div>

ooo this is interesting!

personally I will consider these as major bugs
- Unexpected crashes
- Failed CI/JUnit tests
- Data loss
- Blatantly wrong output (e.g. marking a task deletes it instead)
- Not following iP requirements

But minor "bugs" like the `list 67` example arguably aren't even bugs but are closer to a feature XD It might sound funny, but often times minor bugs actually comes down to GUI/UX design choices rather than incompetence

You might want to be more lenient for users who are not used to typing commands, or you might want the program to exit after 3 wrong inputs if it is storing sensitive information. It really depends on your target audience and value proposition too.

So I will say the real dividing line is not "does it handle every possible input" but "does it ever fail silently or lose the user's data/trust." Everything past that is a design decision, not a defect, and it is worth making that decision deliberately rather than trying to handle every edge case just to be safe.
</panel>

