<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/138#issuecomment-5537220457" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I agree with the distinction everyone has made. The main issue seems to be that “whole–part relationship” can be used in different ways. I think this is mainly a difference in terminology rather than a disagreement about how aggregation and composition behave. For exams, I will follow the textbook’s terminology, but I guess it is still useful to know that external UML sources may classify aggregation as a weaker form of whole–part relationship.

I found [this paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.07223) useful because it explains that UML literature does not always use the terms “whole–part,” “aggregation,” “composition,” and “containment” consistently. This seems to explain the difference between the broader interpretation that aggregation is a weaker whole–part relationship and our course’s narrower distinction between composition and container–containee relationships
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/157#issuecomment-5558680715" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think the key distinction is between how the task's status is represented and how it is exposed to callers. If a task has only two stable states and those states do not affect its behaviour, then a boolean is a simple and reasonable internal representation to me.

I wouldn't introduce an enum just to avoid writing true or false, but I think it becomes worthwhile if different states require different rules rather than just for determining how the task is displayed.

This doesn't resolve the readability concern, but ultimately, it is a trade-off between keeping the design simple and making the code more self-documenting.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/166#issuecomment-5584120303" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

@Krtin888 I agree with your suggestion to apply the smallest refactoring that addresses the specific problem. 

I think the benefits stop justifying the cost when even this small change does not make the code noticeably clearer or easier to maintain. If the new method requires many parameters, adds unnecessary indirection, or it does not provide a meaningful name, I would probably leave the original code unchanged.

My approach would therefore be to identify the problem beforehand, make the smallest change that might solve it, check whether the resulting code actually provides the expected benefit, then possibly discard it if it only adds complexity without making the code clearer, easier to test, or easier to modify.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/212#issuecomment-5675985979" expanded>
