<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/157#issuecomment-5560369300" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think this thread has already covered most of the readability angle really well, but I wanted to add a couple of things.

I think what's being described here already has a name, it's sometimes called a "flag argument," and Clean Code calls it out specifically: a constructor or method that branches on a boolean is quietly doing two different things, and which one you get is hidden in the parameter list rather than the call site. I think that gives a nice way to frame the "it depends" a few people have landed on — a boolean is usually fine as a field, but becomes more of a problem the moment it shows up as a bare literal being passed in.

Building on @ananyakharbanda's new Task("read book", false, true, false) example, I'd say that's actually more than a readability issue. Since all three parameters are the same type, the compiler won't catch it if two of them get swapped by accident, so it's a genuine correctness risk and not just something that's hard to read. That feels like a stronger reason to move to a builder or named factories once you're past one boolean parameter.

One small thing on the "assign it to a named variable first" fix a few people suggested; I think it's a nice lightweight solution, but it mostly helps in an IDE, since something like IntelliJ will still show the isDone: hint next to a literal. That hint's gone once the code shows up in a diff or PR review though, which is probably how most of us actually read each other's code in this module.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/153#issuecomment-5560461262" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Really enjoying this thread! @zikiai's test of asking whether something would survive swapping out JavaFX for another interface is such a good rule of thumb, stealing that one for myself.

Here's a case that had me going back and forth for a bit: the `bye`/exit command. It's a fun one because *deciding* the session should end is obviously domain logic, but *actually closing the window* can only happen in the UI layer, through something like `Platform.exit()`. Turns out AB3 already has a tidy way of handling this. `execute()` just returns a result object with the feedback text plus a small flag on it, something like `isExit()`. The controller checks that flag and takes care of the actual shutdown, but it never needs to know *why* the session's ending, only *that* it is. Same trick would carry over nicely if you ever add a help window down the line.

Small example, but a nice gut-check for whether the "UI vs domain" split really holds up once a command needs to touch something the domain layer just can't reach on its own.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/168#issuecomment-5644644156" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

These are all really helpful. I wanted to add one more workflow I've found useful, especially for the iP/tP context:

Using AI as a codebase navigator for understanding existing code

When working with larger codebases (especially tP where you inherit code or refactor heavily), I've found it helpful to:

Share a class or module with the AI and ask: "What does this do at a high level? What are the key responsibilities and dependencies?"
Then ask follow-up questions like: "How does this interact with X class?" or "What would break if I changed this method signature?"
Before making changes, I ask the AI to trace through the call chain for a specific user action

This is different from asking it to generate code, I'm using it to understand the existing design, which feels like a good middle ground. It's especially useful when:

Jumping into unfamiliar code
Figuring out ripple effects before refactoring
Understanding design patterns used in the existing codebase

Of course, I still read the actual code and run tests to verify, but this helps me ask smarter questions and plan changes more carefully.

Also +1 to @spencercdz's point about treating AI answers as claims that need evidence—this is particularly important in a course context where we need to learn the material ourselves rather than just getting things done.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/219#issuecomment-5682876536" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**5 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Really appreciate everyone's answers here, they've been genuinely helpful to read through! 🙏 One small thought I had, if it's useful; it might help to think of this as two related but slightly separate questions:

1. When do errors share a branch vs. get separate ones?
Building on @advaitio's point, maybe it's about whether the call sequence differs, not the reason for failure. In the OP's example, "invalid command format" and "invalid field" likely both fail during parsing, before a command object exists, so they could share one branch (alt [input is invalid]). "Duplicate entry" is different, since it's only caught once execution reaches the Model — that extra distance travelled probably deserves its own branch.

2. When does a branch deserve a ref frame or separate diagram?
This feels like a different question, less about how different the failure is, more about how much detail it brings. A short duplicate check (an internal lookup) is probably fine inline. @yxlyx's ref idea seems most useful once a branch's own interaction is rich enough to compete with the main flow for attention.

Rough guideline: merge branches with the same call path → keep different call paths as separate alts → only split into a ref/diagram once that branch's detail really warrants it. Just my thoughts, happy to hear other views! 😊
</panel>

