<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/154#issuecomment-5557328348" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**7 :fas-comment:**
</div>

For me, I think extensibility is important, but it should not come at the cost of unnecessary complexity. For iP, I would prefer to first design around the current requirements and keep each component’s responsibility clear, rather than introducing abstractions for features that may never be needed. Only when adding a new feature starts causing duplicated logic or changes across many unrelated parts of the program, then I will think about that may be a good sign that an additional abstraction is useful. In that sense, I think abstractions are most valuable when they solve an actual design problem rather than a hypothetical future one.
So personally, I would aim for a simple and modular design first, and refactor when repeated changes reveal a clear need for further abstraction. Abstraction is not the goal. The goal is to make changes simpler.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/553#issuecomment-5889632886" expanded>
<div slot="header">

