<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/542" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1. :fas-info-circle: Week 7 - System failures (and etc) usefulness in use case diagrams?**
</div>

When reading the notes, I came across this example:

<img width="571" height="267" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/100560da-0035-46ad-903d-9e940f218897" />

In the above image, it is stated under preconditions that:
"Preconditions: All hardware in working order."

However, wouldn't the above be considered an not useful? Since if the hardware isn't working then none of the use cases would work (and hence, we would have to write working hardware for all use cases?).

I have this perception because earlier when talking about extensions, it was noted that:
"Also note that it is not useful to mention events such as power failures or system crashes as extensions because the system cannot function beyond such catastrophic failures."

Why would we consider "working hardware" useful as a preconditon, yet we don't allow "power failures or system crashes" to be useful when mentioned as extensions.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/483" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2. :fas-info-circle: tP feature specification for non-CLI commands**
</div>

For the tP feature specification, must every feature in the feature list correspond to a command that the user explicitly enters through the CLI?

ie, commands like `/add task` and `/delete task`  are commands that users have to type into the CLI in order to run.

However, should features that do not require user's to run be considered in the feature list?

For example, a feature to auto-save task details whenever a command like `/add`, `/delete`, `/update` etc is run
An example of how the code may run could be:
-> User adds a task, task1 (runs `/add ` task1)
-> `/add` command runs internally (called by user)
-> `autoSave` command runs internally (is not called by user, but runs since it is responding to `/add`)

If so, for a feature that is never directly invoked by the user, should we still provide a command format,
</panel>

