<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/177" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4. :fas-info-circle: How to differentiate when to use SLAP vs Inline Function refactoring?**
</div>

I am having some issues understanding when to use Single Level of Abstraction Principle (SLAP).

It states that each function should "avoid having multiple levels of abstraction within a code fragment".

However, for this code example:

```
function getRating(driver) ``{``
  return moreThanFiveLateDeliveries(driver) ? 2 : 1;
``}``

function moreThanFiveLateDeliveries(driver) ``{``
  return driver.numberOfLateDeliveries > 5;
``}``
```

Wouldn't the above be ideal since we abstract the conditional for late deliveries? Hence, we are removing the low level details from `getRating` and passing it on to a helper function `moreThanFiveLateDeliveries`.

However, under "Inline Function" in refactoring, it is recommended to decompose the function to:
```
function getRating(driver) ``{``
  return (driver.numberOfLateDeliveries > 5) ? 2 : 1;
``}``
```

Where we expose the condition to `getRating`. 

I am confused since it seems contradictory to expose the conditional criteria to `getRating` when under SLAP it would be in a different abstraction layer?

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/64" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**5. :fas-info-circle: iP Level 8 - LocalDate for Event?**
</div>

Hi, 
I wanted to check if there is a requirement to change Event's date to a LocalDate object as well? 
It was not specified under the task but it was hinted at under Stretch Goal.

"
Minimal: Store deadline dates as a java.time.LocalDate (or java.time.LocalDateTime) in your task objects. Accept dates in a format such as yyyy-mm-dd (e.g., 2019-10-15) and print in a different format such as MMM dd yyyy (e.g., Oct 15 2019).
Stretch goal: Use dates and times in more meaningful ways, e.g., add a command to print deadlines/events occurring on a specific date.
"
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/157#issuecomment-5557911138" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**6 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I experienced a similar issue regarding the readability for the constructors.

I think in this case, an alternative I had thought of was to do:
```
public Task(String description) ``{``
    this.description = description;
    this.isDone = false;
``}``
```
My rationale was that, if the user had a task to do, why would they fill in a task that is already complete. Therefore it may not be necessary for the caller to explicitly pass `false`.

However, I realised that there may still be cases where we need to construct a task that is already completed, such as when loading previously saved tasks.

I think your factory methods of `complete` and `incomplete` would be a more cohesive way of doing this. It clearly signals to the caller of the state of the task.
</panel>

