<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/165#issuecomment-5578206399" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Nice tip, thanks for sharing! I tried this approach in my GUI. One small UX improvement is to disable the input controls immediately after `bye`, while the delay is running:

'''java
if (command.equals("bye")) ``{``
    sendButton.setDisable(true);
    userInput.setDisable(true);

    PauseTransition delay =
            new PauseTransition(Duration.seconds(1));
    delay.setOnFinished(event -&gt; Platform.exit());
    delay.play();
``}``
'''

This allows the farewell message to render without blocking the thread, while also preventing another command from being submitted during the one-second delay.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/168#issuecomment-5585732671" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**5 :fas-comment:**
</div>

If you're interested in experimenting with harnesses other than the usual Codex or Claude Code, there's quite a few open-source options. I've personally tried Hermes, DeepSeek Harness, and Zed. Hermes is a self-evolving harness, DeepSeek Harness makes everything a plug-in, and Zed is lightweight. 

My current set-up uses Zed and I connect it to my ChatGPT Plus subscription (you can also use the OpenRouter free models API, but the rate-limit is quite bad for more popular models like GLM). I feel like it uses less tokens to complete the same task as compared to Codex but YMMV. If you prefer CLI for prompting, then Pi Agent is another solid option (I prefer GUIs though😆).
</panel>

