<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/262#issuecomment-5696437000" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think in this case, extracting out the command handlers is just objectively better. I also had a similar else if and for each command there was a chunk of code that handled the logic of the command which is just extremely unpleasant to look at when you consider how many commands there are and how some of them are not just simple one liners. 

What I did was to create `handleCommand()` methods for each command like `handleToDo()` and `handleMark()` so each else if was a simple one liner and my `Kevin` class (the chatbot itself) implemented each of the commands. It isn't much more complexity as I'm just copy pasting the code and transferring it to another method, and it definitely makes the else if code so much cleaner and readable. 

And if I ever needed to know or adjust the implementation of each of the commands, I can simply go to that method and edit it rather than having to read through my long else if chain and find which one it is. Improves the quality of the code a lot by some simple copy pasting and it makes maintaining the code much more pleasant as well.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/252#issuecomment-5696544438" expanded>
