<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/207#issuecomment-5686820110" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think the level of detail should depend on the scenario and the purpose of the diagram. For an `add` command, I would include the main interactions that explain the outcome, such as the relevant Parser, Logic, Model, and Storage calls, but not every internal helper method.

A helper call is worth showing if omitting it would make an important decision, validation, or state change unclear. Otherwise, leaving it out keeps the diagram focused rather than turning it into a full execution trace. If a detailed interaction is still relevant but would clutter the main diagram, a `ref` frame could be used.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/484#issuecomment-5740741875" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

All Java classes implicitly extend `Object`, so `Remark` inherits `hashCode()` from `Object`. The `@Override` indicates that this implementation replaces the inherited one, using `value.hashCode()` so two `Remark` objects with the same value can produce the same hash code. If `equals()` is also overridden, it should use the same value-based equality logic.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/450#issuecomment-5748433976" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Your interpretation is reasonable. `getInfo()` is a callback only if `ParserFactory` supplies it to `Parser` (for example, as a method reference/function) and `Parser` invokes it later. If `Parser` directly calls a public method on a `ParserFactory` reference, it is an ordinary method call, not a callback. The diagram should therefore show the callback being passed or registered—e.g., `Parser(getInfo)`—if that is the intended design; otherwise, the relationship should be labelled as a normal dependency.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/542#issuecomment-5790893090" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**5 :fas-comment:**
</div>

“Working hardware” can be a valid precondition because it states an assumption required before the use case can begin. Since it is presumably common to all use cases, it need not be repeated in every use case; it may be documented as a system-wide assumption instead. Whether it is worth including depends on the diagram’s scope and the level of detail intended.

A power failure or system crash is different when listed as an extension: an extension describes an alternative flow that the system can detect and respond to while executing the use case. After a complete power failure or crash, the system cannot continue that flow or perform a meaningful response, so it is outside the use case’s behaviour. Hardware problems that the system can detect and handle—for example, a printer running out of paper—could still be documented as an extension.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/548#issuecomment-5864252477" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**6 :fas-comment:**
</div>

This may be due to a recent GitHub UI change rather than a repository misconfiguration. Your workaround through “New issue” should be fine. If the option is missing for everyone, check that your repository role still includes permission to manage labels; otherwise, a repository admin may need to create them or verify the team’s access.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/76#issuecomment-5864254860" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**7 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I’d generally prefer composition: keep a private `List&lt;Task>` and expose only the operations that `TaskList` actually needs. Although a `TaskList` contains tasks, it may not conceptually be an arbitrary `List`, and inheriting from `ArrayList` tightly couples it to that implementation while exposing methods that could violate its invariants. The extra delegation code is usually a worthwhile trade-off for encapsulation. If the project explicitly requires full `List` behaviour, implementing `List&lt;Task>` or extending an appropriate abstraction could be considered, but I would avoid `extends ArrayList&lt;Task>` just to reuse basic methods.
</panel>

</panel>


