# Murphy Smoke Testing

## Links

- JAR file: [Murphy](https://github.com/aanya007/ip/releases/download/v0.2/murphy.jar)
- User Guide: [Murphy User Guide](https://aanya007.github.io/ip/)

## Operating Systems

Please test Murphy on:

- Windows
- macOS
- Linux

## Setup Instructions

1. Download the [Murphy JAR file](RELEASE_LINK_HERE).
2. Create a new, empty folder.
3. Place `murphy.jar` in the folder.
4. Open a terminal in that folder.
5. Confirm that Java 25 is installed
6. Launch Murphy

## Things to Check
Please check whether:
- Murphy launches successfully without unexpected errors.
- The main GUI appears correctly.
- The commands in the User Guide work as expected.
- The application remains responsive after several commands.
- Invalid commands produce understandable error messages.
- The application does not crash.
- The window can be resized without important controls being cropped.
- The task count and task list update correctly.
- Tasks remain saved after closing and reopening the application.

## Suggested Commands
Please try some of the following commands:
list
todo buy groceries
deadline submit report /by 2026-09-20
event team meeting /from 2pm /to 4pm
find buy
on 2026-09-20
mark 1
unmark 1
delete 1
invalidcommand
Please also try restarting Murphy and checking whether the tasks are still present.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/344" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4. :fas-info-circle: Which errors should be logged in addition to being shown to the user?**
</div>


Question in title. Week 6 describes logging as useful for troubleshooting, but I am unsure whether every invalid user command should be logged.

For example, should a normal invalid command be logged as a warning, or should logging be reserved for unexpected failures such as corrupted data or an unhandled exception?

How should we balance useful diagnostic information against noisy log files?
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/343" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**5. :fas-info-circle: How do we distinguish a component from an ordinary class in an architecture diagram?**
</div>


Question in title. My understanding is that an architecture diagram should show major components such as UI, Logic, Model, and Storage, while leaving out most internal classes.

However, if one class is responsible for an important system-wide decision, could it be shown as an architectural component, or should it still remain part of a larger component?

Is there any rule of thumb should we use when deciding the boundary?
</panel>

