### 114. HEIN..HTET `@Henry336` (7 posts) 
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/88" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1. :fas-info-circle: Changing code from Levels 1-6 to match Coding Standards**
</div>

Hi,
In the iP guidelines, it is stated that future commits to the iP starting from this week should abide by the coding standards, and that past commits needn't be modified. 

Do we still need to change the code we already wrote for Levels 1-6 to have it follow the coding standards or is it just for Level 7 onwards? Thanks.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/152#issuecomment-5550854020" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think another way to look at it is whether the literal is obvious from where it is used rather than just whether it appears once. For example, after reading this thread and looking back on my iP, I realized I currently have these lines:

<img width="366" height="90" alt="Minimum JavaFX window dimensions in Main.java" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/3b4e1e7d-a32c-4b6d-8eeb-0a6c061c3ed3" />

Even though each number only appears once, I think naming them `MIN_WINDOW_HEIGHT` and `MIN_WINDOW_WIDTH` would make their purpose clearer because `480` and `420` don't really explain what they represent by themselves.

So maybe my rule of thumb would be to name it if a reader has to stop and wonder what the value might mean, or if several uses need to change together. 
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/152#issuecomment-5552689283" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

@less413 I think you made a fair point there actually. I was looking at the numbers by themselves but I suppose the method calls already tell us what they're supposed to represent. In this case, naming them might not improve clarity so much.

For `ZERO` and `ONE`, I understand the typo concern, @ykteo000 , but I'm not sure if naming them would help much. In the context of indexing, I feel like `0` and `1` are already conventional, and `ZERO` and `ONE` would only restate their values without explaining their purpose. I think tests or code reviews would be better at catching something like accidentally typing `11` instead of `1`
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/154#issuecomment-5553182196" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Looking back at my own iP, adding `find` from `Level-9` had me changing a few different classes including the main controller and the UI class. But each change was quite small and related to that class's responsibility so I don't think touching several files necessarily means the design is difficult to extend. 

For commands, however, I'm currently using a `CommandType` enum + switch statements, so adding a new command requires some explicit modifications each time. I think I could probably create a `Command` interface and separate command classes... but right now that feels less like a benefit to me. I'd maybe abstract further only when the current approach starts causing duplication or messy dependencies in future increments!
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/166#issuecomment-5580997559" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**5 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think the "cost" here is not just the time spent refactoring, but also any extra indirection it leaves behind. Looking back at my iP, I extracted a long condition into this:

'''java
if (shouldShowDivider(command)) ``{``
    this.ui.showMessageWithLines(response);
``}`` else ``{``
    this.ui.showMessage(response);
``}``
'''

The helper method still contains all the individual command checks, so extracting it adds another method that a reader may have to navigate to. However, at the call site, I can immediately understand why the branch exists without reading through every condition. To me, the benefit is worth the extra indirection here because the method name adds meaning.

On the other hand, if a helper's name tells me nothing beyond the single line inside it, I would probably inline it because jumping to another method would make the code harder to follow. 

So I don't think extract and inline necessarily have opposite goals. Both are trying to make the code easier to understand or change. Just my two cents, but I'd compare the clarity gained from the refactoring against the extra indirection and risk of changing working code, then choose whichever version takes less effort to follow. 
</panel>

