<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/76#issuecomment-5447674080" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I ran into this choice too and ended up using composition. My TaskList keeps a private ArrayList&lt;Task> and only exposes the operations my app needs. Inheritance seemed reasonable at first because a task list sounds like a list of tasks. However, extending ArrayList would also expose methods like clear, set, and addAll. With composition, I can control what other classes are allowed to do and add validation later if needed. Inheritance can work for the current IP, but composition made more sense for my implementation.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/119#issuecomment-5489823512" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I believe that it only applies to the observable state, not temporary states. meaning that if the move is treated as a singular operation, and as long as mid move the task is not able to be accessed, it is not a violation.

This also means that when performing the move, errors need to be taken into account. For example, your program's `move` function calls `remove(task)` followed by `add(task)`, but then `add(task)` fails and the program does not revert the task back to belonging to its original TaskList. In this case, multiplicity is violated.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/166#issuecomment-5615647773" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**5 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Adding on to the points about readability, another benefit to consider is whether a refactoring makes a known upcoming change easier. For example, suppose a task-management application initially handles input, parsing, task operations, and output in one large class. Separating these responsibilities into components such as Parser, Ui, and TaskList introduces an immediate cost: there are more files and methods to navigate, even though the program’s behaviour remains unchanged. 

However, this separation may become valuable when replacing a command-line interface with a GUI. The existing command-processing logic can be reused without untangling it  > from console-specific code at the same time. Therefore, I would consider whether there is a concrete upcoming change, recurring maintenance difficulty, or responsibility that is already becoming tangled. If none exists, the benefit may be too speculative to justify the added complexity. If a likely change would be significantly easier after the refactoring, the upfront cost may be worthwhile even if there is little immediate improvement.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/238#issuecomment-5715124941" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**6 :fas-comment:**
</div>

