All produced the expected results:

1. `list` on an empty list — "Your list is empty. Add a task to get started!"
2. `todo read book` — `[T][ ] read book`, 1 task
3. `deadline return book /by 2026-09-20` — `[D][ ] return book (by: Sep 20 2026)`, 2 tasks
4. `event project meeting /from 2026-10-01 /to 2026-10-02` — `[E][ ] project meeting (from: Oct 01 2026 to: Oct 02 2026)`, 3 tasks
5. `list` — all three in order with the right icons
6. `mark 2` then `unmark 2` — status toggled to `[X]` and back to `[ ]`
7. `find book` and `find BOOK` — both returned `read book` and `return book`, so the search is case-insensitive as documented
8. `edit 2 by 2026-09-25` — `[D][ ] return book (by: Sep 25 2026)`
9. `edit 1 description read two books` — `[T][ ] read two books`
10. `hello` — "Invalid command :( If you need help, type 'help'." / `deadline submit report /by tomorrow` — "Please enter the deadline date as yyyy-MM-dd." Both errors were highlighted, Bob did not crash, and it kept accepting commands
11. `delete 3` — deletion confirmed, 2 tasks remaining; `list` showed `read two books` and `return book (by: Sep 25 2026)`
12. Closed Bob with the window controls and restarted — `list` showed the same two tasks, loaded from `data/bob.txt`:

```
T | 0 | read two books
D | 0 | return book | 2026-09-25
```

## Bugs

**1. `bye` does not close the application.** Bob prints the goodbye message but the window stays open and keeps accepting commands normally. I had to close it with the window controls.

**2. Expected output in the test doc does not match actual output.** Test case 3 expects `(by: Sept 20 2026)`, while case 5 lists the same task as `(by: Sep 20 2026)`. Bob outputs `Sep`, so case 3 reads as a failure. Perhaps need to align the doc with the actual format.

