<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/262#issuecomment-5694366073" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I don't think the main issue is necessarily that `getResponse()` is too long. From my understanding, SLAP is about keeping operations at a consistent level of abstraction, rather than the length itself. Although "extracting each branch into another method" could improve SLAP by making the logic more high level but it wouldn't address a separate issue, where `getResponse()` is handling too many responsibility. Right now, `getResponse()` would still be responsible for both determining which command was requested and knowing that every command exists.

This is why for me, I think a `Command` abstraction is worth considering. Instead of having `getResponse()` determine the command type itself, I separated each operation into a `Command` class, such as `MarkCommand`, `DeleteCommand` and `FindCommand`. Then, i made a parser responsible for translating the user input into the appropriate `Command` object. 

With this, `getResponse()` does not need to distinguish between the different commands but just know that it receives a Command that can be executed. Then, the `Parser` owns the knowledge of how user input maps to command, while each `Command` class owns its command specific behaviour.

</panel>

