<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/127#issuecomment-5537222670" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I agree that Java doesn't enforce UML multiplicity automatically. Beyond making fields `final` and adding defensive checks in constructors and setters, using annotations like `@NonNull` can also help catch potential null assignments during static analysis or build-time, bridging the gap between the design model and the code.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/177#issuecomment-5630613303" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

It definitely is hard choice to make especially when there is so many clean code principles out there and which to choose , I would say ultimately comes down to personal preference && also what the course recommends.

Reading through everyone's points, a good way to look at it is the difference between hiding a business logic versus just wrapping a primitive operation.

Inline when the helper method basically reads like plain English translation of the code itself (like moreThanFiveLateDeliveries), especially if it forces a magic literal into the name. Perhaps it helps less in raising the abstraction level but rather just makes it more tedious.

Extract/Keep as SLAP when the helper encapsulates a meaningful domain concept or business logic (like isFrequentlyLate), hiding low-level details or thresholds so the caller only cares what rule is being checked, not how it's calculated, which actually makes sense based on what we were taught in 2030, that eg a customer should not need to know how the queue in the bank works, they just enter the queue and the bank manages the queue(if i remember correctly!)

In short, SLAP is about protecting the reader from getting overwhelmed in low-level implementation details, whereas the Inline refactoring targets helpers that add noise without actually providing a higher-level abstraction.
</panel>

