### 163. KIEF.. LAU `@keyfurrr67` (5 posts) 
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/142" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1. :fas-info-circle: Is it a must to capitalize the word following @param**
</div>

Just wondering if after writing @param in the javadoc comment that the following word after that has to be capitalise. What does the coding standard say about this.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/138#issuecomment-5536272663" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think ChatGPT and othee sources call both "whole-part" because the official UML standard does treat aggregation as a loose whole-part relationship.

Our textbook uses container-containee instead just to make the boundary much clearer and avoid confusion

Take for example,
Composition is like a car and its steering wheel. The wheel is a direct part of the car, and its lifecycle depends on the car.
But aggregation is like a parking lot and a car. The lot holds the car, but the car isn't a "part" of the lot. If the lot closes down, the car still exists.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/165#issuecomment-5630274783" expanded>
