<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/252" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2. :fas-info-circle: Method Length**
</div>



Hi, I want to check, I know there is no explicit 'maximum length' for our methods within the iP. Thus, if we have a longer method with around 40ish lines of code, do we need to write a justification somewhere for why we chose to have a lengthier method? 
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/266#issuecomment-5707880931" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think the response could depend on whether the corrupted entry is independent of the rest of the file.

If each line represents a self-contained task, one malformed line does not necessarily make the other 19 unreliable, so partial recovery seems reasonable. However, if the corruption affects shared metadata, task relationships, or the file structure itself, continuing to load could produce an inconsistent application state. In that case, stopping the load may be safer.

One possible policy would therefore be:
- Recover valid entries when the error is isolated to particular records.
- Reject the entire load when the file’s overall structure or consistency cannot be trusted.
- In either case, report the problem clearly rather than failing or discarding data silently.

This makes the behaviour depend on whether data integrity can still be guaranteed, rather than applying the same recovery strategy to every type of corruption.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/218#issuecomment-5707940687" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Another criterion might be whether the log entry would lead a maintainer to take any action. If p/abc is rejected exactly as designed, recording every occurrence probably provides little diagnostic value, even at FINE, unless we are actively investigating the validation flow.

However, the same event could become relevant when viewed as a pattern. For example, a large number of rejected phone numbers might suggest that the accepted format is unclear, while repeated suspicious inputs could have security relevance. In those cases, it may be better to record an aggregate count rather than logging every raw command individually.

Therefore, I would first ask what decision the recorded information is meant to support. If there is no realistic debugging, security, or operational use for it, not logging the event may be better than assigning it a level simply because it occurred.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/297#issuecomment-5708010728" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**5 :fas-comment:**
</div>

One additional consideration is where the option is configured. --enable-native-access=javafx.graphics is a JVM launch option, so adding it to a Gradle run task would only affect launches through Gradle. Someone smoke-testing the packaged JAR with java -jar would still see the warning unless the option is included in the documented launch command or launcher script.

It may therefore be useful to configure the option for development runs while also updating the User Guide with the correct command for running the packaged JAR. This would make the expected setup clear and ensure that users and testers launch the application consistently.
</panel>

</panel>


