### 195. ZHU ..IHAN `@zRayhhh` (4 posts) 
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/102" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1. :fas-info-circle: 💡Ensuring Gradle project prints UTF-8 characters in terminal properly**
</div>

This is a fix for a bit of a niche issue that I ran into, regarding the terminal rendering of an ASCII banner made out of UTF-8 characters.
```
String banner = """
                █   █  █████  █      █      ███
                █   █  █      █      █     █   █
                █████  █████  █      █     █   █
                █   █  █      █      █     █   █
                █   █  █      █      █     █   █
                █   █  █████  █████  █████  ███
                """;
System.out.println(banner);
```
With ./gradlew run, this banner gets translated into a bunch of question marks when it appears in the IntelliJ terminal:
```
PS C:\Users\RH\work\2103\ip> chcp
Active code page: 437
PS C:\Users\RH\work\2103\ip> ./gradlew run

> Task :run
?   ?  ?????  ?      ?      ???
?   ?  ?      ?      ?     ?   ?
?????  ?????  ?      ?     ?   ?
?   ?  ?      ?      ?     ?   ?
?   ?  ?      ?      ?     ?   ?
?   ?  ?????  ?????  ?????  ???
```
Presumably the Gradle daemon is using some other JVM with different encoding as code page 437 should be able to display the character "█" without any issue, although by my impression the encoding used by Gradle should match that of the OS (if there is anyone who can enlighten me on how this happens please do).

Anyway, here are some possible fixes to this: 

```
System.setOut(new PrintStream(System.out, true, StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
```
This forces System.out to print in UTF-8 which somehow resolves the issue.

OR

```
tasks.named('run', JavaExec) ``{``
    systemProperty 'file.encoding', 'UTF-8'
    systemProperty 'stdout.encoding', 'UTF-8'
    systemProperty 'stderr.encoding', 'UTF-8'
``}``
```
Having this chunk in build.gradle is another way to force stdout to UTF-8.

For reference, running the .jar of the same simple print in cmd.exe yields this:
```
C:\Users\RH\work\test>chcp
Active code page: 437

C:\Users\RH\work\test>java -jar "test.jar"
█   █  █████  █      █      ███
█   █  █      █      █     █   █
█████  █████  █      █     █   █
█   █  █      █      █     █   █
█   █  █      █      █     █   █
█   █  █████  █████  █████  ███
```

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/148#issuecomment-5548778393" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Hi there, found a fix for this. This is a purely visual bug (with Scenebuilder 26.0.0, I haven't verified versions before that). 
As far as I know, the bug lies in [InfoPanel.fxml](https://github.com/gluonhq/scenebuilder/blob/master/kit/src/main/resources/com/oracle/javafx/scenebuilder/kit/editor/panel/info/InfoPanel.fxml), which contains the following two lines when setting up each column:
```
<TableColumn fx:id="leftTableColumn" maxWidth="1.7976931348623157E308" minWidth="30.0" prefWidth="-1.0" resizable="true" sortable="true" text="fx:id" />
<TableColumn fx:id="rightTableColumn" maxWidth="1.7976931348623157E308" minWidth="100.0" prefWidth="-1.0" sortable="true" text="%info.label.component" />
```
The `prefWidth="-1.0"` is presumably the default value provided by FXML that is equivalent to `USE_COMPUTED_SIZE`. Presumably whatever happens in the computation results in each column width being clamped to its minWidth. I verified this myself with a local build of Scenebuilder from a clone of their 26.0.0 release on [github](https://github.com/gluonhq/scenebuilder/tree/master) with this small segment to log column sizes:
```
        final TableView<IndexEntry> tableView = leftTableColumn.getTableView();
        tableView.widthProperty().addListener((obs, old, val) ->
        System.out.println("table=" + val + " left=" + leftTableColumn.getWidth() + " right=" + rightTableColumn.getWidth()));
```
This code was inserted into [InfoPanelController.java](https://github.com/gluonhq/scenebuilder/blob/master/kit/src/main/java/com/oracle/javafx/scenebuilder/kit/editor/panel/info/InfoPanelController.java) in `performInitialization()` right after the columns were initialized. The command line output was `table=244.8 left=30.0 right=100.0`, which is exactly the minWidths that was set in InfoPanel.fxml.

If you want to fix this, insert this into InfoPanelController.java on a local build:
```
final TableView<IndexEntry> tableView = leftTableColumn.getTableView();
leftTableColumn.prefWidthProperty().bind(tableView.widthProperty().multiply(0.35));
rightTableColumn.prefWidthProperty().bind(tableView.widthProperty().multiply(0.65).subtract(2));
```
This should sit right after the 4 lines setting `leftTableColumn` and `rightTableColumn` at lines 220+. Then rebuild* Scenebuilder:
*I'm assuming you've cloned Scenebuilder from their github page already instead of using the installer
`./mvnw install -pl kit -am -DskipTests` to rebuild kit;
`./mvnw package -pl app -DskipTests` to rebuild app;
`./mvnw javafx:run -f app` to launch with maven wrapper.

<img width="336" height="530" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/74e94920-ed5b-4380-b8a4-bd9e4fd869c7" />

The table should now look like this, and furthermore can be manually resized by dragging the divider.
Honestly might be worth raising as an issue to Gluon, but it's kinda outside my expertise because I dumped pretty much everything into Claude to try to fix this.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/209#issuecomment-5685507133" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Hi there, going to add my two cents on disposable virtual machines: they work (partially) to emulate OSes, but are best for their security benefits.

The one I tried was Oracle's [VirtualBox](https://www.virtualbox.org/wiki/Downloads). As a Windows user, it works fine for setting up sandboxes for Windows and Linux distros, but macOS is a different problem (see the end of the post). 

**Generally the workflow is as follows (adhering mainly to the principle of least privilege for security):**
1. Create a new virtual machine, at this point default settings for everything are fine, just feed it the ISO* of the OS you're emulating
2. Launch the VM and go through the steps to install the OS (Windows guide below)
3. Sign in and download Java 25, then shut down the VM and save a snapshot
4. Relaunch the VM to download the jar file or anything else online (transferring files from local PC to VM guide also below)
5. Shut down the VM, it's time to change the settings
6. Once powered off, disable anything that is not mandatory in the settings, most importantly:   
   - General > Features: make sure Shared Clipboard & Drag-and-Drop are both disabled   
   - System > Motherboard: **check** UEFI and Secure Boot
   - Network: uncheck "Enable Network Adapter"
   - Shared Folders: make sure all shared folders added earlier if transferring files locally are removed
7. Relaunch the VM with these additional settings and run the jar file
8. Once complete, power off the VM and restore the snapshot to skip the OS setup next time you want to test something potentially malicious (or delete the entire VM to be extra secure)

*You can generally download the ISO online, eg. Microsoft provides Windows installation media

**To transfer local files into the VM:**
1. Before launching the VM, add the location of the local file as a Shared Folder in the settings
2. After launching, in the VM screen, select Devices in the top menu > Insert Guest Additions CD image
3. Find the CD Drive (D:) and run the appropriate VBox additions setup for your OS, eg. `VboxWindowsAdditions.exe` for Windows
4. Navigate directly to the shared folder with `\\VBOXSVR\YOUR_FILE_LOCATION` (that's how it worked on Windows at least)
5. Copy the file into a folder in the VM
6. Go to step 5 in the general workflow and relaunch the VM. Make sure to remove the Shared Folder.

**Windows emulation tips:**
1. Just use Windows 10, it should more or less give the same behaviour just with less hoops to jump through in the installation than Windows 11
2. For Windows 11:
   - It requires at minimum 52GB for the Windows installation, hopefully you have something like 60GB free
   - Use a burner email or something to get through the Microsoft account sign in at first
   - On initial startup, you'll be signed in as administrator. Go to Settings > Accounts > Other users > Add other user > I don't have this person's sign-in information > Add a user without a Microsoft account
       - This will allow you to create a local account that does not need to use Windows Hello to verify the PIN (like really why is this even a thing) (this will save you from the deadlock of wanting to disable network access for safety and Windows refusing to verify your PIN because you have no network)
       - Shut down the VM, change the settings, then relaunch and log into the new user, then continue as per normal
       - Remember to save a state with the new user created already

**General notes**
There's levels to how secure using a VM makes things. For one, it's certainly better than plowing ahead with your machine since it provides an isolated environment, but it's really up to how much time you want to sink into making sure you're safe from malware. For example, you can create a new VM and install an OS every single time you want to run something suspicious, but that's a huge time waste and potentially still open to exploits. The only truly secure way is probably to run it on a VM in a burner laptop or something, but for the purposes of the course, a VM is better than nothing.

There are other options out there for making sandbox environments, but I think VirtualBox is the easiest to start with on any OS and the most available to use (Windows Sandbox is not usable on Windows Home for example). [QEMU](https://www.qemu.org/) is far more powerful in emulating just about any OS and architecture at the expense of being run via CLI, making it the better choice for self-testing on different OSes. If typing long and complicated commands is not a turnoff, by all means go ahead. The main idea of restricting the VM as much as possible before running anything untrustworthy should carry over. 

When it comes to emulating for self-testing the program in different OSes, from a Windows perspective, you don't need something as heavyweight as VirtualBox. WSL (which most Windows users should have) is enough to test in a Linux environment, and macOS is not really recommended to emulate on Windows anyway so you're better off sending it to the forums or friends for testing.

For macOS, as far as I'm aware, it can be _troublesome_(maybe slightly understating) to run macOS on non-Apple hardware, partially because it's probably a legal gray area at best. I didn't try this myself, but you can get some of the ISOs from the [Internet Archive](https://archive.org/details/macos_iso) or torrent it from this [Github page](https://github.com/Pyenb/macOS-ISOs) if you really want to give it a go.
</panel>

