<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/343#issuecomment-5709926760" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

A useful rule is to choose component boundaries by responsibility, interface, and dependencies, not by whether one class seems important. The course notes define a component as a major part of the system with one coherent responsibility, and explicitly note that a single Invoice class is too small to be an architectural component.

A class can provide or implement the interface of a component without being a component by itself. I would show it as a separate component only if the team treats it as an independently understandable unit with its own coherent responsibility, a clear interface for the rest of the system, and meaningful dependencies. If it is simply the class that implements an important decision inside Logic or Model, keep it inside that larger component and show the class in a lower-level design diagram if needed.

The notes also clarify that a component is not a special programming construct and is often represented by a package or group of packages plus a type declaring what it offers: https://nus-cs2103-ay2627-s1.github.io/website/schedule/week6/topics.html#w62-architecture-diagrams
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/344#issuecomment-5709938709" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I agree that a normal invalid command should not usually be a WARNING. A practical test is whether the event would help a maintainer diagnose unexpected behaviour later. If the validator correctly rejects bad input and the user receives a clear message, the application is behaving as designed, so no log entry may be needed. If tracing those interactions is useful during debugging, use a lower level such as FINE or INFO instead.

Reserve WARNING for an unexpected but recoverable problem, such as failing to read one data entry, and SEVERE for a failure that prevents an important operation from continuing safely. Also avoid recording the full user input when the diagnostic context alone is enough.

Week 6 describes logging as information kept for future troubleshooting, especially for unanticipated failures, and shows that lower-level messages can be filtered out by setting the logging level to WARNING: https://nus-cs2103-ay2627-s1.github.io/website/schedule/week6/topics.html#w63-logging

There is also an earlier discussion of the same question in #218.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/484#issuecomment-5728725968" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

If I’m not wrong, Java does not specify that `Object.hashCode()` is based on the object’s memory address, and two objects having the same hash code does not mean they are equal. Hash collisions are allowed. The contract only requires equal objects to have the same hash code.

`Remark` uses `value.hashCode()` so that its hash code is consistent with its value-based `equals()` implementation. The `@Override` annotation is valid because a class without an explicit superclass implicitly extends `Object`.

References: [`Object.hashCode()`](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/lang/Object.html#hashCode()) and [JLS §8.1.4](https://docs.oracle.com/javase/specs/jls/se25/html/jls-8.html#jls-8.1.4).
</panel>

</panel>


