<img width="526" height="824" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/c52e2977-15ba-4967-a4bb-62acc8b72b0b" />
<img width="419" height="629" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/97370953-ff8d-4649-94f3-64918c64b577" />

Hi Rayyan! I tested your .jar file on macOS.

- macOS 26.3 (build 25D125), Apple M4 (arm64)
- openjdk version "25.0.3" 2026-04-21 LTS
- OpenJDK Runtime Environment Zulu25.34+17-CA (build 25.0.3+9-LTS)
- OpenJDK 64-Bit Server VM Zulu25.34+17-CA (build 25.0.3+9-LTS, mixed mode, sharing)

I noted that your find and on results are numbered 1...n, but index commands use full-list positions:

```
todo find the crew
deadline reach Ithaca /by 2026-06-06
event sail past the sirens /from dawn /to dusk

find Ithaca
    1. [D][ ] reach Ithaca (by: Jun 6 2026)      <- this is really task 2

mark 1
    Done. One less thing between us and home:
      [T][X] find the crew                        <- it marked task 1 instead
```

`on 2026-06-06` exhibits similar behaviour. So after any find or on, the number shown doesn't match what `mark`, `unmark`, `delete` and `update` accept. With delete this would silently remove the wrong task.

I also noted a truncation behaviour if `|` is added in a description:

```
todo a | b        -&gt; was accepted with output [T][ ] a | b
```

This saved as `T | 0 | a | b`. However, your storage joins fields with `|`, so on relaunch, "| b" disappears.

```
list
    3. [T][ ] a           <- "| b" was gone
```

Moreover, your duplicate rejection works in general but can be bypassed via `update`. This contradicts the claim that duplicate tasks are rejected.

```
todo alpha
todo beta
todo alpha           -&gt; "Already on the list. We don't repeat mistakes." which is correct
update 2 /desc alpha -&gt; accepted
list
    1. [T][ ] alpha
    2. [T][ ] alpha
```

I also found that `update` handles its edge cases well. Everything invalid I tried returned a clear message with no crash, such as 
`todo`, `deadline x`, `deadline x /by Sunday`, `event x /from dawn`, `find`, `mark 99`, `delete 0`, `mark abc`, and an unknown command. `mark  2` with a double space works as well. Lastly, persistence works well.

Other than these, well done!
</panel>

</panel>


