<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/275#issuecomment-5708322239" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

OS: macOS  
Architecture: Apple Silicon  
Java: 25.0.3  
Result: Issues Found

The following warnings are printed when launching the application:

'''text
WARNING: A restricted method in java.lang.System has been called
WARNING: java.lang.System::load has been called by com.sun.glass.utils.NativeLibLoader in module javafx.graphics (jrt:/javafx.graphics)
WARNING: Use --enable-native-access=javafx.graphics to avoid a warning for callers in this module
WARNING: Restricted methods will be blocked in a future release unless native access is enabled
'''

These warnings do not appear to affect the application's functionality.

### Issues found

**1. Duplicate tasks/events can be added**

The application allows identical tasks or events to be added multiple times.

For example, entering the exact same `event` command twice results in two identical entries being stored.

It may be useful to detect duplicate entries and warn the user before adding them.


**2. Events can have an end date earlier than their start date**

The application accepts an event even when the `/to` date occurs before the `/from` date.

For example:

'''text
event meeting /from 2026-09-20 /to 2026-09-19
'''

is accepted even though the event ends before it begins.

The application could validate that the end date is the same as or later than the start date and display an error otherwise.


**3. Invalid command prefixes may be interpreted as valid commands**

Commands beginning with a valid command keyword can sometimes still be interpreted as that command even when there is no space separating the keyword from the arguments.

For example:

'''text
todoabc
'''

can be interpreted as a `todo` command rather than being rejected as an unknown command.

Similarly, inputs beginning with strings such as `find`, `mark`, or `delete` may enter the corresponding command-handling logic even if the command keyword is immediately followed by other characters.

Ideally, command recognition should require either the exact command keyword or the keyword followed by appropriate whitespace.


**4. Minor text-wrapping alignment issue**

When longer messages wrap onto another line, the continuation appears slightly misaligned/indented compared with the first line.

This does not affect functionality, but adjusting the wrapping or padding could improve readability.


### Other observations

The other commands tested worked as expected, and I did not encounter any other major functional issues.

The interface is also polished and visually appealing.
</panel>

