<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/232#issuecomment-5700927915" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Smoke test completed on macOS 26.5.2, Apple Silicon (arm64). Tested from an empty folder, run from the terminal.

Environment
- Java: OpenJDK 25.0.4, Azul Zulu 25.36+15-CA
- JAR SHA-256: ac381e9ff64ddc1f366bd3b144b0d623f44f9408bf076e2a46a598aadff1a3eb

What passed
- Launches with `java -jar yourhand.jar`; the GUI opens with the YourHand header, welcome message, task area, input box and Send button.
- `help`, `todo read lecture notes`, `deadline submit report /by 2026-10-01`, `event team meeting /from 2026-09-20 1400 /to 2026-09-20 1500`, `list`, `find report`, `view schedule 2026-09-20`, `mark 1`, `unmark 1` and `delete 1` all behaved as documented. I also clicked the Send button instead of pressing Enter, and it sent correctly.
- Invalid input (`blah`, and `deadline x /by 2026-13-45`) produced an error message without crashing the app.
- `bye` closes the app normally.
- Storage: `data/yourhand.txt` was created in the same folder as the JAR, so nothing is hard-coded elsewhere, and my tasks were still listed after closing and reopening.

One issue: no Apple Silicon support on a plain JDK. The JAR bundles Intel (x86_64) native libraries for macOS (`libglass.dylib`, `libprism_es2.dylib`, and so on).

My commands above only worked because my JDK ships JavaFX, so the glass native loaded from the JDK itself (`jrt:/javafx.graphics`) and the JAR's copy was never used. On a JDK that does not bundle JavaFX, which is the common case, the JAR's own copies are used and the app fails to start:

'''text
java.lang.UnsatisfiedLinkError: ~/.openjfx/cache/17.0.7/libprism_es2.dylib: mach-o file, but is an incompatible architecture (have 'x86_64', need 'arm64e' or 'arm64')
java.lang.UnsatisfiedLinkError: ~/.openjfx/cache/17.0.7/libglass.dylib: mach-o file, but is an incompatible architecture (have 'x86_64', need 'arm64e' or 'arm64')
Exception in thread "main" java.lang.RuntimeException: java.lang.NoSuchMethodError: notifyPreferencesChanged
'''

The same applies on arm64 Linux. The fix is to bundle the Apple Silicon natives instead of the Intel ones, using the `mac-aarch64` classifier for the JavaFX dependencies in `build.gradle` rather than `mac`. Note that a single fat JAR can only carry one Mac architecture, since both artifacts ship the same file names, but Intel Macs are the rare case now.

<img width="754" height="920" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/f5debd4c-96ec-49de-b7e1-1246215fc498" />
