**1. :fas-info-circle: Request to help with smoke testing JAR file**
</div>

* Link to JAR file: https://github.com/matthewhanyd/ip/releases/download/v0.2/mattchatbot.jar
* Link to the User Guide: https://matthewhanyd.github.io/ip/
* Which OS'es to test on: Windows | Linux
* Things to check specifically:

The main thing I need is whether the window opens at all. I only have an Apple Silicon Mac, and on my machine the app loads JavaFX from my JDK rather than from the JAR, so the bundled Windows and Linux native libraries have never actually been used. The JAR does contain them, 54 DLLs and 10 `.so` files alongside the Mac ones, but that only proves they were packaged, not that they load.

Beyond that, two things are worth a look. First, whether a `data` folder appears next to the JAR the first time you add a task, and whether your tasks are still there after you close and reopen the app. Second, a quick pass through the commands listed in the user guide: `todo`, `deadline`, `event`, `list`, `mark`, `unmark`, `find`, `on`, `update`, `delete` and `bye`.

It needs Java 25. Please run it from a terminal in an empty folder rather than double-clicking it:

```
java -jar mattchatbot.jar
```

Guide for testers:
* Post screenshots from your smoke tests
* Mention which OS you used for testing

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/413#issuecomment-5729905113" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

**OS:** macOS 15.6.1 (Apple Silicon, arm64)
**Java:** Zulu 25.0.3 FX
**JAR:** `clsl.jar` from the v2.0 release, SHA-256 `00500a2dab4e708926feb4d7648f89aeb48936f7ccf5b94d7ae3406d4c3bfca5`
**Launched with:** `java -jar clsl.jar` from an empty folder

Everything on your checklist works and I could not get the app to crash. One thing in the storage layer is worth fixing before you go much further, and I have put it first because it can lose a user's data silently.

**One bad line in the save file destroys every other saved task.** I seeded `data/csls.txt` with two valid tasks followed by a single malformed line. The app opened to an empty list, and adding one new task rewrote the file with only that new task in it. Both valid tasks were gone from disk permanently, after one command. The app does print `Unknown task type in file input`, but it goes to stdout, so anyone who launches the JAR by double-clicking sees nothing at all and just finds an empty list. The fix is not large: skip the bad line and keep the rest, or refuse to overwrite a file that failed to parse. Either one turns this from data loss into an inconvenience.

**Everything else held up.** All ten commands work. `todo`, `deadline` and `event` add correctly, with dates rendering as `Sep 30 2026` and event ranges as `from: Sep 20 2026 to: Sep 21 2026`. `list` numbers the tasks and shows the type letter alongside the status. `mark` and `unmark` flip between `[X]` and `[ ]`. `find` is case-insensitive, so `find BOOK` matched "read book". `on 2026-09-20` returned the event covering that date and correctly left out the to-do. `delete` removed the task I meant and the remaining ones renumbered with no gap. Tasks survive a restart.

`bye` does exactly what you specified, which is the fiddly requirement of the three you listed. It showed the goodbye message, greyed out the text field and the Send button straight away, ignored a `list` I typed during the wait, and closed the window about five seconds later. I checked the same thing from the other direction and the compiled `MainController` disables both controls and then runs a `PauseTransition` of `5.0` seconds before exiting, so the behaviour is deliberate and not a timing accident.

Error handling was solid across 17 bad inputs: an unknown command, blank input, whitespace only, a bare `todo`, `deadline` with no `/by`, `event` with no `/to`, `mark 0`, `mark 99`, `mark abc`, `delete 99`, `delete` with no number, `find` with no keyword, `on` with no date, a malformed date, an impossible date, a reversed event range and a duplicate task. Each one came back with a clear `ERROR:` line and the app kepta running. The exit code was 0 and nothing was written to stderr.

Two smaller things.

**Impossible dates are reported as a format problem.** `deadline submit /by 2026-02-30` replies `ERROR: Please use yyyy-mm-dd format`, but the input is already in that format. The real issue is that 30 February does not exist, and a user who reads your message literally will keep retyping the same thing in the same format. Something like `ERROR: that date does not exist` would point them at the actual mistake.

**Commands are case-sensitive.** `TODO read book` and `List` are both rejected as `I don't understand`. That may well be deliberate, and it is a defensible choice, but the User Guide does not mention it anywhere. Worth a line in the guide if you intend to keep it.

Screenshots below: adding all three task types and listing them, `find` and `on`, the error batch, and `bye` with the input disabled during the five second wait.

