<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/485#issuecomment-5730100394" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

**OS:** macOS 15.6.1 (Apple Silicon, arm64)
**Java:** Zulu 25.0.3 FX
**JAR:** `answerme.jar` from the A-Release tag, SHA-256 `de2bd830a76c5644b79b92e4e9c4c540de6130dca638234f6c8c652230a32661`
**Launched with:** `java -jar answerme.jar` from an empty folder

I could not break either of the two things you asked about. Parsing rejected everything it should have and gave a useful reason each time, and I did not find an uncaught case in the storage loader. Details below, then three small things at the end.

**Command parsing.** All ten commands in the User Guide work: `todo`, `deadline`, `event`, `list`, `delete`, `find`, `mark`, `unmark`, `dedup` and `bye`. All seven documented date and time formats parse, including `MMM d yyyy` and both `HHmm` variants, and a deadline saved with a time still has that time after a restart. Command words are case-insensitive, so `TODO` and `List` both work. Duplicate detection ignores case while still respecting task type, which matches what the guide says.

I put about 25 malformed inputs through it. Every one produced a clear message, the app stayed up, and the exit code was 0 with nothing on stderr. That covered an unknown command, bare `todo`, `deadline` and `event`, a flag with no argument after it, `event` given only one of `/from` and `/to`, a missing index, index 0, a non-numeric index, an out-of-range index, an operation on an empty list, `find` with no search term, an unparseable date, 30 February, a reversed event range, and a pipe character in a description. What I liked here is that the messages actually distinguish the cases: `The list index must be at least 1` is a different error from `The list index must be a valid integer`, which is different again from `The task list is empty so cannot perform operation`. A lot of submissions collapse all three into one generic complaint.

**Malformed storage file.** I ran 24 variants of `data/tasks.txt`: unknown task type, invalid status, too few and too many fields, a `Deadline` with no date, a `Deadline` with an unparseable date, an `Event` carrying only one date, an `Event` whose dates run backwards, an empty description, blank and whitespace-only lines, an empty file, a missing trailing newline, CRLF line endings, a single 100KB line, non-ASCII descriptions, random binary data, a `tasks.txt` that is actually a directory, and a file with its read permission removed. All of them were handled. No stack trace ever reached me and stderr stayed empty the whole way through.

The design here is the right one. A bad line is skipped on its own and the good lines around it still load, the warning names the line number and what was wrong with it, and it says plainly that skipped entries will be dropped on the next save, so nobody loses data without being told first. The unreadable file and the directory case both fall back to `Unable to open data/tasks.txt for loading` and leave the file untouched. Saving got the same care: with the save file made read-only, adding a task reports `Oh no! Unable to save tasks.` and the task does not go into the in-memory list either, so what is on screen still matches what is on disk. That last one is the case I expected to find broken and did not.

Three small things, none of which broke anything.

**1. Fields are not trimmed when loading.** A line of `  Todo  |  Incomplete  |  padded  ` is skipped as `Unknown task type on line 1`. This only bites someone hand-editing the save file, which is the scenario you asked about, and the reason given points at the task type when the real problem is the spacing. Trimming each field before you match it would make the loader tolerant of this for almost no code.

**2. Control characters survive the load.** A description containing a NUL byte comes in without complaint and gets printed raw, which splits that task across two lines in `list`. You already reject pipe characters on input, so extending the same idea to non-printable characters on load would be consistent.

**3. The skipped-line warning punctuates itself inconsistently.** One run gave me `- Unknown task type on line 2`, `- Invalid task status on line 3.`, `- Invalid Deadline on line 4` and `- Invalid Event on line 5`. Only the second ends in a full stop, and the capitalisation switches between lowercase descriptions and capitalised type names. Cosmetic only, and you can see it in the third screenshot.

One observation instead of a defect: `dedup` cannot be reached through normal use, since duplicates are already refused when you add them. The save file is the only way to get duplicates into the list, which makes `dedup` a storage repair command in practice. It does work on file-injected duplicates and removes them case-insensitively, which is the last screenshot.

Screenshots below: adding tasks in several date formats, the pipe and duplicate rejections, the malformed storage warning on startup, and `dedup` clearing duplicates that came in from the file.

