**JAR:** `yourhand.jar` from A-Release-v0.2, SHA-256 `ac381e9ff64ddc1f366bd3b144b0d623f44f9408bf076e2a46a598aadff1a3eb`
**Run with:** `java -jar yourhand.jar` from a terminal, in a new empty folder

It launches on Java 25 and the GUI comes up with everything you listed. All ten commands in your script work, including `view schedule` and `help`. Storage goes to `data/yourhand.txt` relative to the working directory, so nothing is hard-coded, and my tasks were still there after closing and reopening. I tried about 14 malformed commands and none of them crashed it: every one came back with a sensible message, the exit code was 0 and nothing went to stderr. `bye` does close the window, but only after a five second pause, which had me thinking it had hung the first time. Worth a line in the guide.

One thing I would fix. A single bad line in `data/yourhand.txt` throws away every valid task in the file. I put two good tasks on either side of one garbage line, and the app opened with an empty list and then overwrote the file on my next command, so both were gone for good. You do warn with `Man got hacked ggwp`, so the user knows something happened, but skipping the bad line and keeping the ones around it would turn this from data loss into a nuisance.

Smaller thing, since you mentioned you have only tested on Windows: on macOS the window opens at 600x428, and the welcome bubble is taller than that, so the top of the greeting image is cut off until you scroll or resize. You can see it in the first screenshot.

