<div slot="header">

### 234. TAN ..UGEN `@Itscoldhere22` (4 posts) 
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/154#issuecomment-5564567987" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think just like the Occam's razor principle, it is usually better to have a simpler design than a complicated one. In fact, I think extra abstractions are also for simplicity, as that means we can standardise a lot of things that naturally falls in certain categories. It also simplify the process to ensure things work correct when they are interacting with other objects.

Hence, as AI is allowed to used, I think it is always better to focus on simplicity first. Do whatever you think works. In the future when you actually think you should abstract some part instead, just use AI. Give clear and small steps instructions and normally it will abstract things really well. Not to mention version control and unit/integration tests ensure there's always a safe net if things go wrong. 
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/146#issuecomment-5564639007" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

If you are using VSCode, you can use their git extension to write your commit message then push (which can be set to auto stage your files). You can even ask AI to write your commit message.

However, as @Brendan-Lim @ykteo000 has mentioned, sometimes it may not be a good idea to combine these commands. So you can use it regularly but do be conscious about when you should manage your version control more deliberately.

Also really **DON'T** use AI to manage version control. It could be disastrous (my friend's project got wiped out one day before deadline by AI).
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/177#issuecomment-5644584357" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

One thing about software development principles is that they are not the best, just recommended. So if abstracting something make things less readable and less easy to build around with, it may not be good to follow the "principle". It's like on an escalator by principle people stand on the left, but if there's someone standing on the right and no one on the left, you can just walk up the left side, rather than asking him to move to the left and you walk up the right side after that.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/139#issuecomment-5644605661" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Well one day CS2103T may be able to support multiple language, but for now the automated testers, style checkers, and everything is built around Java, which is most probably why we can only still use Java. So we probably can do iP in the language of our liking, but it takes time to build a syllabus around this.
</panel>

</panel>


