<div slot="header">

### 245. PART..AVIN `@kpkavin04` (3 posts) 
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/117#issuecomment-5479157955" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I believe this touches on the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) and Single Level of Abstraction Principle (SLAP). The `processInput` actually hides what the function actually guarantees to do, this forces the reader to check the implementation to understand what is the processing being done.

processInput might be more relevant in the case where it is used as a high-level orchestrator between the different sub-helpers(`removeWhiteSpaceFromInput`, `convertToCamelCase` and so on).
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/154#issuecomment-5657724652" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think it helps to distinguish between keeping code easy to change and building extension points for every possible future change. For the iP, we can still prepare for change by giving classes clear responsibilities, avoiding duplicated assumptions, and keeping methods focused without necessarily adding interfaces or abstract classes upfront.

I would start with the simplest design that fits the current requirements. When a later feature reveals a repeated pattern of changes or a growing switch/conditional structure that is becoming hard to maintain, that is stronger evidence for introducing an abstraction. At that point, the abstraction can be shaped by the actual variations in the code rather than guessed in advance.

It could also be useful to experiment with a more extensible design in a separate branch. Comparing how each design handles the next feature would make the trade-off more concrete as well.
</panel>

