### 249. YONG..RYAN `@Ryanyzh` (3 posts) 
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/454" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1. :fas-info-circle: Request to help with smoke testing JAR file**
</div>

## Help smoke-test Bobby on Windows or Linux

Hi everyone! Could you help smoke-test Bobby, my task-management chatbot? Windows and Linux results would be especially helpful, since I need to confirm the JAR works properly outside my setup.

## Links

JAR: [Download bobby.jar](https://github.com/Ryanyzh/ip/releases/download/A-Release/bobby.jar)  
User Guide: https://ryanyzh.github.io/ip/  
Release: [v0.2](https://github.com/Ryanyzh/ip/releases/tag/A-Release)

## Preparation

Use Java 25 and confirm it in a terminal:

'''bash
java -version
'''

Put `bobby.jar` in a new, empty folder.

Open a terminal in that folder and run:

'''bash
java -jar "bobby.jar"
'''

## Test checklist

Check that the full JavaFX GUI opens, the Bobby title/avatar/images display correctly, and the layout stays readable when you resize the window. Try submitting commands using both Enter and Send.

Starting with an empty task list, enter these commands one at a time:

'''text
todo Read lecture notes
deadline Submit project report /by 2026-09-18 1800
event Team project meeting /from 2026-09-18 1400 /to 2026-09-18 1600
list
mark 1
unmark 1
tag 2 #project
find project
delete 3
list
'''

Please also try these invalid commands. Each should show a helpful error without changing the task list or closing Bobby:

'''text
todo
mark 99
deadline Bad date /by tomorrow
event Bad event /from 2026-09-18 1600 /to 2026-09-18 1400
tag 1 urgent
unknown
'''

Finally:

- Run `mark 1` and then `list`; the first task should be completed.
- Run `bye`; Bobby should display its farewell and close.
- Reopen the JAR from the same folder and run `list`. The remaining tasks, tags, and completed status should be preserved.
- Confirm that the save file was created at `data/bobby.txt` inside the test folder.

## Reporting results

Please attach a screenshot of the complete GUI window and report:

'''text
Operating system and version:
CPU architecture:
Java version:
JAR launched successfully: Yes/No
GUI displayed and resized correctly: Yes/No
Commands and error handling passed: Yes/No
Tasks persisted after restarting: Yes/No
Checks that failed, if any:
Additional observations:
'''

If something fails, please include the command that caused it, the expected and actual behaviour, any terminal error, and whether it still happens after restarting Bobby.

Thank you for helping!
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/430#issuecomment-5725604681" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

## Smoke test results

**OS tested:** macOS 26.2 (Apple silicon)

### Issues found

#### 1. An event can end before it starts

**Steps to reproduce:**

1. Enter `event reversed /from 2026-12-31 /to 2026-01-01`.
2. Enter `list`.

**Observed:** The event is created successfully with an end date earlier than its start date.

**Expected:** Bean should reject the event and explain that the end date cannot be earlier than the start date.

<img width="646" height="846" alt="Event with an end date before its start date" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/c94edb1a-10e5-4c12-ac43-c8b26b72ab15">

#### 2. A quoted empty task name is accepted

**Steps to reproduce:**

1. Enter `todo ""`.

**Observed:** Bean creates a task whose name is `""`.

**Expected:** Bean should treat this as an empty name and reject it, as it does for `todo` and `todo /priority HIGH`.

<img width="646" height="846" alt="Task created with a quoted empty name" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/648ae060-b1d4-4682-975b-e9081f73d263">

### Checks that passed

- Creation of valid to-dos, deadlines, and events
- Rejection of impossible dates such as `2026-02-30`
- Rejection of incorrectly formatted dates such as `31-12-2026`
- Rejection of missing task names and dates, except for the quoted-empty case above
- Deletion and list renumbering
- Marking and unmarking tasks
- Priority sorting in the order HIGH, MEDIUM, LOW
- Case-insensitive keyword and substring search, including multi-word searches
- Rejection of zero, non-numeric, and out-of-range task indexes
</panel>

