### 259. WANG..AHUA `@jiahw4ng` (3 posts) 
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/87#issuecomment-5453454082" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think the Java coding standard gives the impression that `->` should be used when the body of the case is a single method call or value, and `:` otherwise (if the logic of the case is more complex and requires more than one line). Furthermore, `->` is more expressive in switch _statements_ (ones that return a value) because if you use `:`, you have to write `yield &lt;value>`. 

Also note that switch expressions written using `->` have no fall-through behaviour, while you need `break` if you use `:`.

So I think it really depends on your use case. but for the IP chatbot you probably will write a single method to handle each command, so `->` keeps things more concise.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/152#issuecomment-5559861559" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

IMO whether a literal should be named depends on whether the name actually adds useful meaning. Declaring `int ZERO = 0` seems a bit excessive even if the intention is readability or to avoid typos, because they merely restate the values without explaining their role. I mean, 0 is forever zero, there isn't any need to make that explicit.

I think naming string literals is much more valuable, especially if they may be reused or must adhere to some consistent format, which matter for error messages and logging. It makes reading and searching through thousands of lines of logs much easier.

My previous internship had a backend written in Go, and nearly every package had a `constants.go` file that contained strings for method names / actions which we used when printing errors. That allowed us to produce messages like:

```
Error: <description> happened in <method name>
Error: <user> does not have the necessary permissions for <action>
```

Centralising those strings prevented any inconsistent wording / spacing, and also made them way easier to reuse and update.
</panel>

