</div>

I have another take on this -&gt; I think that another factor that people might not consider that decides whether a decision is architecturally significant is if it is ambiguous enough that its reasoning needs documenting.

I think the purpose of an architecture diagram is to help other software engineers understand the significant design decisions of a system, especially choices where the reasoning may not be obvious from the code alone.

In practice, I would judge a decision by asking whether another engineer could reasonably have chosen a different design, and whether that choice affects the assumptions or interfaces of other components.


</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/543#issuecomment-5832299907" expanded>
