### 281. KOH .. WEE `@Yik-Wee` (3 posts) 
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/157#issuecomment-5560255789" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think it depends. The example provided seems perfectly readable to me:
'''java
boolean isDone = parts[1].equals("1");
new Todo(description, isDone);
'''
And the alternative mentioned to add a `incomplete()` or `complete()` factory methods seems reasonable too. I personally think that adding an enum in this case would be overkill since a boolean can only have 2 values, so adding an enum would add unnecessary complexity (perhaps even antithetical to the KISS principle).

However, if the class / constructor was more complicated:
'''java
Task(String description, boolean isDone, boolean isRepeating, boolean isImportant /* and others... */) ``{`` /* ... */ ``}``
'''
Then perhaps some kind of builder class would be more appropriate to improve readability (see [builder design pattern](https://www.baeldung.com/java-builder-pattern)). Then, the following would be much more readable
'''java
Task task = new TaskBuilder("description")
        .done(false)
        .repeating(true)
        .important(false)
        .build();
'''
compared to passing in magic literals (as the course website calls them) directly
'''java
Task task = new Task("description", false, true, false);
'''

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/218#issuecomment-5677158743" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I agree that this shouldn't be logged at `WARNING`, but it may still be useful to log this at a lower level, such as `DEBUG`. According to the [course website](https://nus-cs2103-ay2627-s1.github.io/website/book/errorHandling/logging/what/):
> When problems occur in a system e.g., an unanticipated failure, the associated log files may indicate what went wrong, and actions can then be taken to prevent it from happening again.

In this context, it is possible that the phone number validation method may have missed some edge cases. After all, validating phone numbers, emails, etc. are difficult. Our program could be doing something with this invalid phone number that we thought was valid, leading to potential errors (maybe not in the context of AB3, but perhaps some automated messaging software that sends SMS in bulk). Or, it could have rejected a potentially valid phone number.

In either case, I think having a log of the command accepting or rejecting the phone numbers would probably be useful to be able to debug these kinds of errors, but probably not at the `WARNING` level as you pointed out.
</panel>

