Tested on Windows. Everything on your list worked except mouse wheel scrolling, which is described at the end.

**Environment**

- OS: Windows 11 Pro, build 10.0.26200
- Architecture: AMD64 (AMD Ryzen 9 7940HS)
- Java: 25.0.4, HotSpot 64-Bit Server VM `25.0.4+7-LTS-189`
- Ran `java -jar sophon.jar` from an empty folder
- Result: passed, with one issue

**What worked**

- Starts with no fatal error. Window title is exactly `Sophon · Deep Space Interface`.
- Title, subtitle, input box, TRANSMIT button, scrollbar, message bubbles and both avatars all render. The `SOPHON · RESPONSE` and `YOU · TRANSMISSION` labels line up with the right bubbles.
- Nothing overlapped or was cut off. I also sent a 170 character todo and it wrapped correctly in both the transmission bubble and the response bubble.
- Enter and the TRANSMIT button both submit.
- The conversation scrolls to the newest message after each command.
- Earlier messages are reachable by dragging the scrollbar.
- I sent 20 `todo` commands one after another and it stayed responsive, then `list` printed all 23 tasks with no lag or rendering artifacts.
- All of your listed commands behaved as expected: `todo read a book`, `deadline return book /by 2026-10-15`, `event school break /from 2026-10-20 /to 2026-10-25`, `list`, `find book` (matched both book tasks), `mark 1`, `unmark 1`, `delete 2`, `list`.
- `nonsense` returned "Your message has been observed. Its meaning, however, remains unknown." and the app stayed usable.
- I also tried `mark 99`, `deadline no by date here` and `deadline bad date /by 2026-13-45`. Each one gave a clear message and nothing broke.
- `bye` printed the farewell, greyed out the input box and the TRANSMIT button, and the window closed 3.1 seconds later. The JVM process exited, no stray process left behind.
- Closing and reopening from the same folder restored all 23 tasks, and a task I marked before closing was still `[X]`.

**Issue: mouse wheel cannot scroll up from the newest message**

The scrollbar works, but the mouse wheel does not, whenever the view is sitting at the newest message.

Steps:

1. Send a few commands so the conversation auto scrolls to the bottom.
2. Put the pointer over the conversation and scroll the wheel up. Nothing moves, no matter how far you scroll.
3. Drag the scrollbar thumb up instead. That works, and earlier messages appear.
4. From that position the wheel now works in both directions.
5. Send another command. The view snaps back to the newest message and the wheel is ignored again until you touch the scrollbar.

I reproduced this on a fresh launch as well, so it is not a one off.

A guess at the cause, in case it helps: if the auto scroll is done with something like `scrollPane.vvalueProperty().bind(dialogContainer.heightProperty())`, the property stays bound, so the wheel cannot change it. Setting `vvalue` to 1.0 after each message rather than binding it would leave the wheel free.

**Terminal output**

One warning on every launch, on both runs:

```
WARNING: Unsupported JavaFX configuration: classes were loaded from 'unnamed module @2c5c95'
```

This is the usual warning for a shaded JAR that puts JavaFX on the classpath instead of the module path, and it does not affect anything I tested. Mentioning it since you asked about terminal warnings.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/334#issuecomment-5826977619" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Tested on Windows. All commands and the GUI worked, and I could not get it to crash. A few small things at the end, plus one thing worth checking for Intel Macs.

**Environment**

- OS: Windows 11 Pro, build 10.0.26200
- Architecture: AMD64 (AMD Ryzen 9 7940HS)
- Java: 25.0.4, HotSpot 64-Bit Server VM `25.0.4+7-LTS-189`
- Ran `java -jar magnus.jar` from an empty folder

**Commands**

Every example in your user guide worked:

- `todo prepare tutorial notes`, `deadline submit project report /by 18/09/2026 1600` and `event attend hackathon /from 19/09/2026 0900 /to 20/09/2026 1800` all added, with the date chips showing the right values
- `list`, and `LIST` in caps
- `find PROJECT report` matched regardless of case and with a space in the query
- `list_deadline 18/09/2026` showed the deadline
- `list_event 01/09/2026 30/09/2026` showed the hackathon, and `list_event 19/09/2026 19/09/2026` left it out because it only partly overlaps, as your guide says
- `mark 2`, `unmark 2`, `mark 1`, with the completed count updating each time
- `update 2` followed by `submit final project report /by 20/09/2026 1700` updated the deadline. Giving it an invalid replacement left the task unchanged and ended the update, as documented
- `delete 3`

Bad input, all of which gave a clear message and left the app usable:

- `blah`, `todo` with no description
- `deadline pay rent /by 31/02/2026 1200`
- an event whose end is before its start
- adding `todo prepare tutorial notes` a second time (rejected as a duplicate)
- `todo read chapter /by tomorrow` (rejected for using a reserved field)
- `mark 99` and `mark 0`

**GUI**

- Enter and the Send button both submit.
- A 150 character todo wrapped properly in both the user bubble and the reply.
- Mouse wheel and scrollbar both scroll back through earlier messages.
- Minimum window size is about 525x575. At that size and at 1400 wide everything reflowed with nothing cut off, and the view stayed on the newest message after resizing.
- `bye` showed the farewell, greyed out the input and Send, and the window closed about 2.5 seconds later. The process exited.
- Reopening from the same folder restored all tasks, including `[X]` on the completed one and a description that contains commas.

I also changed the status on line 2 of `data/magnus.txt` to `x` and restarted. It still opened, said the file is corrupted at line 2 with an invalid status `'x'`, locked the input, and left the file untouched. Your user guide says bad data "can prevent Magnus from starting", but what actually happens is better than that, so you could update that sentence to describe it.

**Things you might want to look at**

1. Intel Macs. Your `build.gradle` pulls JavaFX with the `mac-aarch64` classifier only. I checked inside the JAR and the only Mac native libraries are arm64 (`libglass.dylib` is a thin arm64 binary). Apple Silicon should be fine, but on an Intel Mac with a normal JDK there are no matching graphics libraries, so it will most likely fail at startup. I don't have a Mac to confirm this, so it would be worth asking an Intel Mac tester if one replies. If you decide not to support Intel Macs, a line in the user guide saying so would save someone the confusion.

2. Extra blank line in lists. When a todo is followed by another task, there is an empty gap under the todo, and the same gap appears under the header line of `find` results. It looks like the empty date chip row is still taking up space. Cosmetic only.

3. Terminal warnings. Every launch prints these:

   ```
   WARNING: Unsupported JavaFX configuration: classes were loaded from 'unnamed module @7cf4d42d'
   WARNING: A restricted method in java.lang.System has been called
   WARNING: java.lang.System::load has been called by com.sun.glass.utils.NativeLibLoader in an unnamed module
   WARNING: A terminally deprecated method in sun.misc.Unsafe has been called
   WARNING: sun.misc.Unsafe::allocateMemory has been called by com.sun.marlin.OffHeapArray
   ```

   None of them affected anything. They come from running JavaFX 17.0.7 on Java 25. Adding `Enable-Native-Access: ALL-UNNAMED` to the JAR manifest removes the restricted method one. The `Unsafe` one comes from JavaFX's own renderer, so a newer JavaFX version is the likely fix for that.

</panel>

</panel>


