<div slot="header">

### 293. QUEK..THAN `@eatenquek` (3 posts) 
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/215" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1. :fas-info-circle: How do you show an object in a sequence diagram if it already existed before the scenario begins?**
</div>

W6.1's examples all seem to start a diagram at the point where the relevant objects get created (the lifeline begins with a create message) but for something like a delete command, the Person object being deleted, and the AddressBook/Model holding it, already existed before this scenario starts they weren't created as part of handling this command.

Do you still draw their lifelines starting from the top of the diagram or is there a different convention for representing "this object is already here, I'm just interacting with it" And if it's the former, is there a way to visually distinguish "this lifeline starts because the object is newly created here" from "this lifeline is shown from the top only because it's relevant to this scenario, not because it was just created"?
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/207#issuecomment-5675842399" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I'd frame it around what the diagram is for, rather than trying to be exhaustive. W6.1 says a sequence diagram documents one specific scenario. Its job is to let a reader trace how that scenario plays out, not to be a call graph of the codebase.

A rough rule I've been using is include a call if removing it would leave a gap in understanding how the outcome was reached and omit it if it's a pure implementation detail that doesn't change the shape of the interaction.


</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/356#issuecomment-5904875110" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

## Smoke test on macOS

I tested the latest `zaru.jar` release (`v0.2`, asset `zaru.jar`) on macOS using OpenJDK 25.0.3 (Zulu 25.0.3).

### Setup
- Downloaded the JAR into a new empty folder whose path contains spaces: `/tmp/zaru smoke test folder`
- Ran it from that folder with:
  ```
  java -version
  java -jar zaru.jar
  ```

### Result
Java reported:
```
openjdk version "25.0.3" 2026-04-21 LTS
```

The JAR starts JavaFX, but the GUI cannot create its rendering surface in my current environment. It exits/logs this error:
```
java.lang.ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException: Index 0 out of bounds for length 0
 at javafx.graphics/com.sun.glass.ui.Screen.getMainScreen(Screen.java:56)
 at javafx.graphics/com.sun.prism.mtl.MTLPipeline.getDefaultResourceFactory(MTLPipeline.java:79)
```

This appears to be an environment/display issue because the failure occurs during JavaFX screen initialization, before the application UI becomes available. I therefore could not honestly test the documented commands or attach a GUI screenshot from this environment.

I recommend checking the JAR on a normal macOS desktop session with an available display, or checking whether the JavaFX runtime/platform classifier used to build the release matches the target machine.
</panel>

</panel>


