**Tested on:** macOS 27.0
**Java:** Azul Zulu OpenJDK 25.0.3 (the JavaFX build prescribed for Mac users)

Put `meowmeow.jar` in an empty folder and ran `java -jar meowmeow.jar` from the terminal. It launched cleanly; the title bar reads "Meowmeow — reluctantly organised".

**Commands run, in order:** `todo borrow book`, `deadline return book /by 2/12/2026 1800 /p high`, `event project meeting /from 2026-12-02 1400 /to 2026-12-02 1600`, `list`, `mark 1`, `priority 3 low`, `find book`, `list 2/12/2026`, `delete 1`, then three invalid inputs (`blah`, an event that ends before it starts, `deadline y /by 30/2/2026`), then a todo with a ~150-character description, then `bye`.

- Both date formats (`d/M/uuuu HHmm` and `uuuu-M-d HHmm`) were accepted, and `/p high` on the deadline plus `priority 3 low` on the event both stuck.
- Each invalid input got a red error bubble with a clear message (screenshot 1). No crashes.
- `bye` closed the window on its own after a moment.
- `data/meowmeow.txt` appeared next to the JAR with the priorities saved, and after restarting, `list` showed the same tasks (screenshot 2).

**On the text-wrapping point raised above:** the long todo wrapped correctly in both the user bubble and the reply bubble at a 520 px wide window on macOS (screenshot 1), so I could not reproduce it here.

**One optional suggestion:** the avatars are fairly large and the bubbles stop at about two-thirds of the window width, so at 520x900 only about four exchanges fit on screen at once. Smaller avatars, or wider bubbles, would show more of the conversation. The cat is great though.

Screenshots are unedited. Disclosure: I used Claude Code to script typing the commands into the window; the observations above are what it produced.

