<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/207#issuecomment-5665736234" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think it's clearer to just keep methods that contribute to the interaction you're illustrating. A sequence diagram is meant to explain the important runtime interactions for one particular scenario, not to reproduce every single method call that happens internally. Therefore, you only need to show the major interactions that explain how the command travels through the system. 

Also, I think it is important to remember that sequence diagrams are scenario-specific, so one diagram can show successful adding of a task, while another sequence diagram can be used to show deleting of a task. Both diagrams will then have different objects called and different helper methods called.  
</panel>

</panel>


