</div>

Hello everyone,

I would appreciate your assistance in smoke-testing Woofer, my task-management chatbot. Testing on Windows or Linux would be particularly helpful, as development and initial testing were conducted on macOS.
- [Download Woofer v0.1](https://github.com/zwqqwz123/ip/releases/tag/v0.1)
- [User Guide](https://zwqqwz123.github.io/ip/)

Please place woofer.jar in an empty folder and run the following commands from that folder:

java -version
java -jar woofer.jar

Ensure that your Java version is 25. Please check whether the GUI launches successfully and try adding, listing, marking, finding, deleting, and undoing tasks. You may also submit an incomplete command, such as todo, to check error handling.
Finally, close and reopen the application to verify that your tasks have been saved.
When reporting your results, please include your operating system, Java version, and any issues encountered, along with error messages or screenshots where relevant.
Thank you for your time and assistance.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/201#issuecomment-5658713843" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I agree with @spencercdz that data persistence can be a feature: it lets users reopen the app and continue using their saved records. On the other hand, “Command-based navigation” is less specific. It tells us that users operate the app through commands, but we still need to explain what those commands let them do, such as adding or finding contacts.

In fact, a feature need not have its own command. For instance, in the case of automatic saving, perhaps we could mark the command format as “not applicable” and explain when saving and loading happen. This would describe the feature clearly even though users do not need to activate it.
</panel>

</panel>


