### 390. DYLA.. TAY `@ZSDcode` (1 posts) 
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/139" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1. :fas-info-circle: [Suggestion] Revamping iP**
</div>

In the spirit of a greenfield project, why not give the student autonomy to pick the language he/she wants to code in, and give the student the autonomy to make the application a TUI or GUI instead.
I understand the reasoning behind Java, being a language focused on in CS2030S and CS2040S so we can still keep the guides in Java and JavaFX and all that. But why not for iP, push students to use a language they desire in with some helping guidelines, still meeting the requirements of Levels 1 to 10.
It's even possible to recommend for a non-exhaustive list of languages what particular libraries we should touch or strongly discourage developers to use to avoid certain hazards.
Lua is used so much for TUIs now, Rust and C++ is viewed as mem-safe, and Go is generally for threads and all
Just enforce languages which enforces an OOP structure.
Tests and code should work as intended, and we could have a more flexible coding standard for iP, while sticking to the current one for tP and informing students that in the tP, Java will be used.

1. Being greenfield implies we are allowed to direct it
2. Allows us to explore new environments

I, for one, would appreciate if I can code this project in Rust using Ratatui as a TUI instead of a GUI in JavaFX.
</panel>

</panel>


