### 424. LE X.. SON `@sonlexuan3000` (1 posts) 
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/282#issuecomment-5696928269" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Hi! Here is an AI-assisted smoke test of Ducky v0.2 on **macOS 15.6, Apple Silicon (aarch64)**, using **Zulu Java 25.0.3+9-LTS (25.0.3.fx-zulu)**, following the current course Java version requirement.

The downloaded JAR starts with `java -jar ducky.jar`. Basic `todo`, `deadline`, `event`, `list`, `mark`, `unmark`, and `delete` commands worked. Invalid dates and out-of-range task numbers produced errors without crashing, and the remaining tasks were preserved after `bye` and restarting.

One potentially confusing behavior is the numbering after `find`:

1. Add a todo, a deadline tagged `#school`, and an event tagged `#school`, in that order.
2. Run `find #school`: the deadline is displayed as **1** and the event as **2**.
3. Run `mark 2`: Ducky marks the **deadline**, which is number 2 in the original full list, rather than the event displayed as number 2 in the search results.

The screenshot shows the full list, search results, and response together. A possible improvement is to preserve the full-list indices in search results, or consistently operate on the displayed list. This suggestion has not been patched/tested in your code.

<img width="600" height="700" alt="Ducky on macOS: find renumbers tasks but mark uses the original list index" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/9253c8e7-0dd9-4f21-936c-a93d391f4a4c" />

**AI disclosure:** OpenAI Codex ran these commands and reproduced the behavior in the actual GUI on my Mac; this is not a claim that I manually repeated the tests. For GUI automation/screenshots, Codex used a temporary local launcher containing the unchanged v0.2 JAR with the same Java 25 runtime, in addition to checking the direct `java -jar` launch. The original and launcher-copy JAR SHA-256 hashes match (`45a301757c5268ccd9e5d171d8feee30ad61a7e10e18fa6434b2f3c6ee7b9843`).

</panel>

</panel>


